    38	## A note on method, before anything else
    39	
    40	This report has an unusual constraint imposed on it: **trust data, not text.**
    41	
    42	The `clifft` tree contains a large amount of prose — design documents, plans,
    43	post-mortems, header comments — written across several months by several
    44	authors (human and otherwise). Much of it is accurate. Some of it is stale, and
    45	a smaller amount of it is simply wrong, having been written from a hypothesis
    46	that later measurement refuted but that nobody went back to delete.
    47	
    48	Every claim in this report was therefore re-derived from an artifact before
    49	being written down. The rule used was:
    50	
    51	> A fact is *verified* only if it can be re-derived from a committed artifact —
    52	> a CSV, a `summary.json`, a SLURM log, an `.hsaco` — or from a file+line in the
    53	> source tree. Prose in a design doc is a *claim*, not a fact.
    54	
    55	The audit is committed as `V2_performance/VERIFIED_FACTS.md`, and every section
    56	below cites it. Three findings from that audit are worth stating up front,
    57	because they contradict documents still in the tree:
    58	
    59	1. **V2 does not emit MLIR.** It emits C. The directory is still called
    60	   `mlir/v2/` for historical reasons, and that name is the only MLIR left in it.
    61	   (§6.1)
    62	2. **V1 authored zero custom MLIR passes**, and one of the three stock passes it
    63	   ran was a provable no-op. (§5.4)
    64	3. **The one circuit family where V2 loses did not run V2's specializer at all** —
    65	   its specialization failed the correctness gate and fell back to the
    66	   interpreter. An earlier draft of the audit itself got this wrong, and the
    67	   correction is recorded in the ledger's §13. (§11.1)
    68	
    69	Where a number could not be re-measured, it is marked **[unverified]** and
    70	attributed rather than asserted. There are two such items, both flagged in place.
