    97	## 1. Executive summary
    98	
    99	`clifft` simulates near-Clifford quantum circuits by tracking a Pauli frame plus
   100	a small dense statevector. The GPU backend has been rewritten three times. This
   101	report characterizes all four generations and evaluates the newest against the
   102	baseline it must beat.
   103	
   104	### 1.1 The four generations
   105	
   106	| generation | what it is | per-circuit compile? | device language | verdict |
   107	|---|---|---|---|---|
   108	| **SVM** | bytecode interpreter kernel | no | HIP | the baseline; fast, correct, hard to beat |
   109	| **Hybrid** | per-circuit HIP source, AOT-compiled | yes | HIP | correct and competitive, but HIP-bound and never pulled ahead |
   110	| **MLIR V1** | per-circuit hand-emitted MLIR text | yes | MLIR→LLVM | **failed**: 5–60× slower, 221 s compiles, 20 MB IR |
   111	| **V2** | per-circuit C, specialized operands, runtime loops | yes | C→amdgcn (no HIP) | **1.0–3.9× faster than SVM** on **26 of 26** circuits |
   112	
   113	### 1.2 The headline number
   114	
   115	Across the 26-circuit corpus, comparing GPU kernel time (not host wall time),
   116	V2 versus the SVM interpreter:
   117	
   118	| regime | circuits | V2/SVM ratio | reading |
   119	|---|---|---|---|
   120	| Large surface codes, global tier | 5 | **0.256 – 0.310** | V2 is **3.2–3.9× faster** |
   121	| Mid-size surface + QV10, coop tier | 5 | 0.319 – 0.534 | V2 is **1.9–3.1× faster** |
   122	| Register tier (`four_t`, `frame_h`, `circuit_d3`, `surface_d7_t5`) | 4 | 0.509 – 0.742 | V2 is **1.3–2.0× faster** |
   123	| `circuit_d5` + `cultivation_d5`, coop tier | 6 | 0.786 – 0.856 | V2 is **1.2–1.3× faster** (post-fence, §1.3(d)) |
   124	| Quantum-volume, global tier rank 20–24 | 6 | 0.732 – 0.990 | V2 wins **1–37 %**, shrinking with rank (§10.6) |
   125	
   126	**mean 0.626, median 0.670, wins 26/26** — all 26 circuits, both backends, one
   127	job (50793), one node (`smci350-rck-g03-d13-21`), zero profiler aborts. Every
   128	circuit dispatches `clifft_v2_spec`; none falls back to the interpreter.
   129	
   130	**26 circuits, 26 wins.** The five regimes sum to exactly 26, and membership is
   131	assigned by *measured launch geometry* rather than by circuit name — the census
   132	of §10.5 is precisely the finding that names are unreliable here. (This bit
   133	during the audit: `surface_d7_t5` was filed under the coop band on the strength
   134	of its name, and its measured grid is 79 workgroups — register geometry.)
   135	
   136	The geometry is unambiguous. Global-tier kernels launch a **resident pool sized
   137	from the HBM budget** and then work-steal shots; coop-tier kernels launch **one
   138	workgroup per shot**. Reading grid ÷ 256 from the measured runs:
   139	
   140	| circuits | workgroups | shape |
   141	|---|---|---|
   142	| `surface_d7/d9/d11_t19`, `surface_d9/d11_t15`, `qv20` (×2) | 2,048 | global pool, capped (`v2_kernel.cc:441`) |
   143	| `qv21` → `qv24` | 1,360 → 680 → 336 → 168 | global pool, **shrinking as rank grows** |
   144	| `circuit_d5` (×5), `surface_d7/d9/d11_t10`, `surface_d7_t15` | 10,000 | coop, = shot count |
   145	| `cultivation_d5`, `qv10` | 20,000 | coop, = shot count |
   146	| `four_t`, `frame_h`, `circuit_d3`, `surface_d7_t5` | 79 | register, = ⌈shots/256⌉ |
   147	
   148	The QV row is the §10.2 budget mechanism visible from the outside, and it can be
   149	predicted exactly rather than merely described. Each resident workgroup owns one
   150	amplitude slice plus a half-size scratch — 12 bytes per amplitude — drawn from a
   151	32 GB budget (`v2_kernel.cc:436-446`). Evaluating that formula against the
   152	measured grids:
   153	
   154	| rank | bytes/wg | predicted wgs | **measured wgs** |
   155	|---|---|---|---|
   156	| 20 | 12 MB | 2,048 (capped) | **2,048** ✓ |
   157	| 21 | 24 MB | 1,360 | **1,360** ✓ |
   158	| 22 | 48 MB | 680 | **680** ✓ |
   159	| 23 | 96 MB | 336 | **336** ✓ |
   160	| 24 | 192 MB | 168 | **168** ✓ |
   161	
   162	Five for five, including the XCD-alignment rounding. So the QV circuits lose
   163	occupancy geometrically as rank climbs — the pool halves with every added qubit
   164	— and that compounds with the register spilling of §10.6. Two independent
   165	mechanisms degrade the same six circuits, which is why their ratios approach 1.0
   166	from a corpus whose median is 0.670.
   167	
   168	All 26 are **byte-exact** against the SVM interpreter and against the f64 CPU
   169	reference (modulo the documented f32/f64 branch divergence of §12).
   170	
   171	> **Provenance.** The ratios above are job **50793**
   172	> (`20260727T125310Z_report-final-allfixtures`): 26 circuits, both backends,
   173	> one node, commit `79d4463` clean, zero `rocprofv3` aborts. It replaces two
   174	> earlier attempts — a retracted run that dispatched stale kernels (§14.0's
   175	> §0 notice) and job 50785, which ran only 18 of 26 because eight fixture paths
   176	> had gone stale, aborted silently, and reported "wins 18/18" over the
   177	> truncated set. That truncation dropped the six QV circuits, the corpus's
   178	> weakest wins, and pulled the median from 0.670 to 0.518 — a *flattering*
   179	> error produced entirely by missing data. `bench_all.sh:127-135` now aborts
   180	> the run on a missing fixture rather than continuing past it.
   181	
   182	### 1.3 The five results this report argues for
   183	
   184	**(a) The disease V1 had was unrolling the operand *sequence*, and V2 cured it
   185	exactly.** The measurable form of that cure is source-IR density — lines of
   186	emitted source per bytecode instruction:
   187	
   188	| circuit | instrs | V1 MLIR | Hybrid HIP | **V2 C** |
   189	|---|---|---|---|---|
   190	| `coop_qv10` | 140 | 336,988 lines (**2407/instr**) | 7,805 (55.8/instr) | **179 (1.27/instr)** |
   191	| `coop_circuit_d5` | 1,720 | 118,493 (68.9/instr) | 33,508 (19.5/instr) | **1,760 (1.02/instr)** |
   192	| `coop_surface_d7_t10` | 4,134 | 228,544 (55.3/instr) | 75,409 (18.2/instr) | **4,174 (1.00/instr)** |
   193	
   194	V2 converges to **exactly one line of source per bytecode instruction**. V1 and
   195	Hybrid both emit a per-instruction *block*; V2 emits a per-instruction *call*.
   196	(§6.3)
   197	
   198	**(b) Specialization's gain is not uniform, and the variation is the interesting
   199	part.** Eight isolated A/B experiments (§7) measure per-class gains from 5.50×
   200	(VALU, frame-operand folding) down to **1.02×** (noise blocks, where the loop is
   201	genuinely data-dependent). The negative result is as informative as the
   202	positive ones and predicts exactly which circuits benefit.
   203	
   204	**(c) The win mechanism is visible in the hardware counters and is not what one
   205	might guess.** It is not floating-point throughput. **SALU instruction count
   206	falls 2.8–9.1× on all 20 circuits V2 wins** (ratios 0.110–0.361, no exceptions)
   207	— that is the interpreter's `switch` dispatch leaving the scalar unit. VALU
   208	falls too, but far less: 1.2–2.3×, and on `circuit_d3` barely at all (0.818).
   209	The counter that separates the two backends is the scalar one.
   210	
   211	The sign flips exactly where the fallback happened: on the six pre-fix losses,
   212	SALU goes **up** to 1.467–1.469 and VALU up to 2.23–2.30. Those six were running
   213	the interpreter (§1.3(d)), so the inversion is not a counter-example — it is the
   214	same measurement confirming which kernel actually ran. (§14.2)
   215	
   216	**(d) V2's one loss was a correctness failure wearing a performance costume —
