   299	
   300	## 2. The SVM: what is actually being simulated
   301	
   302	Before any GPU discussion, it is worth being precise about what the machine
   303	executes, because every subsequent design decision — the tiers, the
   304	specialization classes, the f32/f64 gap, even the noise regression — follows
   305	from the structure of this one object.
   306	
   307	### 2.1 The factored state
   308	
   309	`clifft` does not carry a dense statevector over all *N* qubits. It carries the
   310	factored representation documented at `src/clifft/svm/svm.h:92-103`:
   311	
   312	```
   313	|psi> = gamma · U_C · P · ( |phi>_A ⊗ |0>_D )
   314	```
   315	
   316	| symbol | what it is | storage |
   317	|---|---|---|
   318	| `gamma` | global scalar: phase + deferred normalization | one complex scalar |
   319	| `U_C` | the Clifford part, tracked symbolically | implicit in the frame |
   320	| `P` | a Pauli frame — one X bit and one Z bit per qubit | 2 × `ceil(N/64)` words |
   321	| `|phi>_A` | the **active** subspace: a dense statevector over `k` qubits | 2^k complex amplitudes |
   322	| `|0>_D` | the **dormant** qubits, provably still in the computational basis | nothing |
   323	
   324	The whole performance story lives in `k`, the **active rank**. A circuit that is
   325	pure Clifford never grows `k` at all: every gate is a frame update, `2^k = 1`,
   326	and simulation is polynomial. Each non-Clifford operation (`T`, `ROT`, an
   327	`EXPAND`) may promote a dormant qubit into the active set, `k → k+1`, doubling
   328	the dense array. Each measurement of an active qubit collapses it back, `k → k-1`.
   329	
   330	The **peak rank** of a compiled circuit — the maximum `k` over the whole program
   331	— therefore determines both the memory footprint (2^peak_rank complex
   332	amplitudes) and, as §3 shows, which GPU execution strategy is even viable.
   333	
   334	<figure>
   335	<img src="diagrams/factored-state.svg" alt="Factored state representation" width="100%">
   336	<figcaption><b>Figure 2.1</b> — The factored state. Dormant qubits cost zero
   337	storage; the Pauli frame costs 2 bits per qubit; only the active subspace costs
   338	exponential memory. Rank growth is driven by non-Clifford gates and undone by
   339	measurement.</figcaption>
   340	</figure>
   341	
   342	### 2.2 The bytecode
   343	
   344	The **Schrödinger Virtual Machine** is a bytecode interpreter over that state.
   345	The instruction set is *localized*: the ahead-of-time compiler has already
   346	resolved all global topology into 1- and 2-qubit virtual-axis operations, so the
   347	VM never evaluates basis spans or commutation relations
   348	(`src/clifft/backend/backend.h:21-23`).
   349	
   350	The opcode set (`backend.h:25-86` — **41 opcodes** plus the `NUM_OPCODES`
   351	sentinel) partitions cleanly by *what it costs*. The count matters later: it is
   352	41 arms that a CPU interpreter must dispatch over and that a specialized kernel
   353	never dispatches over at all.
   354	
   355	| family | count | opcodes | cost |
   356	|---|---|---|---|
   357	| **Frame** | 6 | `FRAME_{CNOT,CZ,H,S,S_DAG,SWAP}` | O(1) — two bit reads, two bit XORs. No amplitude touched. |
   358	| **Array** | 13 | `ARRAY_{CNOT,CZ,SWAP,MULTI_CNOT,MULTI_CZ,H,S,S_DAG,T,T_DAG,ROT,U2,U4}` | O(2^k) — a sweep over amplitude groups. |
   359	| **Expansion** | 4 | `EXPAND`, `EXPAND_T`, `EXPAND_T_DAG`, `EXPAND_ROT` | O(2^k) and **grows k** |
   360	| **Measurement** | 5 | `MEAS_DORMANT_{STATIC,RANDOM}`, `MEAS_ACTIVE_{DIAGONAL,INTERFERE}`, `SWAP_MEAS_INTERFERE` | dormant: O(1). active: O(2^k) reduction, **shrinks k** |
   361	| **Forced variants** | 5 | the five `*_FORCED` mirrors of the measurement family | synthesized at runtime by a bytecode rewrite; read the outcome from a side buffer instead of the PRNG |
   362	| **Classical / noise** | 8 | `APPLY_PAULI`, `NOISE`, `NOISE_BLOCK`, `READOUT_NOISE`, `DETECTOR`, `POSTSELECT`, `OBSERVABLE`, `EXP_VAL` | O(1)–O(words), but **PRNG-consuming** |
   363	
   364	6 + 13 + 4 + 5 + 5 + 8 = 41. ✓
   365	
   366	The `FRAME_*` family is why `clifft` is fast at all: a Clifford gate is two bit
   367	flips. The `ARRAY_*` and `EXPAND` families are where the GPU work is. The noise
   368	family is where, as §11 shows, specialization stops paying.
   369	
   370	Each instruction is exactly **32 bytes** — asserted, not assumed
   371	(`backend.h:163`) — so that two land in one 64-byte cache line
   372	(`backend.h:92`):
   373	
   374	```c
   375	struct alignas(32) Instruction {
   376	    Opcode   opcode;      // offset 0
   377	    uint8_t  _reserved;   // offset 1
   378	    uint8_t  flags;       // offset 2   FLAG_SIGN | FLAG_HIDDEN | FLAG_IDENTITY | FLAG_EXPECTED_ONE
   379	    uint8_t  _pad;        // offset 3
   380	    uint16_t axis_1;      // offset 4   virtual axis (target/control)
   381	    uint16_t axis_2;      // offset 6   virtual axis 2
   382	    union {               // offsets 8..31 — seven payload variants + raw access
   383	        struct { double   weight_re, weight_im;         } math;       // A
   384	        struct { uint32_t classical_idx, expected_val;  } classical;  // B
   385	        struct { uint32_t cp_mask_idx, condition_idx;   } pauli;      // C
   386	        struct { uint64_t mask;                         } multi_gate; // D
   387	        struct { uint32_t cp_idx;                       } u2;         // E  -> fused_u2_nodes
   388	        struct { uint32_t cp_idx;                       } u4;         // F  -> fused_u4_nodes
   389	        struct { uint32_t cp_exp_val_idx, exp_val_idx;  } exp_val;    // G  -> exp_val_masks
   390	        uint8_t raw[24];                                              //     full payload access
   391	    };
   392	};
   393	
   394	static_assert(sizeof(Instruction) == 32, "Instruction must be exactly 32 bytes");
   395	```
   396	
   397	(Padding members elided above; each variant is padded out to the full 24 bytes
   398	in the header.)
   399	
   400	Note variants **E**, **F**, and **G**: their entire payload is a `uint32_t`
   401	*index*. Anything that does not fit in 24 bytes — fused 2×2 and 4×4 unitary
   402	matrices, full N-bit Pauli masks, noise-channel tables — lives in a side
   403	`ConstantPool` and is referenced by index. **That indirection is exactly what
   404	§7's S6 and S8 experiments measure the specialization limit of**: the *index*
   405	folds to an immediate, the *contents* stay in a device buffer. A specializer
   406	can eliminate the load of the index; it cannot eliminate the load of the matrix.
   407	
   408	<figure>
   409	<img src="diagrams/bytecode-layout.svg" alt="32-byte instruction encoding" width="100%">
   410	<figcaption><b>Figure 2.2</b> — The 32-byte instruction. Every field in the
   411	fixed header, and the payload's discriminant, is known to the ahead-of-time
   412	compiler. This is the entire raw material available to a specializer.</figcaption>
   413	</figure>
   414	
   415	### 2.3 The interpreter loop, and why its shape matters
   416	
   417	The CPU reference uses a **computed-goto threaded dispatch table**
   418	(`svm_kernels.inl:2191-2196`), sized to 256 and initialized with designated
   419	initializers so each of the 41 opcodes gets its own indirect-branch history
   420	entry:
   421	
   422	```cpp
   423	#if defined(__GNUC__) || defined(__clang__)
   424	    // Threaded dispatch table (computed gotos) gives each opcode its own
   425	    // indirect-branch history entry, dramatically improving prediction.
   426	    // Sized to 256; designated initializers map enums directly to labels.
   427	    static const void* dispatch_table[256] = {
   428	        [static_cast<uint8_t>(Opcode::OP_FRAME_CNOT)] = &&L_OP_FRAME_CNOT,
   429	        [static_cast<uint8_t>(Opcode::OP_FRAME_CZ)]   = &&L_OP_FRAME_CZ,
   430	        ...
   431	    };
   432	```
   433	
   434	That comment is the thesis of this entire report, stated by the CPU backend
   435	years earlier. **Dispatch is a first-class cost**, and it is worth writing
   436	non-portable code to attack. On a CPU you fight it with branch-target
   437	prediction — the `#if defined(__GNUC__)` guard exists because the technique is a
   438	compiler extension, and someone decided the win justified the portability
   439	fallback path.
   440	
   441	On a GPU there is no branch predictor to help you. A wavefront that hits an
   442	indirect branch does not "predict"; it serializes across whatever divergent
   443	targets its 64 lanes want. The CPU's mitigation is unavailable, which is why the
   444	GPU port cannot use this trick, and why removing dispatch *entirely* — rather
   445	than making it cheaper — is the only move available. That move is worth
   446	**2.8–9.1× on the scalar unit** (§14.2).
   447	
   448	### 2.4 The PRNG, and why it must be bit-exact
   449	
   450	Stochastic simulation means every backend consumes the same random stream in the
   451	same order, or results are not comparable shot-for-shot. `clifft` uses
   452	**xoshiro256++** seeded by **SplitMix64** (`svm.h:21-34`, the Blackman–Vigna
   453	reference implementation, CC0), and converts to a double with an
   454	explicitly-specified expression (`svm.h:148-150`):
   455	
   456	```cpp
   457	// CRITICAL: Do NOT use std::uniform_real_distribution -- its output is
   458	// implementation-defined and varies across compilers (GCC vs Clang vs MSVC).
   459	[[nodiscard]] double random_double() { return static_cast<double>(rng_() >> 11) * 0x1.0p-53; }
   460	```
   461	
   462	The V2 device code reproduces this byte-for-byte (`v2_ops.h:155-177`, `rng_next`/`rng_uniform`), down to
   463	the rotate constants and the same `>> 11` / `0x1.0p-53` mapping:
   464	
   465	```c
   466	static inline u64 v2_rotl64(u64 x, int k) { return (x << k) | (x >> (64 - k)); }
   467	static inline u64 rng_next(u64* s) {
   468	    u64 result = v2_rotl64(s[0] + s[3], 23) + s[0];
   469	    u64 t = s[1] << 17;
   470	    s[2] ^= s[0]; s[3] ^= s[1]; s[1] ^= s[2]; s[0] ^= s[3];
   471	    s[2] ^= t;    s[3] = v2_rotl64(s[3], 45);
   472	    return result;
   473	}
   474	static inline double rng_uniform(u64* s) {
   475	    return (double)(rng_next(s) >> 11) * 0x1.0p-53;
   476	}
   477	```
   478	
   479	Note what is *not* here: no `ocml` call, no device-library dependency, no
   480	fast-math-sensitive arithmetic. The conversion is an integer shift and an exact
   481	power-of-two multiply, so it is bit-identical on any target with IEEE doubles.
   482	This is the one place in the whole pipeline where "compile the same source for
   483	both" was not good enough — the CPU side is C++ and the device side is freestanding
   484	C23, so the sequence had to be transliterated and then verified by output
   485	comparison rather than shared.
   486	
   487	The consequence, and it is the key to understanding §12: **any divergence in how
   488	many random numbers a backend draws is unrecoverable.** Not "slightly different
   489	results" — the two streams decorrelate permanently from the first extra draw.
   490	A branch taken differently, an early-exit that skips a draw, a dust threshold
   491	that clamps on one side and rolls on the other: all of these are the same bug.
   492	
   493	### 2.5 What this means for a GPU
   494	
   495	Three properties of the workload determine everything downstream:
   496	
   497	1. **The hot loop is a butterfly over amplitude pairs**, not a matrix multiply.
   498	   `ARRAY_U2` applies a 2×2 complex matrix to 2^(k-1) amplitude pairs;
   499	   `ARRAY_U4` a 4×4 to 2^(k-2) quadruples. There is no GEMM here — which is why
   500	   **`SQ_INSTS_MFMA` is 0.0 in every counter block collected** — all 52

