   514	
   515	## 3. The original GPU SVM: one interpreter, three tiers
   516	
   517	The first GPU backend is a direct port of the interpreter: one kernel, written
   518	in HIP, compiled once at build time, that walks the same bytecode the CPU walks.
   519	It is the baseline every later generation is measured against, and — three
   520	rewrites later — it is still the thing to beat on one circuit family.
   521	
   522	### 3.1 The kernel is a `for(pc) switch`
   523	
   524	`src/clifft/gpu/sampler/hip_sampler.hip:728-731`:
   525	
   526	```cpp
   527	__device__ void execute_shot(const GpuProgram& program, Rng& rng, ShotState& st) {
   528	    for (uint32_t pc = 0; pc < program.num_instrs; ++pc) {
   529	        const GpuInstr& instr = program.instrs[pc];
   530	        switch (instr.opcode) {
   531	            case static_cast<uint8_t>(clifft::Opcode::OP_FRAME_CNOT):
   532	                frame_cnot(st, instr.axis_1, instr.axis_2);
   533	                break;
   534	            case static_cast<uint8_t>(clifft::Opcode::OP_FRAME_CZ):
   535	                frame_cz(st, instr.axis_1, instr.axis_2);
   536	                break;
   537	            ...
   538	```
   539	
   540	Note what the CPU's computed-goto trick has become: a plain `switch`. There is
   541	no indirect-branch predictor on a GPU to reward threading the dispatch. What
   542	this costs shows up directly in the scalar unit, and §14.2 quantifies it.
   543	
   544	Two properties of this shape are worth naming now, because V2 preserves one and
   545	destroys the other:
   546	
   547	- **Every operand is a runtime load.** `instr.axis_1`, `instr.flags`,
   548	  `instr.mask` — all read from memory, every shot, every instruction.
   549	- **`st.active_k` is a runtime variable.** Every amplitude sweep computes its
   550	  trip count as `1u << (st.active_k - 1)` at runtime, and every scatter index is
   551	  recomputed per amplitude.
   552	
   553	V2 keeps the shot loop and kills both of these (§6, §7).
   554	
   555	### 3.2 Three tiers, because the working set spans 10^7
   556	
   557	A statevector of 1 amplitude and a statevector of 16.7 million cannot use the
   558	same parallelization. The SVM backend therefore ships three kernels, selected by
   559	compiled `peak_rank`:
   560	
   561	| tier | rank | topology | amplitude storage | source |
   562	|---|---|---|---|---|
   563	| **register** | ≤ 4 | 1 shot per **thread** | `GpuComplex v[16]` in VGPRs | `hip_sampler.hip:153,1778` |
   564	| **coop** | 5–10 | 1 shot per **workgroup** (256 threads) | `__shared__ GpuComplex v[1024]` | `hip_sampler.hip:1861` |
   565	| **global** | 11–26 | persistent workgroup pool, work-stealing | HBM slice per workgroup | `hip_sampler.hip:1937` |
   566	
   567	Constants: `kThreadMaxPeakRank = 4`, `kSharedMaxPeakRank = 10`,
   568	`kGlobalMaxPeakRank = 26` (`gpu_types.h:8-18`).
   569	
   570	<figure>
   571	<img src="diagrams/memory-hierarchy-tiers.svg" alt="Three-tier memory hierarchy" width="100%">
   572	<figcaption><b>Figure 3.1</b> — The three tiers map rank onto the memory
   573	hierarchy: VGPRs → LDS → HBM. The tier boundary is where the statevector stops
   574	fitting in the level above.</figcaption>
   575	</figure>
   576	
   577	**Register tier** (`hip_sampler.hip:1778`). One thread = one shot. The
   578	statevector is `GpuComplex v[kThreadMaxAmplitudes]` = 16 complex floats, held in
   579	registers. No LDS, no barriers, no cooperation. Block-level reduction of the
   580	per-shot counters happens once at the end via shared memory.
   581	
   582	**Coop tier** (`hip_sampler.hip:1861-1885`). One workgroup = one shot; 256
   583	threads cooperate on the amplitude sweeps. The entire shot state moves to LDS:
   584	
   585	```cpp
   586	__shared__ GpuComplex v[kSharedMaxAmplitudes];        // 1024 amps  =  8 KB
   587	__shared__ GpuComplex scratch[kSharedMaxAmplitudes/2];//  512 amps  =  4 KB
   588	__shared__ uint8_t    meas[kMaxMeas];                 // 4096 slots =  4 KB
   589	__shared__ uint64_t   px[kPauliWords], pz[kPauliWords];
   590	__shared__ double     red0[256], red1[256];           //            =  4 KB
   591	__shared__ uint32_t   scatter_lut[kSharedMaxAmplitudes/2];
   592	```
   593	
   594	That LDS budget is the coop tier's occupancy constraint, and it is where V2's
   595	first two optimizations went (§9.1). Note `scatter_lut`: the SVM caches computed
   596	scatter indices in LDS, keyed by `(axis1, axis2, k, mode)`. This is a *runtime*
   597	answer to the same problem V2 answers at *compile* time (§7.5) — and the SVM
   598	leaves this LUT **off** for the global tier, which is precisely why V2's global
   599	specialization wins so decisively (§9.3).
   600	
   601	**Global tier** (`hip_sampler.hip:1937`). The statevector no longer fits in LDS,
   602	so each resident workgroup owns an HBM slice and shots are pulled from per-XCD
   603	work counters. The grid is a fixed *pool* of persistent workgroups, not one
   604	workgroup per shot.
   605	
   606	### 3.3 The cooperative reduction
   607	
   608	Active-qubit measurement needs `sum |v_i|^2` over each half of the array — a
   609	reduction across all 256 threads. Both backends implement the identical
   610	two-phase pattern: an intra-wavefront butterfly using `ds_bpermute` (64 lanes,
   611	6 steps), then an inter-wavefront combine through LDS
   612	(`hip_sampler.hip:948`, mirrored in `v2_ops.h:224-262`, `coop_reduce2`).
   613	
   614	<figure>
   615	<img src="diagrams/warp-shuffle-reduction.svg" alt="Two-phase cooperative reduction" width="100%">
   616	<figcaption><b>Figure 3.2</b> — Phase 1: 6-step XOR butterfly within each
   617	64-lane wavefront via <code>ds_bpermute</code>. Phase 2: the four wavefront
   618	partials combine through LDS. The summation <em>order</em> is part of the ABI —
   619	change it and f64 rounding diverges at measurement branch points.</figcaption>
   620	</figure>
   621	
   622	The comment above V2's copy is emphatic and explains a constraint that recurs
   623	throughout this report (`v2_ops.h:235-236`):
   624	
   625	> `// MUST reproduce SVM coop_reduce2's exact summation order or f64 rounding`
   626	> `// diverges at measurement branch points.`
   627	
   628	A reduction is not associative in floating point. Two implementations that sum
   629	the same values in different orders produce different totals in the last bits,
   630	and a measurement whose probability sits near the sampling threshold will then
   631	branch differently — which, per §2.4, desynchronizes the PRNG forever. **The
   632	summation order is part of the ABI.** This single constraint rules out most of
   633	the obvious "optimizations" one would reach for.
   634	
   635	### 3.4 What the SVM backend does well
   636	
   637	It is worth stating plainly, because two successor generations failed to beat it:
   638	
   639	- **One kernel, compiled once.** No per-circuit compile latency, no cache, no
   640	  gate. Ship it and it runs.
   641	- **The dispatch loop keeps IR small.** The bytecode is *data*. A 16,000-op
   642	  circuit and a 4-op circuit compile to the same kernel. V1 forgot this and paid
   643	  221-second compiles for it (§5.3).
   644	- **It is heavily tuned.** The tag history (`svm-opt-1-noinline-extended`,
   645	  `svm-opt-2-noinline-coop-sweeps`, `svm-opt-3-geak-warp-shuffle`,
   646	  `svm-opt-4-scatter-lut`) records four rounds of optimization: selective
   647	  `noinline` to control register pressure, GEAK-derived warp-shuffle reduction,
   648	  and the scatter LUT. **This is not a strawman baseline.**
   649	
   650	### 3.5 What it leaves on the table
   651	
   652	Everything the interpreter re-derives at runtime that was already known when the
   653	circuit was compiled:
   654	
   655	| known at compile time | paid at runtime, every shot |
   656	|---|---|
   657	| the opcode at each `pc` | a `switch` — scalar branch-target computation |
   658	| `axis_1`, `axis_2`, `flags`, `mask` index | a 32-byte instruction load + field extraction |
   659	| `active_k` before each instruction | a load, then `1u << (k-1)` for every trip count |
   660	| the scatter-index pattern for fixed axes | `scatter_bits_2()` per amplitude — or an LDS LUT with a cache-validity check |
   661	| which of a measurement's two paths is live | both paths emitted, one predicated |
   662	
   663	That table is the entire specialization opportunity, and §7 turns each row into
   664	a measured experiment.
   665	
   666	---
   667	
   668	## 4. Hybrid: compiling the circuit into HIP, and why it stalled
   669	
   670	### 4.1 The idea
   671	
   672	If the operands are known when the circuit is compiled, generate a bespoke
   673	kernel per circuit. The Hybrid backend (`src/clifft/gpu/codegen/`) does exactly
   674	that: it walks the bytecode, emits HIP source with the operands as literals, and
   675	compiles it ahead of the sampling run.
   676	
   677	The emitter is structured around a **used-function analysis**
   678	(`kernel_codegen.h:17-50`): a `UsedFunctions` struct with one boolean per opcode
   679	family, populated by scanning the bytecode, used to emit only the device
   680	functions the circuit actually needs. A pure-Clifford circuit does not carry the
   681	`ARRAY_U4` code at all.
   682	
   683	### 4.2 The compile path — and the first appearance of an HSA/HIP split
   684	
   685	`kernel_cache.cc:105-170`:
   686	
   687	```cpp
   688	// Step 1: Compile HIP source → offload bundle (.bundle)
   689	// --offload-device-only produces a Clang Offload Bundle, not a raw ELF.
   690	// HSA needs a raw AMDGCN ELF, so we unbundle in step 2.
   691	cmd << clangpp << " -x hip"
   692	    << " --offload-arch=" << gpu_arch
   693	    << " -O2 -ffast-math"
   694	    << " --offload-device-only"
   695	    << " -o " << bundle_path << " " << tmp_src;
   696	```
   697	
   698	then `clang-offload-bundler --unbundle` to extract the raw ELF, then
   699	`hsa_load_kernel(hsaco_path, kernel_func_name, 0)` (`kernel_cache.cc:191`).
   700	
   701	**Hybrid already dispatches through HSA, not `hipModuleLoad`.** It uses HIP only
   702	as a *source language*, and then has to fight its way back out of HIP's
   703	packaging to get an object HSA will accept. That awkwardness — writing HIP to
   704	get a device binary, then unwrapping the HIP container to hand it to HSA — is
   705	the seed of V2's decision to drop HIP entirely (§13).
   706	
   707	Note also `-ffast-math`. Hybrid was allowed reassociation; V2 explicitly is not
   708	(`-ffp-contract=off`, no fast-math, §6.4), because V2's correctness contract is
   709	byte-exactness against the interpreter and fast-math would break it.
   710	
   711	### 4.3 The result: correct, competitive, never ahead
   712	
   713	The v1-era measurement (`docs/v2/reference_v1.md:270-278`, 10,000 shots,
   714	MI350X, `sample_seconds`) shows Hybrid at or slightly better than SVM:
   715	
   716	| circuit | SVM | **Hybrid** | MLIR (v1) |
   717	|---|---|---|---|
   718	| frame_h | 0.066 | **0.053** | 0.271 |
   719	| qv10 | 0.074 | **0.058** | 3.54 |
   720	| circuit_d5 | 0.078 | **0.065** | 1.22 |
   721	| surface_d9_t10 | 0.090 | 0.112 | 5.50 |
   722	
   723	> **[unverified, v1-era]** — these are `sample_seconds` (host wall time
   724	> including setup), not isolated kernel time, from a run whose node identity was
   725	> not recorded. They are quoted for their *shape*, not their magnitude: Hybrid
   726	> tracks SVM within ±25%, and MLIR is off the chart. That shape is corroborated
   727	> by every later measurement.
   728	
   729	Hybrid wins by 10–20% on three of four and loses by 25% on the fourth. After
   730	per-circuit code generation, an entire compile-cache subsystem, and an
   731	offload-bundle unwrapping dance, the answer is **noise-level**.
   732	
   733	### 4.4 Why it stalled — the density measurement
   734	
   735	The cleanest diagnosis comes from an artifact built for this report:
   736	`V2_performance/lowering/ir_density.csv`, which counts lines of emitted source
   737	per bytecode instruction.
   738	
   739	| circuit | instrs | Hybrid HIP lines | **lines/instr** |
   740	|---|---|---|---|
   741	| reg_circuit_d3 | 344 | 5,301 | 15.4 |
   742	| coop_qv10 | 140 | 7,805 | 55.8 |
   743	| coop_circuit_d5 | 1,720 | 33,508 | 19.5 |
   744	| coop_surface_d7_t10 | 4,134 | 75,409 | 18.2 |
   745	| glob_surface_d7_t19 | 4,296 | 75,794 | 17.6 |
   746	
   747	Hybrid emits a **block** per instruction — 15 to 56 lines of HIP each. It is
   748	linear in circuit size with a large constant. That is not pathological (V1's is,
   749	§5), but it means:
   750	
   751	- the compiler's problem grows with the circuit, so compile time does too;
   752	- register pressure grows with the circuit, because the body is one long
   753	  straight-line region;
   754	- the win from constant operands is diluted by the sheer volume of code the
   755	  optimizer must chew through.
   756	
   757	**The lesson Hybrid teaches, and the reason it is in this report:** per-circuit
   758	compilation is not, by itself, a performance strategy. What matters is *what you
   759	emit per instruction*. Hybrid emitted a block. V2 emits a call (§6.3), and the
   760	same idea that netted Hybrid ~0 % nets V2 **1.3–4.0×**.
   761	
   762	Note the shape of the density column, because it is the whole argument in one
   763	number: Hybrid's lines-per-instruction is 15.4–55.8 and does not improve with
   764	scale, while V2's converges *downward* to 1.00–1.27 on every circuit large
   765	enough to matter — 1.00–1.11 on the three Clifford-dominated ones, and 1.27 on
   766	`qv10`, whose fused unitaries are the one gate family V2 still emits more than
   767	one line for. Same input, same target, same compiler — a 14–44× difference in
   768	how much code the optimizer is handed.
   769	
   770	---
   771	
   772	## 5. V1: the MLIR backend and its failure
   773	
   774	V1 is the generation that did not work. It is documented here at length because
   775	its failure defines V2's design constraints — every rule in V2's architecture
   776	(§6) exists because V1 violated it and paid.
   777	
   778	### 5.1 The original idea
   779	
   780	Hybrid proved that per-circuit code generation was *possible* but not
   781	*profitable*. The V1 hypothesis was that the problem was the intermediate
   782	representation: HIP source is a poor target for a code generator, clang has to
   783	re-parse it, and everything interesting is buried under a general-purpose
   784	frontend. Emit **MLIR** instead — a compiler IR designed to be produced by
   785	machines — and the generator gains direct access to the optimizer.
   786	
   787	The pipeline (`src/clifft/gpu/mlir/`):
   788	
   789	```
   790	bytecode → mlir_emit.cc (~3,425 lines) → .mlir text
   791	         → mlir-opt --canonicalize --cse --convert-func-to-llvm
   792	         → mlir-translate --mlir-to-llvmir
   793	         → opt -O2 → llc → ld.lld → .hsaco
   794	```
   795	
   796	The stated goals were the ones MLIR is genuinely good at: a multi-level IR where
   797	quantum-specific structure survives into the middle end, custom passes that
   798	understand `EXPAND`/`MEAS` rank algebra, progressive lowering from a domain
   799	dialect down to `amdgpu`.
   800	
   801	### 5.2 What was actually built — three verified findings
   802	
   803	The audit checked those goals against the artifacts. All three failed.
   804	
   805	**Finding 1: V1's MLIR was 100% `llvm` dialect.** A dialect census on the
   806	emitted IR (`lowering/v1/frame_h.1_emitted.mlir`, `circuit_d3.1_emitted.mlir`):
   807	
   808	| dialect op | frame_h | circuit_d3 |
   809	|---|---|---|
   810	| `func.func` | **0** | **0** |
   811	| `arith.*` | **0** | **0** |
   812	| `scf.*` | **0** | **0** |
   813	| `memref.*` | **0** | **0** |
   814	| `llvm.func` | 6 | 7 |
   815	
   816	There is no multi-level IR here. **V1 used MLIR as a text serialization format
   817	for LLVM IR.** The op census confirms it — the emitted `frame_h` module is
   818	`llvm.mlir.constant` × 240, `llvm.ptr` × 192, `llvm.shl` × 73, `llvm.xor` × 68…
   819	Pointer arithmetic and bit twiddling, written in MLIR's syntax.
   820	
   821	**Finding 2: V1 authored zero custom passes.** `mlir_codegen.cc:65-67` (and
   822	verbatim again at `:172` and `:239`) runs
   823	exactly three stock upstream passes:
   824	
   825	```
   826	mlir-opt --canonicalize --cse --convert-func-to-llvm
   827	```
   828	
   829	No domain dialect, no rank-algebra pass, no quantum-aware transformation. The
   830	"progressive lowering" that motivated the choice of MLIR was never written.
   831	
   832	**Finding 3: one of those three passes is a provable no-op.** Per-pass snapshots
   833	via `mlir-opt --mlir-print-ir-tree-dir` (`lowering/v1_passes/frame_h/`) show the
   834	diff between `1_cse.mlir` and `2_convert-func-to-llvm.mlir` in full:
   835	
   836	```diff
   837	--- 1_cse.mlir
   838	+++ 2_convert-func-to-llvm.mlir
   839	@@ -1,7 +1,7 @@
   840	-// -----// IR Dump After CSE (cse) //----- //
   841	+// -----// IR Dump After ConvertFuncToLLVMPass (convert-func-to-llvm) //----- //
   842	 module attributes {llvm.target_triple = "amdgcn-amd-amdhsa"} {
   843	   llvm.func @llvm.amdgcn.workitem.id.x() -> i32
   844	   llvm.func @llvm.amdgcn.workgroup.id.x() -> i32
   845	   llvm.func @llvm.amdgcn.s.barrier()
   846	   llvm.func @llvm.amdgcn.ds.bpermute(i32, i32) -> i32
   847	   llvm.func @clifft_log(%arg0: f64) -> f64 {
   848	```
   849	
   850	**Two lines changed, and both are the "IR Dump After" banner comment.** 608
   851	lines in, 608 lines out, byte-identical apart from the header. There is no
   852	`func` dialect to convert because the emitter never produced any. Rendered at
   853	`V2_performance/lowering/diffs/v1pass.frame_h.1_cse__2_convert-func-to-llvm.{diff,html}`.
   854	
   855	What the other two passes did accomplish, on `frame_h`: 947 lines → 736
   856	(canonicalize) → 608 (cse). Constant deduplication (`llvm.mlir.constant`
   857	240 → 59) and redundant `shl`/`and`/`xor` elimination. Real, but this is
   858	cleanup after a sloppy emitter, not compilation.
   859	
   860	<figure>
   861	<img src="diagrams/v1-mlir-reality.svg" alt="V1's intended vs actual MLIR usage" width="100%">
   862	<figcaption><b>Figure 5.1</b> — Intended (left): a domain dialect lowered
   863	progressively through custom passes. Actual (right): direct <code>llvm</code>-dialect
   864	emission, three stock passes, one of them a no-op. The middle levels were never
   865	built.</figcaption>
   866	</figure>
   867	
   868	### 5.3 The actual failure: unrolling the operand sequence
   869	
   870	The choice of IR was not V1's problem. Its problem was structural, and it is
   871	visible in a single line of the emitter — `mlir_emit.cc:2377`:
   872	
   873	```cpp
   874	    out << "  // --- Instruction sequence (" << flat.instrs.size() << " ops) ---\n";
   875	    g_emit_ak = 0;
   876	
   877	    for (size_t pc = 0; pc < flat.instrs.size(); ++pc) {
   878	        const GpuInstr& ins = flat.instrs[pc];
   879	```
   880	
   881	That `for` loop is on the **host, at emit time**. `pc` is a C++ variable that
   882	never appears in the generated program. Every bytecode instruction becomes its
   883	own block of IR, so the program counter is resolved by the code generator and
   884	the GPU never has one: **V1 flattened the entire bytecode into straight-line IR
   885	with no runtime loop over instructions.**
   886	
   887	The contrast with V2 is one character of scope. V2's emitter has the same host
   888	loop, but what it writes into it is a *call* — `v2_op_expand(st, v, 3u);` — and
   889	the device program keeps its own `for` over the instruction array. Same loop,
   890	different side of the compile.
   891	
   892	Every bytecode instruction became an inlined block of IR. A circuit is not data
   893	walked at runtime; it is *code*. The consequences compound:
   894	
   895	| effect | measured | source |
   896	|---|---|---|
   897	| IR size | `surface_d11_t5` (16,432 ops) → **19,856,511 bytes of LLVM IR** | `results/d11err_49530.log:19` |
   898	| Compile time | `glob_surface_d7_t19`: **221.48 s** end-to-end | `lowering/matrix.csv` |
   899	| Optimizer viability | size-adaptive fallback: >4 MB → `llc -O2` + `opt -O1`; >16 MB → `llc -O1`, `opt` skipped entirely | `mlir_kernel_cache.cc:105-131` |
   900	| Register allocation | one enormous basic-block chain; the allocator has no loop structure to work with | — |
   901	
   902	That third row deserves emphasis. **The build system had to detune the optimizer
   903	based on file size** to make links complete at all — the constants are literal
   904	in the source:
   905	
   906	```cpp
   907	    size_t ir_bytes = llvmir.size();
   908	    const size_t kLargeIrThreshold = 4u * 1024 * 1024;   // 4 MB
   909	    const size_t kHugeIrThreshold  = 16u * 1024 * 1024;  // 16 MB
   910	    bool large_ir = ir_bytes > kLargeIrThreshold;
   911	    bool huge_ir = ir_bytes > kHugeIrThreshold;
   912	    const char* llc_opt = huge_ir ? "-O1" : (large_ir ? "-O2" : "-O3");
   913	```
   914	
   915	with the maintainer's own rationale six lines above it
   916	(`mlir_kernel_cache.cc:88-92`):
   917	
   918	```cpp
   919	// Large fully-unrolled circuits (surface codes emit 40MB+ IR from U2/U4
   920	// inlining) make opt -O3 + llc -O3 take >300s. For large/huge IR we lower the
   921	// opt level (and skip the separate opt pass) so compilation completes.
   922	// Correctness is unaffected — only optimization aggressiveness changes.
   923	```
   924	
   925	When a code generator's output requires the optimizer to be turned down, the
   926	generator is the problem.
   927	
   928	And the detuning was not sufficient. On `surface_d11_t5` the pipeline ran to
   929	completion and still produced nothing loadable:
   930	
   931	```
   932	[clifft-mlir] generated 19856511 bytes of LLVM-IR for 16432 ops
   933	[clifft-mlir-mlir] large IR (18 MB): llc -O0, opt skipped
   934	[clifft-mlir-mlir] compiled in 32435.2 ms (19391 KB IR)
   935	[clifft-hsa-dispatch] cannot open .../mlir_db5b1a0c2e405879_gfx950.hsaco
   936	```
   937	
   938	32 seconds of compilation, then the `.hsaco` does not exist. The 10-shot
   939	correctness sweep across the whole corpus (`results/fullsweep_49529.log`, job
   940	49529) shows this is not a `surface_d11` quirk — 18 circuits pass, and the five
   941	that fail all fail the same way:
   942	
   943	```
   944	surface_d9_t19            13        1        1 OK
   945	surface_d11_t5             3        0     FAIL NOCOMPILE
   946	surface_d11_t10            7        0     FAIL NOCOMPILE
   947	surface_d11_t15           11        0     FAIL NOCOMPILE
   948	surface_d11_t19           14        0     FAIL NOCOMPILE
   949	qv10                      10    10000    10000 OK
   950	qv20_seed42               20        ?     FAIL NOCOMPILE
   951	```
   952	
   953	Look at the rank column, because it rules out the obvious explanation.
   954	`surface_d11_t5` fails at **rank 3**; `surface_d9_t19` passes at **rank 13**.
   955	The failures are not the high-rank circuits — rank drives *state size*, and
   956	V1's global tier handled rank 13 fine.
   957	
   958	What the five failures share is **emitted IR volume**, which is instruction
   959	count × per-instruction density, and they arrive there by two different routes:
   960	
   961	| failing circuit | instrs | density driver | est. IR |
   962	|---|---|---|---|
   963	| `surface_d11_*` | **16,432** | ordinary (~56 lines/instr) | 19.9 MB, *measured* |
   964	| `qv20_seed42` | **418** | **U2/U4-dense** (`qv10`: 2,407 lines/instr) | ~1.0 M lines |
   965	
   966	`surface_d11_t5` is long and ordinary. `qv20_seed42` is short and *dense* —
   967	418 instructions, but a QV circuit is nearly all fused unitaries, and §5.3's
   968	U2/U4 mechanism inflates each one by up to 1,500 IR ops. Different route, same
   969	wall.
   970	
   971	That is the whole diagnosis. V1's limit was never the physics; it was how much
   972	IR the circuit expanded into, because V1 turned every instruction into code
   973	instead of data. Both routes to a large program are closed by the same fix, and
   974	that fix is V2's first rule (§6.1).
   975	
   976	V1 fought the symptom repeatedly. Two bloat sources were mitigated
   977	(`reference_v1.md:243-250`):
   978	
   979	- `OP_NOISE_BLOCK` used to unroll one full site-block per site — ~1,200 sites for
   980	  `circuit_d5`, ~100k lines / 32 MB of IR that OOM'd `llc`. Converted to a
   981	  runtime loop over the site index.
   982	- `log(x)` and `draw_next_noise` were inlined at every noise draw
   983	  (`surface_d9_t5`: 1,444 draws × ~110/~317 lines each). Hoisted to shared
   984	  `@clifft_log` / `@clifft_draw_next_noise` functions.
   985	
   986	Both fixes are *the V2 principle applied locally*: replace an unroll with a
   987	loop, replace an inline with a call. They worked. They were just never
   988	generalized to the thing that mattered — the instruction sequence itself.
   989	
   990	And the unmitigated case is spectacular. `OP_ARRAY_U2`/`U4` remained
   991	matrix-unrolled: each unrolls per incoming-frame-state branch (U2: 4 branches,
   992	~150–200 IR ops; **U4: up to 16 branches, ~800–1,500+ IR ops *per
   993	instruction***), each branch wrapping a fully unrolled complex matmul
   994	(`mlir_emit.cc:1813,1920`). `mlir_kernel_cache.cc:89-90` records the outcome:
   995	"surface codes emit 40MB+ IR from U2/U4 inlining."
   996	
   997	The density measurement makes this concrete
   998	(`V2_performance/lowering/ir_density.csv`):
   999	
  1000	| circuit | instrs | v1 MLIR lines | **lines/instr** |

