  1051	nine, and they are reproduced in full because the two outliers are the argument:
  1052	
  1053	| circuit | rank | SVM_s | HYB_s | **MLIR_s** | MLIR/HYB |
  1054	|---|---|---|---|---|---|
  1055	| `frame_h` | 0 | 0.0658 | 0.0534 | **0.2712** | 5.1× |
  1056	| `circuit_d3` | 4 | 0.0729 | 0.0552 | **0.4509** | 8.2× |
  1057	| `surface_d7_t5` | 3 | 0.0790 | 0.0781 | **2.1165** | 27.1× |
  1058	| **`qv10`** | 10 | 0.0741 | 0.0576 | **3.5449** | **61.5×** |
  1059	| `circuit_d5` | 10 | 0.0778 | 0.0649 | **1.2230** | 18.9× |
  1060	| `surface_d7_t10` | 7 | 0.0794 | 0.0783 | **2.4039** | 30.7× |
  1061	| `surface_d7_t19` | 12 | 0.0839 | 0.0827 | **2.5316** | 30.6× |
  1062	| `surface_d9_t10` | 7 | 0.0896 | 0.1117 | **5.5035** | 49.3× |
  1063	| `surface_d9_t19` | 13 | 0.1018 | — | **aborted** | — |
  1064	
  1065	> **[v1-era, quoted for shape]** — `sample_seconds` (host wall, not kernel
  1066	> time), node unrecorded, superseded toolchain. Not comparable to §15's
  1067	> ratios, which are kernel-time on a pinned node. The shot count is not
  1068	> recorded either: the log preserves the harness command only for the run that
  1069	> aborted, and that one used `--shots 10`. Treat the ratios as the finding and
  1070	> the absolute seconds as unnormalized.
  1071	
  1072	Two things in this table are load-bearing.
  1073	
  1074	**`qv10` is the outlier in both columns.** It is the densest IR in the corpus
  1075	(2,407 lines/instr, §5.3) *and* the worst slowdown (61.5×). No other circuit
  1076	comes close on either axis. The IR-density measurement and the runtime
  1077	measurement were taken by different tools, from different artifacts, at
  1078	different times — and they agree on which circuit is worst. That is the
  1079	corroboration that matters, and it is why the mechanism in §5.3 (U2/U4
  1080	unrolling) is the accepted explanation rather than one hypothesis among
  1081	several.
  1082	
  1083	**The last row is not a slow number; it is a missing one.** `surface_d9_t19`
  1084	aborted under a 250-second timeout — and the log records the harness command
  1085	verbatim, which is worth reading closely:
  1086	
  1087	```
  1088	timeout 250 "$BIN" --circuit "$f" --shots 10 --seed 42 --mlir
  1089	```
  1090	
  1091	**Ten shots**, not ten thousand. V1 could not finish *ten* shots of
  1092	`surface_d9_t19` inside four minutes, on a circuit SVM completes in 0.10 s at
  1093	10,000. The job also ended with `Detected 7 oom_kill events`, so the wall V1 hit
  1094	here is memory as much as time.
  1095	
  1096	A 61× slowdown is a performance result. The compile failures in §5.3 are a
  1097	viability result, and that is the one that ended V1.
  1098	
  1099	### 5.5 The spill claim: real, but mis-attributed
  1100	
  1101	`reference_v1.md:265` states that **"the register-tier kernel reports private =
  1102	115 KB/thread"** of spill scratch. The number is real. The tier is wrong, and
  1103	the distinction matters.
  1104	
  1105	The only 115 KB in the entire artifact corpus is `results/gt19_49517.log:11`:
  1106	
  1107	```
  1108	[clifft-hsa-dispatch] loaded kernel 'compiled_mlir_kernel_global' from
  1109	  .../mlir_global_7989aa5d4826ef77_gfx950.hsaco
  1110	  (private=115224 group=8248 kernarg=352 ...)
  1111	```
  1112	
  1113	`compiled_mlir_kernel_global` — the **global** tier, on `surface_d7_t19`. A
  1114	sweep of every `private=` line ever logged puts a hard ceiling on each tier:
  1115	
  1116	| v1 kernel | max recorded `private=` |
  1117	|---|---|
  1118	| `compiled_mlir_kernel` (register) | 4,192 B |
  1119	| `compiled_mlir_kernel_coop` | 608 B |
  1120	| `compiled_mlir_kernel_global` | **115,224 B** |
  1121	
  1122	The register tier never exceeded 4 KB. 115 KB is a global-tier number, and it
  1123	is 27× the worst register-tier figure — so the corrected attribution makes the
  1124	fact *more* striking, not less.
  1125	
  1126	One further qualification the design doc omits, visible two lines up in the same
  1127	log: that kernel was built at **`llc -O0` with `opt` skipped**, because 9,967,049
  1128	bytes of IR tripped the huge-IR fallback from §5.3. So 115 KB/thread is not what
  1129	the optimizer produced from V1's IR — it is what the optimizer produced *after
  1130	being switched off* because V1's IR was too large to optimize. The two failures
  1131	are the same failure, reported once each.
  1132	
  1133	The independently measured artifacts agree with the ceiling. From
  1134	`lowering/matrix.csv`, ISA-level `ScratchSize` at `-O2` (the level the matrix
  1135	uses, not the fallback):
  1136	
  1137	| v1 circuit | tier | ScratchSize | VGPR | occupancy |
  1138	|---|---|---|---|---|
  1139	| `reg_frame_h` | register | 0 B | 16 | 8 |
  1140	| `reg_four_t` | register | 0 B | 16 | 8 |
  1141	| `reg_circuit_d3` | register | **156 B** | 128 | 4 |
  1142	| `coop_qv10` | coop | 0 B | 53 | 8 |
  1143	| `coop_circuit_d5` | coop | 48 B | 73 | 6 |
  1144	| `glob_surface_d7_t19` | global | **320 B** | 128 | 4 |
  1145	
  1146	Note the VGPR column, which is the register-tier story the scratch number was
  1147	being used to tell: `circuit_d3` pins **128 VGPRs — the architectural
  1148	maximum — and drops to occupancy 4**, while the two small circuits sit at 16
  1149	VGPRs and occupancy 8. The unrolled body does exhaust the register file. It
  1150	just does so by *saturating* VGPRs on the register tier rather than by spilling
  1151	115 KB, and spills catastrophically only once it reaches the global tier with
  1152	the optimizer disabled.
  1153	
  1154	The emitter itself documents fighting this. Note *which way* the trade-off runs
  1155	(`mlir_emit.cc:216-218`):
  1156	
  1157	```cpp
  1158	    // alignment=8 for coop/global tiers enables ds_read_b64 / global_load_b64
  1159	    // EXCLUDED from register tier to avoid VGPR spill catastrophe (P1.9)
  1160	    const char* align = (lds_amplitudes || cooperative_mode) ? " {alignment = 8 : i64}" : "";
  1161	```
  1162	
  1163	V1 gave up wide 64-bit amplitude loads on the register tier — a real
  1164	throughput loss — purely to keep the allocator from spilling. That is a
  1165	generator paying for its own IR shape with the hardware's memory bandwidth.
  1166	
  1167	This is the report's ground rule cutting in both directions. The first audit
  1168	pass rejected 115 KB as unreproducible — wrong; it was looking at
  1169	`matrix.csv`'s register rows for a global-tier number. Design documents are
  1170	claims rather than facts, but so is any single artifact read in isolation.
  1171	
  1172	### 5.6 The duplication tax
  1173	
  1174	A structural cost worth naming, because V2 fixes it directly. By V1, every
  1175	opcode was implemented **three to four times**: the CPU SVM interpreter, the GPU
  1176	SVM interpreter, the Hybrid HIP template, and the MLIR emitter — with register
  1177	and coop variants doubling several of those. A fix in one did not propagate.
  1178	
  1179	Tracing a single opcode across the tree shows the shape. `MULTI_CNOT` has
  1180	semantic implementations in:
  1181	
  1182	| file | role |
  1183	|---|---|
  1184	| `svm/svm_kernels.inl` | CPU + GPU SVM interpreter (the de-facto spec) |
  1185	| `gpu/sampler/hip_sampler.hip` | GPU SVM dispatch |
  1186	| `gpu/codegen/ops/multi_qubit_ops.inc` | Hybrid HIP-source emitter |
  1187	| `gpu/mlir/ops/mlir_array_ops.inc` | **V1 MLIR emitter** — where the bug was |
  1188	| `gpu/mlir/v2/v2_ops_body.inc` | V2 shared operand library |
  1189	
  1190	Four independent transcriptions of one gate's semantics, plus V2's. The last
  1191	row is the fix: V2 adds a *fifth* file precisely so it can eventually be the
  1192	only one.
  1193	
  1194	The canonical incident (`reference_v1.md:228-235`): `OP_ARRAY_MULTI_CNOT` had
  1195	`px`/`pz` **swapped only in the MLIR frame update**, while SVM and Hybrid were
  1196	correct. The post-mortem is still inline at
  1197	`src/clifft/gpu/mlir/ops/mlir_array_ops.inc:428-435`:
  1198	
  1199	```cpp
  1200	    // SVM ref exec_array_multi_cnot -> exec_frame_cnot (svm_kernels.inl:502/exec_frame_cnot).
  1201	    // NOTE: read pz[target] ONCE up front — the per-control pz[c] ^= pz[target]
  1202	    // never touches pz[target] (target is not a control), so it stays stable;
  1203	    // likewise accumulate Σ px[c] before writing px[target]. (The previous
  1204	    // emission had px/pz swapped here, corrupting the frame for controlled
  1205	    // measurements that follow — the circuit_d5 coop MX bug.)
  1206	```
  1207	
  1208	Read what that comment is doing: it cites the SVM implementation by file and
  1209	line as the *specification*, then re-derives the correct ordering by hand.
  1210	That is the duplication tax made literal — the correctness of one backend
  1211	maintained by a prose reference to another, checked by a human.
  1212	
  1213	Only cross-backend output diffing catches this class of bug, and because the
  1214	GPU legitimately differs from the CPU in f32-vs-f64 rounding, there is no
  1215	single bit-exact oracle to diff against — "wrong" and "just f32" look alike.
  1216	Note also *which* backend was wrong: the newest one, with the most code
  1217	generated per opcode.
  1218	
  1219	V2's answer is a single shared operand library (`v2_ops.h`) compiled two ways
  1220	(§6.2), which makes the interpreter and the specialized kernel byte-exact *by
  1221	construction* rather than by testing.
  1222	
  1223	### 5.7 The three lessons V2 is built on
  1224	
  1225	1. **Never unroll the operand sequence.** The bytecode is data. This is the
  1226	   disease, and it is the only disease — V1's IR choice was irrelevant.
  1227	2. **One implementation of each opcode.** Divergence between backends is
  1228	   invisible until an output diff, and there is no exact oracle.
  1229	3. **Small IR is not an aesthetic preference.** It is what keeps the optimizer
  1230	   at full strength and the register allocator able to see loop structure.
  1231	
  1232	---
  1233	
  1234	## 6. V2: the architecture
  1235	
  1236	### 6.1 The one rule, stated precisely
  1237	
  1238	V1's disease was unrolling the operand **sequence**. The naive lesson — "don't
  1239	unroll anything" — is wrong and would give up the entire specialization
  1240	opportunity. V2's design document (`docs/v2/V2.md:104-132`, §3.1) is precise
  1241	about three *different* loops with *different* rules:
  1242	
  1243	| loop | rule | why |
  1244	|---|---|---|
  1245	| **over shots** | **ALWAYS a runtime loop** | shots are data; a persistent kernel with work-stealing pulls them |
  1246	| **over the bytecode** | **ALWAYS a runtime loop*** | the operand sequence is data; unrolling it is the disease |
  1247	| **over amplitude groups** | **stays a loop**, but bounds/constants may specialize | at rank 10 that is 256–512 groups; flattening reinstates the bloat |
  1248	| **the 2×2 / 4×4 butterfly** | **fully unrolled** | a constant 4–16 multiply-adds; no size risk |
  1249	
  1250	*The asterisk on the bytecode loop is the whole invention. V2 does not *unroll*
  1251	the bytecode loop — it **removes** it, by emitting one **call** per instruction
  1252	instead of one **block**. The distinction sounds like hair-splitting and is the
  1253	entire result: a call site is O(1) IR regardless of what the callee does. §6.3
  1254	measures it.
  1255	
  1256	<figure>
  1257	<img src="diagrams/three-loops.svg" alt="The three loops and their rules" width="100%">
  1258	<figcaption><b>Figure 6.1</b> — The three nested loops. V1 flattened the middle
  1259	one into code. V2 replaces it with a call sequence whose <em>operands</em> are
  1260	constants but whose <em>bodies</em> are shared and still contain their own
  1261	loops.</figcaption>
  1262	</figure>
  1263	
  1264	### 6.2 One operand library, compiled two ways
  1265	
  1266	The substrate is `src/clifft/gpu/mlir/v2/v2_ops.h` + `v2_ops_body.inc`: every
  1267	opcode body as a `static inline` function taking its operands **and the pre-op
  1268	`active_k`** by value.
  1269	
  1270	```c
  1271	static inline void v2_op_array_cnot(V2State* st, CV2Complex* v,
  1272	                                    u32 active_k, u32 a1, u32 a2);
  1273	static inline void v2_op_meas_dormant_static(V2State* st, u32 axis,
  1274	                                             u32 slot, u32 flags);
  1275	```
  1276	
  1277	That signature is the whole trick. The **same source** serves two consumers:
  1278	
  1279	- the **interpreter** (`coop_interpreter.c`) passes runtime values —
  1280	  `v2_op_array_cnot(st, v, st->active_k, in.axis_1, in.axis_2)`;
  1281	- the **specializer** (`v2_specializer.cc`) emits the same call with literals —
  1282	  `v2_op_array_cnot(st, v, 8u, 2u, 5u)`.
  1283	
  1284	**Byte-exactness is by construction, not by testing.** The bodies are the same
  1285	bodies. This directly retires V1's duplication tax (§5.6).
  1286	
  1287	The tier dimension is handled by preprocessor parameterization of *cooperation*,
  1288	not of arithmetic (`v2_ops.h:128-152`, quoted verbatim with the barrier body
  1289	elided to §11.2):
  1290	
  1291	```c
  1292	#ifdef V2_REGISTER
  1293	#  define V2_STRIDE 1u
  1294	static inline u32 v2_tid(void)  { return 0u; }
  1295	static inline void v2_barrier(void) {}            // no cooperation, no barrier
  1296	#  define V2_REDUCE2(T, L0, L1, O0, O1) do { *(O0) = (L0); *(O1) = (L1); } while (0)
  1297	#  define IS_OWNER 1
  1298	#else
  1299	#  define V2_STRIDE 256u
  1300	static inline u32 v2_tid(void)  { return __builtin_amdgcn_workitem_id_x(); }
  1301	static inline void v2_barrier(void) { /* release fence; s_barrier; acquire fence — §11.2 */ }
  1302	#  define V2_REDUCE2(T, L0, L1, O0, O1) coop_reduce2((T), (L0), (L1), (O0), (O1))
  1303	#  define IS_OWNER (t == 0)
  1304	#endif
  1305	```
  1306	
  1307	`V2_REDUCE2` in the register tier is not a stub for "reduction not needed" — it
  1308	is an assignment. A stride-1 loop over all amplitudes has already accumulated
  1309	both partial sums into `L0`/`L1`, so the cross-lane reduction degenerates to a
  1310	copy. Same call site, same argument list, two different meanings, zero runtime
  1311	branch.
  1312	
  1313	**The same opcode arithmetic compiles two ways from one source.** Register tier:
  1314	1 shot/thread, private state, stride 1, reduction is the identity because a
  1315	stride-1 loop has already summed everything. Coop/global: 256 threads/shot,
  1316	stride 256, fenced barriers, butterfly reduction.
  1317	
  1318	### 6.3 What the specializer emits
  1319	
  1320	`v2_specializer.cc:26-97` is a `switch` over opcodes that prints exactly one
  1321	line each — 35 of the 41 opcodes are handled; the six that are not (the five
  1322	`*_FORCED` variants and `OP_EXP_VAL`) `return false`, which aborts emission and
  1323	falls the whole circuit back to the interpreter (`v2_specializer.cc:92-93`).
  1324	
  1325	The register-tier `circuit_d3` output (`lowering/v2_src/reg_circuit_d3.c`, 383
  1326	lines for 344 instructions) is reproduced verbatim below, lines 1–3 and 15–25
  1327	and 42–49 and 360–361, with `…` marking the elisions:
  1328	
  1329	```c
  1330	#define V2_REGISTER 1
  1331	#define V2_NOISE_ATTR __attribute__((noinline))
  1332	#include "clifft/gpu/mlir/v2/v2_ops.h"
  1333	…
  1334	    v2_shot_init(st, v, amp_capacity, shot_id, num_observables, seed, noise_hazards, num_noise_sites);
  1335	    v2_op_meas_dormant_static(st, 6u, 33u, 0u);
  1336	    v2_op_meas_dormant_static(st, 8u, 32u, 0u);
  1337	    v2_op_meas_dormant_static(st, 11u, 31u, 0u);
  1338	    v2_op_meas_dormant_random(st, 13u, 30u, 0u);
  1339	    v2_op_meas_dormant_random(st, 5u, 29u, 0u);
  1340	    v2_op_meas_dormant_random(st, 0u, 28u, 0u);
  1341	…
  1342	    v2_op_noise_block(st, noise_sites, noise_channels, noise_hazards, num_noise_sites, 0u, 131u);
  1343	    v2_op_frame_cnot(st, 3u, 10u);
  1344	    v2_op_frame_cnot(st, 3u, 12u);
  1345	    v2_op_frame_cz(st, 3u, 6u);
  1346	    v2_op_frame_cz(st, 3u, 8u);
  1347	    v2_op_frame_swap(st, 3u, 0u);
  1348	    v2_op_frame_h(st, 0u);
  1349	    v2_op_expand_t(st, v, 0u, 0u, 1);          // <- active_k = 0, tracked statically
  1350	…
  1351	    v2_shot_aggregate(st, block_counts, num_observables, expected_obs_mask);
  1352	}
  1353	```
  1354	
  1355	**Count check.** Lines 16–360 of that file contain exactly **344** `v2_op_*`
  1356	call statements, one per bytecode instruction, no more and no fewer. The 39
  1357	non-call lines are the 14-line `spec_body` signature, the `v2_shot_init` /
  1358	`v2_shot_aggregate` bracket, and the 21-line kernel wrapper at 363–383.
  1359	
  1360	Every operand is a literal. Note `v2_op_expand_t(st, v, **0u**, 0u, 1)`: the
  1361	third argument is `active_k`, which the specializer tracks statically as it
  1362	walks the program. The mechanism is visible in the emitter itself — every
  1363	rank-changing case both *prints* the current `*k` and *updates* it
  1364	(`v2_specializer.cc:42-56`):
  1365	
  1366	```cpp
  1367	    case Opcode::OP_EXPAND:
  1368	        o << "v2_op_expand(st, v, " << *k << "u);"; ++*k; break;
  1369	    case Opcode::OP_EXPAND_T:
  1370	        o << "v2_op_expand_t(st, v, " << *k << "u, " << a1 << "u, 0);"; ++*k; break;
  1371	    …
  1372	    case Opcode::OP_MEAS_ACTIVE_DIAGONAL:
  1373	        o << "v2_op_meas_active_diagonal(st, v, " << *k << "u, " << a1 << "u, "
  1374	          << a << "u, " << flags << "u);";
  1375	        if (*k) --*k; break;
  1376	```
  1377	
  1378	**The interpreter must load `st->active_k` after every rank-changing op; the
  1379	specializer knows it at emission time.** §7.3 measures what that alone is worth.
  1380	
  1381	Two details in that switch are worth noticing because they are free wins that
  1382	fall out of the "one call per instruction" shape:
  1383	
  1384	- `OP_FRAME_S` and `OP_FRAME_S_DAG` emit the *same* call
  1385	  (`v2_specializer.cc:35-36`); so do `OP_ARRAY_S`/`OP_ARRAY_S_DAG` and
  1386	  `OP_ARRAY_T`/`OP_ARRAY_T_DAG`, differing only in a literal `0`/`1` dagger flag.
  1387	  Six opcodes collapse to three call sites with a constant argument — which the
  1388	  optimizer then resolves to two distinct specializations of each body.
  1389	- `OP_DETECTOR` emits `v2_op_detector();` — a call taking *no arguments at all*.
  1390	  The detector's work is entirely compile-time in the specialized path.
  1391	- `OP_POSTSELECT` is the one case that emits control flow rather than a
  1392	  statement: `if (v2_op_postselect(...)) return;`. A shot that fails
  1393	  post-selection exits `spec_body` directly, with no flag to test at the next
  1394	  instruction.
  1395	
  1396	**The measured invariant.** `V2_performance/lowering/ir_density.csv`:
  1397	
  1398	| circuit | instrs | v2 C lines | **lines/instr** |
  1399	|---|---|---|---|
  1400	| reg_frame_h | 4 | 43 | 10.75 |
  1401	| reg_four_t | 11 | 49 | 4.45 |
  1402	| reg_circuit_d3 | 344 | 383 | **1.11** |
  1403	| coop_qv10 | 140 | 179 | **1.27** |
  1404	| coop_circuit_d5 | 1,720 | 1,760 | **1.02** |
  1405	| coop_surface_d7_t10 | 4,134 | 4,174 | **1.00** |
  1406	| glob_surface_d7_t19 | 4,296 | 4,346 | **1.01** |
  1407	
  1408	**One line of source per bytecode instruction, plus a fixed ~40-line kernel
  1409	wrapper.** The two register-tier outliers are entirely that wrapper amortized
  1410	over 4 and 11 instructions; by 344 it has vanished into the noise.
  1411	
  1412	Against V1's 55–2,407 lines/instr and Hybrid's 15–56, this is a different
  1413	*asymptotic class* of code generator, not a tuning improvement.
  1414	
  1415	### 6.4 The compile pipeline — no MLIR, no HIP
  1416	
  1417	The directory is named `mlir/v2/`. **That name is the only MLIR left in V2.**
  1418	`v2_specializer.cc:127` emits a C translation unit whose first include is
  1419	`"clifft/gpu/mlir/v2/v2_ops.h"`, and `v2_compile_cache.cc:151-176` compiles it
  1420	with the raw LLVM toolchain:
  1421	
  1422	```cpp
  1423	// 1) C -> amdgcn bitcode. Flags are IDENTICAL to the build-time interpreter
  1424	// pipeline (ClifftAmdgcn.cmake) so the shared v2_op_*() bodies compile to the
  1425	// same code -> byte-exact. -ffp-contract=off with no fast-math is what makes
  1426	// that hold regardless of inlining decisions: without reassociation or
  1427	// contraction the optimizer cannot change an FP result.
  1428	clang --target=amdgcn-amd-amdhsa -mcpu=gfx950 \
  1429	      -ffreestanding -nostdlib -nogpulib -std=c23 -O2 -ffp-contract=off \
  1430	      -emit-llvm -c -o spec.bc spec.c
  1431	
  1432	// 2) link ocml + oclc controls (identical set to ClifftAmdgcn.cmake)
  1433	llvm-link -o linked.bc spec.bc ocml.bc ockl.bc \
  1434	      oclc_wavefrontsize64_on.bc oclc_daz_opt_off.bc oclc_finite_only_off.bc \
  1435	      oclc_unsafe_math_off.bc oclc_correctly_rounded_sqrt_on.bc \
  1436	      oclc_abi_version_500.bc oclc_isa_version_950.bc
  1437	
  1438	opt -O2 -o linked.bc linked.bc                                    // 3
  1439	llc -mtriple=amdgcn-amd-amdhsa -mcpu=gfx950 \
  1440	    -mattr=+wavefrontsize64 -filetype=obj -O2 -o spec.o linked.bc  // 4
  1441	ld.lld -shared -o spec.hsaco spec.o                                // 5
  1442	```
  1443	
  1444	Three things about this deserve emphasis.
  1445	
  1446	**No HIP anywhere.** Not as a source language, not as a runtime, not as a
  1447	packaging format. Contrast Hybrid, which had to emit HIP, produce a Clang
  1448	Offload Bundle, then unbundle it to get an ELF that HSA would accept (§4.2). V2
  1449	targets `amdgcn-amd-amdhsa` directly and gets a plain ELF from `ld.lld`. §13
  1450	covers why this matters beyond aesthetics.
  1451	
  1452	**The flags are identical to the build-time interpreter's**, deliberately and
  1453	with a comment that reads as an instruction to future maintainers
  1454	(`v2_compile_cache.cc:155-156`):
  1455	
  1456	> `Do NOT add vectorize/unroll/fast-math flags that would diverge from the interpreter.`
  1457	
  1458	`-ffp-contract=off`, no fast-math: without reassociation or FMA contraction,
  1459	**the optimizer cannot legally change a floating-point result**, so the
  1460	specialized kernel and the interpreter agree bit for bit no matter what inlining
  1461	decisions differ between them. Hybrid did the opposite — `kernel_cache.cc:115`
  1462	compiles its HIP with `-O2 -ffast-math`. V2 cannot, and the reason is §2.4: a
  1463	1-ULP difference flips a sampling branch and desynchronizes the PRNG forever.
  1464	Hybrid got away with it because Hybrid was never byte-compared against the CPU
  1465	reference.
  1466	
  1467	Note also `-mattr=+wavefrontsize64` at step 4 and `oclc_wavefrontsize64_on.bc`
  1468	at step 2. gfx950 supports wave32; V2 pins wave64 in both the device-library
  1469	control set and the backend because the coop tier's `V2_STRIDE 256u` and its
  1470	`V2_RED_WARPS` reduction geometry are written for 64-lane waves.
  1471	
  1472	**Compilation is cached and never on the sampling path**, and the cache key is
  1473	the part that has already gone wrong once. `v2_compile_cache.cc:125-127`:
  1474	
  1475	```cpp
  1476	std::string ident = csrc + "|" + llvm_bin("clang") + "|" + arch() + "|" +
  1477	                    bitcode_dir() + "|" + device_header_ident();
  1478	size_t h = std::hash<std::string>{}(ident);
  1479	```
  1480	
  1481	That last term is a repair. The key originally hashed only `csrc` — the
  1482	*generated* C — which is barely half the translation unit: every `v2_op_*` body,
  1483	`v2_barrier()`, and every tunable constant lives in the included headers. A
  1484	header fix therefore produced the same key, and the stale `.hsaco` **plus its
  1485	stale `.gate` verdict** silently won. §11.4 documents the benchmark run this
  1486	destroyed. The fix hashes the contents of all three device headers
  1487	(`v2_ops.h`, `v2_ops_body.inc`, `device_abi.h`) into the key — content, not
  1488	mtime, since mtimes change on every checkout and would defeat the cache for no
  1489	reason.
  1490	
  1491	### 6.5 The correctness gate
  1492	
  1493	Specialization is a compiler, and compilers have bugs. V2 does not ask you to
  1494	trust it: `v2_kernel.cc:370-398` validates each freshly compiled `.hsaco`
  1495	against the interpreter on a shot sample, and **persists the verdict to disk**:
  1496	
  1497	```cpp
  1498	// The verdict is written to "<spath>.gate" so it is computed ONCE ever (not
  1499	// per process) and NEVER re-run during a profiled sample dispatch -- the
  1500	// gate's own validation dispatches would otherwise pollute rocprofv3 traces.

