  1582	### 6.6 The specializer is opt-in
  1583	
  1584	`getenv("V2_SPECIALIZE")` (`v2_kernel.cc:358`). Unset, V2 runs its bytecode
  1585	interpreter.
  1586	
  1587	This is a real hazard when reading any V2 benchmark. **With `V2_SPECIALIZE`
  1588	unset, a benchmark measures V2's interpreter against SVM's tuned interpreter and
  1589	reports what looks like a 2–3× regression.** Every number in this report was
  1590	produced with it set; §11.4 shows what happens when a stale gate verdict un-sets
  1591	it for you.
  1592	
  1593	### 6.7 The three tier wrappers
  1594	
  1595	One `spec_body`, three wrappers (`v2_specializer.cc:172-225`). `spec_body` takes
  1596	21 parameters; the first five are the tier-dependent ones (state, amplitudes,
  1597	scratch, capacity, shot id) and the remaining 16 are identical across tiers —
  1598	emitted once into a `const char* fwd` at `v2_specializer.cc:166-170` and reused
  1599	verbatim by each branch, so the tier choice cannot accidentally change what the
  1600	body receives.
  1601	
  1602	Every wrapper opens with the same line:
  1603	
  1604	```c
  1605	    (void)peak_rank; (void)num_instrs; (void)instrs; (void)total_meas_slots;
  1606	```
  1607	
  1608	Those four kernel arguments are what the *interpreter* runs on: the bytecode
  1609	pointer, its length, the rank, and the measurement-slot count. The specialized
  1610	kernel takes them because the kernarg layout must stay identical (one dispatch
  1611	path, two kernels), and then **discards all four**. That single line is the
  1612	specialization thesis in the ABI: the program is no longer an input.
  1613	
  1614	**Register** — one shot per thread, statevector in VGPRs:
  1615	
  1616	```c
  1617	#define V2_REG_MAX_AMP 16
  1618	__attribute__((amdgpu_kernel, visibility("default")))
  1619	void clifft_v2_spec(...) {
  1620	    u64 shot_id = shot_offset + ((u64)v2_bid() * 256ul + __builtin_amdgcn_workitem_id_x());
  1621	    if (shot_id >= shots) return;
  1622	    V2State st; CV2Complex vloc[V2_REG_MAX_AMP]; CV2Complex sloc[V2_REG_MAX_AMP/2];
  1623	    spec_body(&st, vloc, sloc, V2_REG_MAX_AMP, shot_id, ...);
  1624	}
  1625	```
  1626	
  1627	**Coop** — one workgroup per shot, everything in LDS:
  1628	
  1629	```c
  1630	extern __attribute__((address_space(3))) CV2Complex lds_v[V2_MAX_AMP];
  1631	extern __attribute__((address_space(3))) CV2Complex lds_red_scratch[V2_SCRATCH_AMP];
  1632	extern __attribute__((address_space(3))) V2State lds_state;
  1633	void clifft_v2_spec(...) {
  1634	    u64 shot_id = shot_offset + (u64)v2_bid();
  1635	    if (shot_id >= shots) return;
  1636	    spec_body((V2State*)&lds_state, (CV2Complex*)lds_v, (CV2Complex*)lds_red_scratch, ...);
  1637	}
  1638	```
  1639	
  1640	**Global** — a persistent pool draining shots from an atomic counter, with the
  1641	amplitude capacity folded to a literal:
  1642	
  1643	```c
  1644	void clifft_v2_spec(..., CV2Complex* global_v, CV2Complex* global_scratch, u64* work_counter) {
  1645	    u32 t = v2_tid(), slot = v2_bid();
  1646	    const u64 amp_capacity = 8192ull;                    // <- 1<<peak_rank, a constant
  1647	    CV2Complex* v       = global_v       + (u64)slot * amp_capacity;
  1648	    CV2Complex* scratch = global_scratch + (u64)slot * (amp_capacity >> 1);
  1649	    for (;;) {
  1650	        if (t == 0) lds_shot = __atomic_fetch_add(&work_counter[0], 1UL, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
  1651	        v2_barrier();
  1652	        u64 batch_shot = lds_shot;
  1653	        if (batch_shot >= shots) return;
  1654	        spec_body((V2State*)&lds_state, v, scratch, (u32)amp_capacity, shot_offset + batch_shot, ...);
  1655	        v2_barrier();
  1656	    }
  1657	}
  1658	```
  1659	
  1660	<figure>
  1661	<img src="diagrams/persistent-kernel.svg" alt="Persistent kernel with work-stealing" width="100%">
  1662	<figcaption><b>Figure 6.2</b> — The global tier's persistent kernel. A fixed
  1663	pool of resident workgroups, each owning an HBM amplitude slice, drains shots
  1664	from an atomic counter. The grid does not scale with shot count.</figcaption>
  1665	</figure>
  1666	
  1667	The resident pool is sized from an **HBM budget** rather than from the rank cap
  1668	(`v2_kernel.cc:436-445`):
  1669	
  1670	```cpp
  1671	const uint64_t amp = 1ull << flat.peak_rank;
  1672	const uint64_t bytes_per_wg = amp * sizeof(GpuComplex) + (amp / 2) * sizeof(GpuComplex);
  1673	const uint64_t budget = 32ull << 30;  // 32 GB
  1674	uint64_t wgs = budget / bytes_per_wg;
  1675	if (wgs < 1) wgs = 1;               // rank 26+: at least one resident wg
  1676	if (wgs > 2048) wgs = 2048;
  1677	global_grid_wgs = static_cast<uint32_t>(wgs);
  1678	// Prefer a multiple of the XCD count when the budget allows it, so the
  1679	// resident pool spreads evenly; never inflate past the budget.
  1680	if (global_grid_wgs > kNumXCDs) global_grid_wgs -= global_grid_wgs % kNumXCDs;
  1681	```
  1682	
  1683	`GpuComplex` is `{float re; float im;}` — 8 bytes (`gpu_types.h:38-41`) — so
  1684	`bytes_per_wg` is a slice plus a half-size scratch, i.e. **12 bytes per
  1685	amplitude**. Three clauses, each doing distinct work: the budget divide, a floor
  1686	of 1 so rank 26 is *possible* rather than *fast*, a ceiling of 2,048, and a
  1687	round-down to a multiple of `kNumXCDs = 8` so the resident pool spreads evenly
  1688	across the device's eight compute dies.
  1689	
  1690	Evaluating that arithmetic gives the resident pool at every global-tier rank:
  1691	
  1692	| peak rank | MiB / workgroup | resident workgroups | regime |
  1693	|---|---|---|---|
  1694	| 11–19 | 0.02 – 6.00 | **2048** | ceiling-limited |
  1695	| 20 | 12.00 | **2048** | ceiling-limited (last one) |
  1696	| 21 | 24.00 | **1360** | budget-limited |
  1697	| 22 | 48.00 | **680** | budget-limited |
  1698	| 23 | 96.00 | **336** | budget-limited |
  1699	| 24 | 192.00 | **168** | budget-limited |
  1700	| 25 | 384.00 | 80 | budget-limited |
  1701	| 26 | 768.00 | 40 | budget-limited |
  1702	
  1703	**Rank 20 is the knee.** Below it the pool is flat at the 2,048 ceiling and rank
  1704	costs nothing in occupancy; from 21 up, every added qubit halves the resident
  1705	pool. §10 shows this table's five measurable rows (20–24) reproducing exactly in
  1706	the observed `Grid_Size_X ÷ 256`, and §15 shows the performance decay that
  1707	follows from it — a geometric loss that is *by design*, not a bug, and is why
  1708	§10 covers what made rank > 19 possible at all rather than what made it fast.
  1709	
