  1711	
  1712	## 7. What V2 specializes — eight classes, eight experiments
  1713	
  1714	Chapter 6 described the mechanism: the specializer emits one `v2_op_*()` call
  1715	per bytecode instruction, with the operands and the statically-tracked
  1716	`active_k` written out as C literals. This chapter answers the obvious follow-up
  1717	question — *what does that actually buy, per opcode family?* — and it answers it
  1718	with measurements rather than with reasoning about what the compiler ought to
  1719	do.
  1720	
  1721	### 7.1 The experiment
  1722	
  1723	Eight cases, one per specialization class. Each case is a single `.c` file that
  1724	compiles **twice from the same source**, calling the **identical** `v2_op_*`
  1725	body out of `v2_ops.h`:
  1726	
  1727	| Build | Meaning |
  1728	|---|---|
  1729	| `-DSPEC_FORM=0` | **interpreter form** — operands arrive in a `CV2Instr` loaded from device memory at a runtime `pc`, exactly as `coop_interpreter.c`'s `for(pc) switch` supplies them |
  1730	| `-DSPEC_FORM=1` | **specialized form** — operands are the literal constants `v2_specializer.cc` emits for that instruction |
  1731	
  1732	The two forms differ in **exactly one variable**: whether the operands were
  1733	compile-time constants. Everything else — the op body, the tier macro, the
  1734	compiler, the flags — is held fixed. The flags are the production flags copied
  1735	out of `v2_compile_cache.cc`:
  1736	
  1737	```
  1738	clang --target=amdgcn-amd-amdhsa -mcpu=gfx950 -ffreestanding -nostdlib -nogpulib \
  1739	      -std=c23 -O2 -ffp-contract=off -I<root>/src -S
  1740	```
  1741	
  1742	One methodology point deserves quoting, because getting it wrong would have
  1743	inflated every number in this chapter. From `spec_examples/harness.h`:
  1744	
  1745	> The interpreter form reads its operands out of `instrs`, a plain
  1746	> `const CV2Instr*` kernel argument indexed by a runtime `pc`, which is exactly
  1747	> how `coop_interpreter.c`'s `for(pc)switch` supplies them. **NOT volatile:**
  1748	> marking it volatile would force system-coherent (`sc0 sc1`) reads the real
  1749	> interpreter never issues and would overstate the interpreter's cost. The
  1750	> operands are unknowable at compile time simply because `instrs` points at
  1751	> device memory.
  1752	
  1753	So the interpreter form is not a strawman. It is given every optimization the
  1754	real interpreter gets; the only thing withheld is knowledge of the operand
  1755	values. The harness kernel signature is the same in both builds:
  1756	
  1757	```c
  1758	#define CASE_KERNEL_HEAD                                                    \
  1759	    __attribute__((visibility("default"))) __attribute__((amdgpu_kernel))   \
  1760	    void case_kernel(V2State* st, CV2Complex* v, CV2Complex* scratch,       \
  1761	                     const CV2Instr* instrs, u32 pc,                        \
  1762	                     const CV2FusedU2Entry* fused_u2, ...)
  1763	
  1764	#define IN(n) (instrs[pc + (n)])   // fetch instruction pc+n, the interpreter's access pattern
  1765	```
  1766	
  1767	Metrics are scraped from the emitted assembly by `build_examples.sh`:
  1768	instruction count, `num_vgpr`/`numbered_sgpr`/`private_seg_size` from the
  1769	AMDGPU `.set` directives, and counts of `s_load`, `ds_read`/`ds_write`, `v_*`,
  1770	and `s_cbranch`/`s_branch`. Raw output is in
  1771	`V2_performance/lowering/spec_examples/stats.csv`; the `.s` files for both forms
  1772	of all eight cases are checked in alongside it, and `build_examples.sh`
  1773	reproduces all sixteen byte-for-byte (`ARCH` defaults to `gfx950`; the
  1774	register-tier cases additionally get `-DV2_REGISTER=1`, which is what selects
  1775	`V2_STRIDE 1` over `V2_STRIDE 256`).
  1776	
