  1846	Four things fall out of this table immediately, and they set up the rest of the
  1847	report:
  1848	
  1849	1. **The VALU ratios still exceed the instruction ratios, but the gap is now
  1850	   honest.** S1 deletes 82 % of its vector ALU work and 65 % of its
  1851	   instructions.
  1852	
  1853	   An earlier draft of this chapter claimed the deleted VALU work was *replaced
  1854	   by scalar work* — "the same computation moved from the vector pipe to the
  1855	   scalar pipe" — and pointed at S2's SGPR column as the tell. **That claim is
  1856	   wrong, and the data in this very table refutes it.** Counting scalar
  1857	   instructions the same way the table counts vector ones:
  1858	
  1859	   | Case | SALU interp→spec | ratio | VALU interp→spec | ratio |
  1860	   |---|---|---|---|---|
  1861	   | S1 | 74→35 | 2.11× | 44→8 | 5.50× |
  1862	   | S2 | 93→45 | 2.07× | 86→19 | 4.53× |
  1863	   | S3 | 84→48 | 1.75× | 67→27 | 2.48× |
  1864	   | S4 | 133→93 | 1.43× | 139→96 | 1.45× |
  1865	   | S5 | 102→33 | **3.09×** | 84→33 | 2.55× |
  1866	   | S6 | 83→36 | 2.31× | 54→42 | 1.29× |
  1867	   | S7 | 177→141 | 1.26× | 214→210 | 1.02× |
  1868	   | S8 | 87→49 | 1.78× | 71→63 | 1.13× |
  1869	
  1870	   **Scalar instructions fall in all eight cases**, and in S5, S6 and S8 they
  1871	   fall *faster* than vector instructions. Nothing moved to the scalar pipe;
  1872	   scalar work was deleted too, and on the array ops it was deleted harder.
  1873	   §14.3 confirms this at corpus scale — the SALU count falls faster than the
  1874	   VALU count on **26 of 26** circuits, and the *absolute* number of scalar
  1875	   instructions removed exceeds the vector count removed by ~3.8× on the surface
  1876	   family. The mechanism is deletion, not substitution.
  1877	
  1878	   What survives of the original observation is narrower and still true: the
  1879	   *register* column can go up where the *instruction* column goes down (S2's
  1880	   SGPR 13→26), because straight-line scalar code holding many folded literals
  1881	   needs somewhere to hold them. That is a register-pressure effect, not a
  1882	   work-migration effect.
  1883	2. **The spread is enormous — 5.50× down to 1.02× on VALU.** Specialization is
  1884	   not a uniform multiplier. It is worth a great deal on frame/flag/index ops
  1885	   and almost nothing on data-dependent ops. Which opcodes a circuit is made of
  1886	   therefore predicts its speedup, and §14 confirms that it does.
  1887	3. **S7 is the negative result, and it is in the table on purpose.** 1.10×
  1888	   instructions, 1.02× VALU, and — now that the column is read correctly —
  1889	   **VGPR 56→56, unchanged**. The noise block is genuinely data-dependent; there
  1890	   is nothing to fold, and specialization does not even relieve register
  1891	   pressure. This is the case that predicts the `circuit_d5` regression in §11.1,
  1892	   and the corrected register number strengthens that prediction rather than
  1893	   weakening it.
  1894	4. **S2 is the one case where SGPR pressure goes *up*, 13→26** — while its
  1895	   scalar *instruction* count falls 93→45 and its branch count falls to zero.
  1896	   Fewer scalar instructions, more scalar registers: the eighteen deleted
  1897	   branches were replaced by `s_cselect`s over folded literals, and those
  1898	   literals have to live somewhere. On the register tier this is a good trade —
  1899	   SGPRs are not the occupancy limiter there — but it is worth flagging as the
  1900	   one place specialization *costs* something measurable, and as the reason the
  1901	   register columns and the instruction columns must be read separately.
  1902	
