  1917	
  1918	### 7.3 S1 — frame operand folding
  1919	
  1920	**The op.** `OP_FRAME_CNOT` touches only the Pauli frame. Two bit reads, two bit
  1921	XORs, at word/bit offsets derived from the axes (`v2_ops_body.inc:12-19`):
  1922	
  1923	```c
  1924	static inline __attribute__((always_inline)) void v2_op_frame_cnot(V2State* st, u32 ctrl, u32 tgt) {
  1925	    u32 t = v2_tid();
  1926	    if (IS_OWNER) {
  1927	        int px_c = fget(st->px, ctrl), pz_tt = fget(st->pz, tgt);
  1928	        fxor(st->px, tgt, px_c); fxor(st->pz, ctrl, pz_tt);
  1929	    }
  1930	    v2_barrier();
  1931	}
  1932	```
  1933	
  1934	`fget`/`fxor` are `(w[a >> 6] >> (a & 63)) & 1` style bit accessors. With a
  1935	**constant** axis, `a >> 6` and `a & 63` are compile-time; the word index
  1936	becomes a fixed offset and the bit becomes a fixed mask. With a **runtime** axis
  1937	each access costs a shift/mask chain *plus a dynamic index* into `st->px` /
  1938	`st->pz`.
  1939	
  1940	**The two forms** (`cases/S1_frame_cnot.c`):
  1941	
  1942	```c
  1943	#if SPEC_FORM
  1944	    v2_op_frame_cnot(st, 3u, 10u);
  1945	    v2_op_frame_cnot(st, 3u, 12u);
  1946	    v2_op_frame_cz(st, 3u, 6u);
  1947	#else
  1948	    CV2Instr i0 = IN(0), i1 = IN(1), i2 = IN(2);
  1949	    v2_op_frame_cnot(st, i0.axis_1, i0.axis_2);
  1950	    v2_op_frame_cnot(st, i1.axis_1, i1.axis_2);
  1951	    v2_op_frame_cz(st, i2.axis_1, i2.axis_2);
  1952	#endif
  1953	```
  1954	
  1955	**What the assembly shows.** This is the single clearest diff in the whole
  1956	study, so it is worth reading both sides. The specialized form, in full:
  1957	
  1958	```asm
  1959	case_kernel:
  1960		s_load_dwordx2 s[0:1], s[0:1], 0x0
  1961		s_mov_b32   s7, 0
  1962		s_waitcnt   lgkmcnt(0)
  1963		s_load_dwordx2 s[4:5], s[0:1], 0x0
  1964		s_load_dwordx2 s[2:3], s[0:1], 0x28
  1965		s_waitcnt   lgkmcnt(0)
  1966		s_and_b32   s6, s4, 8               ; fget(px, 3)  -> a constant mask
  1967		s_cmp_eq_u64 s[6:7], 0
  1968		s_cbranch_scc1 .LBB0_2
  1969		s_xor_b32   s4, s4, 0x400           ; fxor(px, 10) -> a constant mask
  1970		v_mov_b32_e32 v2, 0
  1971		v_mov_b64_e32 v[0:1], s[4:5]
  1972		global_store_dwordx2 v2, v[0:1], s[0:1]
  1973		s_and_b32   s6, s2, 0x400
  1974		...
  1975	```
  1976	
  1977	Every frame access is `s_and_b32` / `s_xor_b32` against a **literal mask** on
  1978	the **scalar** unit. The full set of masks in the specialized kernel is `8`,
  1979	`0x400`, `0x1000` and `64` — respectively bit 3 (`ctrl=3`), bit 10 and bit 12
  1980	(the two CNOT targets) and bit 6 (the CZ target), each appearing exactly where
  1981	the source says it should.
  1982	
  1983	Three CNOT/CZ ops compile to **8 VALU instructions total, and all 8 are `v_mov`**
  1984	— four `v_mov_b32_e32 v2, 0` supplying the zero address offset and four
  1985	`v_mov_b64_e32 v[0:1], s[..]` staging a scalar result into a vector register for
  1986	`global_store`. Not one arithmetic vector instruction survives. The frame logic
  1987	itself has left the vector pipe entirely; what remains is purely the
  1988	scalar→vector staging the store instruction's encoding requires.
  1989	
  1990	The interpreter form for the identical three ops. The instruction fetch
  1991	(`interp/S1_frame_cnot.s:9-18`):
  1992	
  1993	```asm
  1994		s_load_dword s6, s[0:1], 0x20       ; pc
  1995		s_load_dwordx2 s[2:3], s[0:1], 0x18 ; instrs
  1996		v_mov_b32_e32 v3, 0
  1997		s_load_dwordx2 s[0:1], s[0:1], 0x0
  1998		s_waitcnt lgkmcnt(0)
  1999		s_mul_i32 s7, s6, 40                ; pc * sizeof(CV2Instr) -- address math
  2000		s_mul_hi_u32 s5, s6, 40

