  2001		s_add_u32 s4, s2, s7
  2002		s_addc_u32 s5, s3, s5
  2003		global_load_dword v0, v3, s[4:5] offset:2   ; load the instruction
  2004	```
  2005	
  2006	The `v_readfirstlane_b32 s9, v0` that moves the loaded operand to the scalar
  2007	unit follows five lines later, at `:23`, after an `s_waitcnt vmcnt(0)` that
  2008	stalls on the load. Then, for each frame access (`:34-46`, with three
  2009	interleaved scalar instructions for the *second* frame word elided):
  2010	
  2011	```asm
  2012		v_lshrrev_b32_e32 v2, 3, v0         ; a >> 6 -> word index, DYNAMIC
  2013		v_and_b32_e32 v2, 0x1ff8, v2
  2014		v_readfirstlane_b32 s2, v2
  2015		s_load_dwordx2 s[6:7], s[0:1], s2 offset:0x0  ; dynamically-indexed frame word
  2016		v_lshlrev_b64 v[0:1], v0, 1         ; 1 << (a & 63) -> bit mask, DYNAMIC
  2017		s_waitcnt lgkmcnt(0)
  2018		v_and_b32_e32 v4, s6, v0
  2019		v_and_b32_e32 v5, s7, v1
  2020		v_cmp_eq_u64_e32 vcc, 0, v[4:5]
  2021	```
  2022	
  2023	Note the two `s_waitcnt`s. They are not incidental: the interpreter's operand
  2024	arrives from memory, so the dependent address math cannot issue until the load
  2025	retires, and the frame word it then addresses is itself a second dependent load.
  2026	The specialized form has no such chain — its masks are immediates in the
  2027	instruction encoding. It contains **2** `s_waitcnt`s against the interpreter
  2028	form's **15**, and both of the two guard the unavoidable two-step
  2029	kernel-argument load (kernarg → `st`, then `st` → the frame words).
  2030	
  2031	Three separate costs are visible and all three disappear under specialization:
  2032	
  2033	1. **Instruction fetch** — `s_mul_i32 s7, s6, 40` plus a `global_load_dword`,
  2034	   per instruction. The bytecode itself is a memory operand. (`40` is
  2035	   `sizeof(CV2Instr)`, which `device_abi_checks.cc:58` pins equal to
  2036	   `sizeof(GpuInstr)`; the multiply is `pc * sizeof(instr)` with a runtime `pc`,
  2037	   so it needs a 64-bit product — hence the `s_mul_i32`/`s_mul_hi_u32` pair and
  2038	   the `s_add_u32`/`s_addc_u32` that follow.)
  2039	2. **`v_readfirstlane_b32`** — the operand arrives in a *vector* register
  2040	   (it came from a `global_load` whose address is uniform but whose result the
  2041	   compiler must materialize in a VGPR), so it must be moved to the scalar unit
  2042	   before it can be used as an address. That is a cross-pipe transfer per
  2043	   operand.
  2044	3. **Dynamic bit math on the vector pipe** — `v_lshrrev_b32` / `v_and_b32` /
  2045	   `v_lshlrev_b64` to compute the word index and the bit mask. Under
  2046	   specialization these are *literals in the instruction encoding*.
  2047	
  2048	The 44 → 8 VALU drop (5.50×) is the sum of (2) and (3), and the whole kernel
  2049	falls 136 → 47 instructions (2.89×) — the largest instruction-level gain in the
  2050	study. Note also VGPR 8 → 3: with the operands and masks resolved, the op needs
  2051	almost no vector registers, which directly raises tier occupancy (§9).
  2052	
  2053	The scalar *registers* barely move, 14 → 12, because the same frame words are
  2054	still being read and XORed — they are simply addressed by literal masks now
  2055	instead of by computed ones. But the scalar *instruction count* falls
  2056	substantially, **74 → 35 (2.11×)**: the address arithmetic in (1), the
  2057	`v_readfirstlane` destinations in (2), and the mask construction in (3) were
  2058	scalar work as much as vector work, and specialization removes both.
  2059	
  2060	> **This is the mechanism behind the SALU finding in §14.3, and it is a deletion
  2061	> mechanism.** V2's speedup is not "the same computation moved from the vector
  2062	> pipe to the scalar pipe" — an earlier draft said that, and §7.2's table
  2063	> refutes it. It is that *both* pipes issue less: the interpreter's operand
  2064	> fetch, dispatch and address arithmetic are pure overhead with respect to the
  2065	> simulation, and constants delete them outright. §14.3 shows the effect is
  2066	> larger on the scalar side, by an absolute margin of roughly 3.8× on the
  2067	> surface family.
  2068	
  2069	---
  2070	
  2071	### 7.4 S2 — flag folding on dormant measurements
  2072	
  2073	**The op.** `OP_MEAS_DORMANT_STATIC` writes a measurement slot without touching
  2074	the amplitude array. `flags` selects between a constant-outcome path
  2075	(`FLAG_IDENTITY`) and a frame-read path, and supplies the sign XOR
  2076	(`v2_ops_body.inc:53-65`):
  2077	
  2078	```c
  2079	static inline __attribute__((always_inline)) void v2_op_meas_dormant_static(V2State* st, u32 axis, u32 slot, u8 flags) {
  2080	    u32 t = v2_tid();
  2081	    if (IS_OWNER) {
  2082	        u8 mval;
  2083	        if (flags & FLAG_IDENTITY) mval = (flags & FLAG_SIGN) ? 1u : 0u;
  2084	        else {
  2085	            u8 outcome = (u8)fget(st->px, axis);
  2086	            mval = outcome ^ (u8)((flags & FLAG_SIGN) != 0);
  2087	        }
  2088	        if (slot < V2_MAX_MEAS) mset(st->meas, slot, mval);
  2089	    }
  2090	    v2_barrier();
  2091	}
  2092	```
  2093	
  2094	**The two forms** (`cases/S2_meas_dormant.c`):
  2095	
  2096	```c
  2097	#if SPEC_FORM
  2098	    v2_op_meas_dormant_static(st, 6u, 33u, 0u);
  2099	    v2_op_meas_dormant_static(st, 8u, 32u, 1u);
  2100	    v2_op_meas_dormant_random(st, 13u, 30u, 0u);
  2101	#else
  2102	    CV2Instr i0 = IN(0), i1 = IN(1), i2 = IN(2);
  2103	    v2_op_meas_dormant_static(st, i0.axis_1, i0.a, i0.flags);
  2104	    ...
  2105	#endif
  2106	```
  2107	
  2108	**Result: 18 branches → 0.** This is the cleanest positive in the study. A
  2109	constant `flags` deletes one of the two paths *outright* — not predicts it,
  2110	deletes it — along with the test that chose between them. A constant `slot`
  2111	deletes the `slot < V2_MAX_MEAS` bounds check. And `mset(st->meas, slot, ...)`
  2112	on a bit-packed array becomes a fixed word and a fixed mask, exactly as in S1.
  2113	
  2114	The specialized assembly contains **no `s_cbranch` or `s_branch` at all** —
  2115	not merely none in the measurement logic; a `grep` over the whole file returns
  2116	zero, against 18 in the interpreter form. What remains is a straight scalar
  2117	sequence. Even the `rng_uniform` compare inside the `_random` variant, which
  2118	is genuinely data-dependent, is if-converted (`spec/S2_meas_dormant.s:49-65`,
  2119	with the intervening stores and `v_mov` staging elided):
  2120	
  2121	```asm
  2122		v_cvt_f64_u32_e32 v[8:9], s11       ; xoshiro output hi -> double
  2123		v_cvt_f64_u32_e32 v[10:11], s10     ; ... and lo
  2124		v_ldexp_f64  v[0:1], v[8:9], 32     ; hi * 2^32
  2125		v_add_f64    v[0:1], v[0:1], v[10:11]
  2126		v_ldexp_f64  v[0:1], v[0:1], s18    ; * 2^-53
  2127		v_cmp_gt_f64_e32 vcc, 0.5, v[0:1]   ; rng_uniform(st->rng) < 0.5
  2128		s_and_b64    s[2:3], vcc, exec
  2129		s_cselect_b32 s18, 0, 0x2000        ; ... resolved with a SELECT, not a branch
  2130	```
  2131	
  2132	Note the `s_cselect_b32`: even the genuinely random outcome is if-converted,
  2133	because with constant `axis`/`slot` both arms write the same fixed bit and the
  2134	compiler can pick the *value* rather than the *path*. The neighbouring
  2135	`s_bitset0_b32 s12, 13` is the same fold in its purest form — `mset` of a
  2136	constant slot on a bit-packed array is one instruction with the bit number
  2137	in the encoding.
  2138	
  2139	VALU falls 86 → 19 (4.53×) and the kernel as a whole 206 → 70 instructions.
  2140	The **2.94× is the largest instruction ratio of the eight**, though S1's 5.50×
  2141	remains the largest VALU ratio.
  2142	
  2143	This is also the case where **SGPR usage doubles, 13 → 26** — the only such case
  2144	in the study, and the one place in this chapter where specialization measurably
  2145	*costs* something. The eighteen deleted branches were replaced by `s_cselect`s
  2146	over folded literals, and those literals need scalar registers to live in.
  2147	
  2148	It is worth being precise about what this does and does not show, because an
  2149	earlier draft read it as evidence that specialization *moves* work from the
  2150	vector pipe to the scalar pipe. It does not: S2's scalar instruction count falls
  2151	93 → 45 at the same time as its scalar register count rises 13 → 26. Fewer
  2152	scalar instructions, more scalar registers. The register pressure is the price
  2153	of straight-lining, not the destination of migrated work — and §14.3 shows the
  2154	scalar instruction count falling faster than the vector one on every circuit in
  2155	the corpus.
  2156	
  2157	Why this matters more on a GPU than the raw count suggests: on AMDGCN a taken
  2158	`s_cbranch` inside a divergent region forces `s_and_saveexec_b64` /
  2159	`s_or_b64 exec` mask manipulation and serializes the two sides. Eighteen of
  2160	those in a three-instruction sequence is pure interpretive overhead — and
  2161	dormant measurements are extremely common in surface-code circuits, which is
  2162	why the surface family shows some of the largest gains in §14.
  2163	
  2164	---
  2165	
  2166	### 7.5 S3 — static rank tracking (the load-bearing one)
  2167	
  2168	The case file's own header comment states the claim:
  2169	
  2170	> S3 — **STATIC RANK TRACKING.** This is the load-bearing specialization: the
  2171	> specializer knows `active_k` at every program point, so `half = 1u << active_k`
  2172	> becomes a literal and the strided sweep gets a compile-time trip count. The
  2173	> interpreter must re-read `st->active_k` after every rank-changing op.
  2174	> **Note what is NOT happening: the sweep is still a LOOP. V1 unrolled it.**
  2175	
  2176	That last sentence is the entire difference between V1 and V2 in one line, and
  2177	§5 already showed what unrolling cost.
  2178	
  2179	**How the specializer knows.** It maintains `k` as it walks the program
  2180	(`v2_specializer.cc:26-56`) — `++*k` on the expansion family, `--*k` on
  2181	rank-reducing measurements:
  2182	
  2183	```cpp
  2184	case Opcode::OP_EXPAND:
  2185	    o << "v2_op_expand(st, v, " << *k << "u);"; ++*k; break;
  2186	case Opcode::OP_EXPAND_T:
  2187	    o << "v2_op_expand_t(st, v, " << *k << "u, " << a1 << "u, 0);"; ++*k; break;
  2188	case Opcode::OP_MEAS_ACTIVE_INTERFERE:
  2189	    o << "v2_op_meas_active_interfere(st, v, " << *k << "u, " << a1 << "u, "
  2190	      << a << "u, " << flags << "u);";
  2191	    if (*k) --*k; break;
  2192	```
  2193	
  2194	Compare the interpreter, which has to re-read it every iteration
  2195	(`coop_interpreter.c:50-53`):
  2196	
  2197	```c
  2198	for (u32 pc = 0; pc < num_instrs; ++pc) {
  2199	    CV2Instr ins = instrs[pc];
  2200	    u32 k = st->active_k;          // <- a LOAD, every instruction
  2201	    switch (ins.opcode) {
  2202	```
  2203	
  2204	**The op** (`v2_ops_body.inc:85-96`):
  2205	
  2206	```c
  2207	static inline __attribute__((always_inline)) void v2_op_expand_t(V2State* st, CV2Complex* v, u32 active_k, u32 axis, int dagger) {
  2208	    u32 t = v2_tid();
  2209	    u32 half = 1u << active_k;
  2210	    int px = fget(st->px, axis);
  2211	    double imag = dagger ? -V2_INV_SQRT2 : V2_INV_SQRT2;
  2212	    if (px) imag = -imag;
  2213	    CV2Complex phase; phase.re = (float)V2_INV_SQRT2; phase.im = (float)imag;
  2214	    for (u32 i = t; i < half; i += V2_STRIDE) v[i + half] = cmul(v[i], phase);
  2215	    v2_barrier();
  2216	    if (IS_OWNER) st->active_k = active_k + 1;
  2217	    v2_barrier();
  2218	}
  2219	```
  2220	
  2221	**The two forms** (`cases/S3_expand_rank.c`) — note that only the *third*
  2222	argument changes:
  2223	
  2224	```c
  2225	#if SPEC_FORM
  2226	    v2_op_expand_t(st, v, 0u, 0u, 1);
  2227	    v2_op_expand_t(st, v, 1u, 3u, 0);
  2228	    v2_op_expand(st, v, 2u);
  2229	#else
  2230	    v2_op_expand_t(st, v, st->active_k, i0.axis_1, 1);
  2231	    v2_op_expand_t(st, v, st->active_k, i1.axis_1, 0);
  2232	    v2_op_expand(st, v, st->active_k);
  2233	#endif
  2234	```
  2235	
  2236	Because the specializer tracks the rank, the second call gets `1u` and the third
  2237	gets `2u` — the compiler sees the rank *grow*, statically, across the program.
  2238	
  2239	**What it buys:** 164→83 instructions (1.98×), VALU 67→27 (2.48×), VGPR 18→8,
  2240	SGPR 16→10. The `1u << active_k` becomes a literal bound, so the loop's trip
  2241	count is known; `dagger` being a literal folds the `imag` sign at compile time
  2242	(the `?:` and the negate both disappear); and the store address `v[i + half]`
  2243	gets a constant displacement instead of a computed one. The `s_load` count drops
  2244	6→4, and the specific load that disappears is visible in the artifacts: the
  2245	interpreter form contains exactly one `s_load_dword ..., 0x278` — the
  2246	`st->active_k` read, `active_k`'s offset in `V2State` — and the specialized form
  2247	contains **none**. The full accounting is worth reading, because the count does
  2248	not fall by simple deletion:
  2249	
  2250	| | interpreter | specialized |
  2251	|---|---|---|
  2252	| `0x20` — `pc` | ✓ | — |
  2253	| `0x18` — `instrs` | ✓ | — |
  2254	| `0x0` — kernarg block | ✓ | ✓ |
  2255	| `0x278` — `st->active_k` | ✓ | — |
  2256	| dynamically-indexed frame word (`s10`, `s0`) | ✓ ✓ | — |
  2257	| `st->px` / phase operands at fixed offsets | — | ✓ ✓ ✓ |
  2258	
  2259	Three interpreter-only loads (`pc`, `instrs`, `active_k`) and both
  2260	*dynamically-indexed* frame reads disappear; three *statically*-addressed loads
  2261	appear in their place. The net is 6→4, but the character of what remains is the
  2262	point: every surviving load in the specialized form has a compile-time address.
  2263	
  2264	**What it does not buy, and this is the point:** the loop is still there. At
  2265	rank 8 with `V2_STRIDE = 256` it is a handful of iterations; at rank 22 it is
  2266	thousands. V1 tried to emit those iterations as code (§5.3) and produced 20 MB
  2267	of IR that took 221 s to compile. V2 emits *one loop with a known bound*, which
  2268	is what LLVM's loop optimizer actually wants.
  2269	
  2270	<figure>
  2271	<img src="diagrams/static-rank-tracking.svg" alt="Static rank tracking through a program" width="100%">
  2272	<figcaption><b>Figure 7.2</b> — The specializer walks the bytecode maintaining
  2273	<code>k</code>, so every rank-dependent bound is a literal at its use site. The
  2274	interpreter must reload <code>st-&gt;active_k</code> at every instruction because
  2275	any preceding op could have changed it.</figcaption>
  2276	</figure>
  2277	
  2278	---
  2279	
  2280	### 7.6 S4 — rank-folded cooperative reduction
  2281	
  2282	**The op** (`v2_ops_body.inc:135-165`) — the most expensive single opcode in the
  2283	ISA, and the one with the ABI constraint from §3:
  2284	
  2285	```c
  2286	static inline V2_NOISE_ATTR void v2_op_meas_active_interfere(V2State* st, CV2Complex* v, u32 active_k,
  2287	                                               u32 axis, u32 slot, u8 flags) {
  2288	    u32 t = v2_tid();
  2289	    u32 half = 1u << (active_k - 1u);
  2290	    int pz = fget(st->pz, axis);
  2291	    double lp = 0.0, lm = 0.0;
  2292	    for (u32 i = t; i < half; i += V2_STRIDE) {
  2293	        CV2Complex vi = v[i], vh = v[i + half];
  2294	        lp += cnorm(cadd(vi, vh)); lm += cnorm(csub(vi, vh));
  2295	    }
  2296	    double p_plus, p_minus; V2_REDUCE2(t, lp, lm, &p_plus, &p_minus);
  2297	    if (IS_OWNER) {
  2298	        u8 b = sample_branch(st->rng, p_plus, p_minus, p_plus + p_minus);
  2299	        st->branch = b;
  2300	        u8 m_abs = b ^ (u8)pz;
  2301	        if (slot < V2_MAX_MEAS) mset(st->meas, slot, m_abs ^ (u8)((flags & FLAG_SIGN) != 0));
  2302	    }
  2303	    v2_barrier();
  2304	    for (u32 i = t; i < half; i += V2_STRIDE) {          // fold
  2305	        CV2Complex vi = v[i], vh = v[i + half];
  2306	        CV2Complex folded = (st->branch == 0) ? cadd(vi, vh) : csub(vi, vh);
  2307	        v[i] = cscale(folded, V2_INV_SQRT2);
  2308	    }
  2309	    v2_barrier();
  2310	    if (IS_OWNER) {
  2311	        st->active_k = active_k - 1;
  2312	        u8 m_abs = mget(st->meas, slot) ^ (u8)((flags & FLAG_SIGN) != 0);
  2313	        fset(st->px, axis, m_abs != 0); fset(st->pz, axis, 0);
  2314	    }
  2315	    v2_barrier();
  2316	}
  2317	```
  2318	
  2319	Two full sweeps over `2^(k-1)` amplitudes with an f64 two-way reduction between
  2320	them, and a PRNG draw at the branch point.
  2321	
  2322	**The two forms** (`cases/S4_meas_active.c`) are a single call:
  2323	
  2324	```c
  2325	#if SPEC_FORM
  2326	    v2_op_meas_active_interfere(st, v, 8u, 5u, 12u, 0u);
  2327	#else
  2328	    v2_op_meas_active_interfere(st, v, st->active_k, i0.axis_1, i0.a, i0.flags);
  2329	#endif
  2330	```
  2331	
  2332	**Result:** 331→240 instructions (1.38×), VALU 139→96 (1.45×), branches 15→7,
  2333	VGPR 24→14, SGPR 30→26. Constant `k` fixes both trip counts and the LDS offsets
  2334	the reduction indexes with; constant `slot`/`flags` folds the bounds check and
  2335	the sign XOR as in S2.
  2336	
  2337	**The `ds_op` column stays at 4 in both forms**, and that is the load-bearing
  2338	observation for §12. Those four are two `ds_write2_b64` / `ds_read2_b64` pairs —
  2339	the cross-wave combine step, where each wave's partial sums go through LDS —
  2340	and specialization does **not** remove or reorder them.
  2341	
  2342	The column understates the evidence, though, because `build_examples.sh` counts
  2343	`ds_(read|write)` only. Counting the shuffle instructions as well:
  2344	
  2345	| | interpreter | specialized |
  2346	|---|---|---|
  2347	| `ds_bpermute_b32` | 32 | 32 |
  2348	| `ds_write2_b64` / `ds_read2_b64` | 2 / 2 | 2 / 2 |
  2349	
  2350	**32 `ds_bpermute_b32` in both forms, exactly.** That is the butterfly: six XOR
  2351	steps at offsets 32/16/8/4/2/1, two f64 values per step, two 32-bit halves per
  2352	f64 — and the second, four-lane reduction on top. Every one survives
  2353	specialization unchanged, in the same order. That is deliberate:
  2354	`v2_ops.h:235-236` records the constraint —
  2355	
  2356	> MUST reproduce SVM `coop_reduce2`'s exact summation order or f64 rounding
  2357	> diverges at measurement branch points.
  2358	
  2359	The reduction is part of the ABI. If specialization had reassociated it, V2
  2360	would compute different `p_plus`/`p_minus` in the last bits, `sample_branch`
  2361	would take a different branch on some shot, the PRNG streams would desync, and
  2362	the outputs would stop being byte-exact. The `4→4` in that column is the
  2363	evidence that it did not. This is also why the compile line carries
  2364	`-ffp-contract=off` (§6.4): FMA contraction would change the summation the same
  2365	way.
  2366	
  2367	VALU 1.45× is the *lowest* gain among the array ops, and that is expected: most
  2368	of the 139 vector instructions are the actual complex arithmetic — `cadd`,
  2369	`csub`, `cnorm`, `cmul` — which is real work, not interpretive overhead. There
  2370	is nothing to fold in an `f32` multiply. Specialization removes the *addressing*
  2371	around the arithmetic, not the arithmetic.
  2372	
