  2374	
  2375	### 7.7 S5 — scatter-index folding
  2376	
  2377	**The op** (`v2_ops_body.inc:168-180`):
  2378	
  2379	```c
  2380	static inline __attribute__((always_inline)) void v2_op_array_cnot(V2State* st, CV2Complex* v, u32 active_k, u32 c, u32 tg) {
  2381	    u32 t = v2_tid();
  2382	    u64 c_bit = 1ull << c, t_bit = 1ull << tg;
  2383	    u64 iters = 1ull << (active_k - 2u);
  2384	    for (u64 i = t; i < iters; i += V2_STRIDE) {
  2385	        u64 base = scatter_bits_2(i, c, tg) | c_bit;
  2386	        CV2Complex a = v[base], b = v[base | t_bit];
  2387	        v[base] = b; v[base | t_bit] = a;
  2388	    }
  2389	    v2_barrier();
  2390	    if (IS_OWNER) { int px_c = fget(st->px, c), pz_t = fget(st->pz, tg); fxor(st->px, tg, px_c); fxor(st->pz, c, pz_t); }
  2391	    v2_barrier();
  2392	}
  2393	```
  2394	
  2395	`scatter_bits_2(i, a, b)` inserts two zero bits at positions `a` and `b` — the
  2396	standard "enumerate all indices with these two bits cleared" trick. With runtime
  2397	axes it is a `min`/`max` plus two variable-shift mask constructions plus three
  2398	shift/or steps, all on 64-bit values, *per iteration*.
  2399	
  2400	**The two forms** (`cases/S5_array_cnot.c`):
  2401	
  2402	```c
  2403	#if SPEC_FORM
  2404	    v2_op_array_cnot(st, v, 8u, 2u, 5u);
  2405	    v2_op_array_cz(st, v, 8u, 1u, 6u);
  2406	#else
  2407	    v2_op_array_cnot(st, v, st->active_k, i0.axis_1, i0.axis_2);
  2408	    v2_op_array_cz(st, v, st->active_k, i1.axis_1, i1.axis_2);
  2409	#endif
  2410	```
  2411	
  2412	**The assembly diff is dramatic.** Specialized, the entire index computation for
  2413	`k=8, c=2, t=5` collapses to five instructions with literal masks:
  2414	
  2415	```asm
  2416		v_cmp_gt_u32_e64 s[0:1], 64, v0     ; i < iters, iters = 1<<(8-2) = 64, a LITERAL
  2417		v_lshlrev_b32_e32 v3, 1, v0
  2418		v_and_b32_e32 v2, 3, v0             ; scatter_bits_2 collapsed to
  2419		v_and_b32_e32 v3, 24, v3            ;   three constant masks
  2420		v_and_b32_e32 v4, 0xc0, v1          ;   and one v_or3
  2421		v_or3_b32     v2, v3, v2, v4
  2422		v_lshlrev_b32_e32 v6, 3, v2
  2423		global_load_dwordx2 v[2:3], v6, s[6:7] offset:288   ; base|t_bit -> a fixed DISPLACEMENT
  2424		global_load_dwordx2 v[4:5], v6, s[6:7] offset:32
  2425	```
  2426	
  2427	`t_bit = 1 << 5` at 8 bytes per amplitude is `offset:288` versus `offset:32` —
  2428	the paired access becomes a **constant address displacement**, folded into the
  2429	memory instruction's encoding. No second address register, no add.
  2430	
  2431	The interpreter form has to build all of it at runtime. First the trip count,
  2432	which cannot be known until `active_k` has been loaded
  2433	(`interp/S5_array_cnot.s:31-35`):
  2434	
  2435	```asm
  2436		s_load_dword s23, s[4:5], 0x278     ; st->active_k -- a memory round trip
  2437		s_add_i32 s23, s23, -2              ; k - 2
  2438		v_lshrrev_b64 v[4:5], s23, v[0:1]   ; iters = 1<<(k-2), by VARIABLE shift
  2439		v_cmp_eq_u64_e32 vcc, 0, v[4:5]
  2440	```
  2441	
  2442	then the index math itself, once per loop entry (`:41-48`):
  2443	
  2444	```asm
  2445		s_min_u32 s10, s22, s21             ; scatter_bits_2: min/max of the two axes
  2446		s_max_u32 s14, s22, s21
  2447		v_and_b32_e32 v2, 0xffff, v2
  2448		s_and_b32 s8, 0xffff, s21
  2449		s_lshl_b64 s[10:11], -1, s10        ; ...then variable-shift masks
  2450		s_lshl_b64 s[14:15], -1, s14
  2451		v_lshlrev_b64 v[2:3], v2, 1         ; 1 << c, on the VECTOR pipe
  2452		s_lshl_b64 s[8:9], 1, s8
  2453	```
  2454	
  2455	followed at `:49-50` by two `s_not_b64` to complete the masks. Those two
  2456	`s_not_b64` appear **twice** in the interpreter file — at `:49-50` and again at
  2457	`:151-152`, once per call site, since `array_cnot` and `array_cz` each rebuild
  2458	the masks — and **zero** times in the specialized file.
  2459	
  2460	Every one of those operands is a literal in the specialized form, so the entire
  2461	sequence — both blocks, both call sites — collapses into the five constant-mask
  2462	instructions quoted above.
  2463	
  2464	**Result:** 207→81 instructions (2.56×), VALU 84→33 (2.55×), VGPR 16→8, SGPR
  2465	24→10, and **`s_load` 4→1** — the largest relative scalar-load reduction in the
  2466	study. The four interpreter loads are, in file order:
  2467	
  2468	```
  2469	:9   s_load_dword   s6,      s[0:1], 0x20    ; pc
  2470	:10  s_load_dwordx2 s[2:3],  s[0:1], 0x18    ; the instrs pointer
  2471	:27  s_load_dwordx4 s[4:7],  s[0:1], 0x0     ; the kernel argument block
  2472	:31  s_load_dword   s23,     s[4:5], 0x278   ; st->active_k
  2473	```
  2474	
  2475	and the specialized form contains exactly one:
  2476	
  2477	```
  2478	:9   s_load_dwordx4 s[4:7],  s[0:1], 0x0     ; the kernel argument block
  2479	```
  2480	
  2481	Only the third survives — it is the kernel argument pointer, which nothing can
  2482	remove. The other three are, precisely, the three things specialization knows:
  2483	where in the program we are, what the instruction says, and what the rank is.
  2484	
  2485	The 16→8 VGPR halving is significant beyond the instruction count: on the coop
  2486	tier, VGPR count sets waves-per-SIMD occupancy, and array two-qubit ops
  2487	dominate the register pressure of any circuit with a lot of entangling gates.
  2488	§9 traces how this fed into the LDS/occupancy work.
  2489	
