  2604	-	s_mul_i32 s6, s0, 40                       ; instruction address
  2605	-	s_load_dwordx2 s[70:71], s[4:5], 0x8       ; ins.a = start, ins.b = count
  2606	-	s_add_i32 s69, s71, s70                    ; end = start + count, at RUNTIME
  2607	-	s_cmp_lt_u32 s0, s70                       ; next_noise < start
  2608	-	s_cselect_b64 s[2:3], -1, 0
  2609	-	s_cmp_ge_u32 s0, s69                       ; next_noise >= end
  2610	-	s_cselect_b64 s[4:5], -1, 0
  2611	-	s_or_b64 s[2:3], s[2:3], s[4:5]
  2612	 	s_load_dword s0, s[64:65], 0x27c           ; st->next_noise -- in BOTH forms
  2613	+	s_add_i32 s1, s0, 0xffffff7d               ; next_noise - 131
  2614	+	s_cmp_gt_u32 s1, 56                        ; ...single unsigned compare vs 56
  2615	```
  2616	
  2617	The two-compare range test becomes the classic single-compare `(x - lo) > (hi -
  2618	lo)` idiom. That is the whole win: four scalar instructions. The 210 surviving
  2619	VALU instructions are `apply_noise_site` and the `ocml_log_f64` hazard draw, and
  2620	none of them care what `start` was.
  2621	
  2622	**Why this negative result is important.** It is the first-principles prediction
  2623	of the `circuit_d5` regression in §11.1. A circuit whose instruction mix is
  2624	dominated by noise ops has almost nothing for the specializer to fold — so
  2625	specialization buys ~1.05× on the op bodies, while *paying* the cost of
  2626	straight-lining hundreds of them into one function (register pressure, I-cache
  2627	pressure, and the FP-scheduling hazard that `V2_NOISE_ATTR` exists to fence).
  2628	
  2629	`coop_circuit_d5.c`, the emitted specialization for `circuit_d5`, contains
  2630	**1,720** `v2_op_*` calls. Counting them by opcode gives the mix directly:
  2631	
  2632	| Opcode family | calls | share | S7 verdict |
  2633	|---|---:|---:|---|
  2634	| `frame_cnot` / `frame_cz` / `frame_h` / `frame_swap` | 757 | 44.0 % | folds well (S1) |
  2635	| `meas_dormant_static` / `_random` | 215 | 12.5 % | folds well (S2) |
  2636	| **`noise` / `noise_block` / `readout_noise`** | **329** | **19.1 %** | **does not fold (S7)** |
  2637	| `apply_pauli` | 135 | 7.8 % | bounded (S8) |
  2638	| `detector` / `observable` | 109 | 6.3 % | — |
  2639	| array ops (`array_t`, `multi_cnot`, `cnot`, `s`, `multi_cz`) | 136 | 7.9 % | bounded (S5, S6) |
  2640	| `expand_t` / `swap_meas_interfere` | 39 | 2.3 % | folds (S3, S4) |
  2641	
  2642	Nearly a fifth of the program is in the one class S7 measured at 1.02× — and
  2643	that class is by far the most *expensive* per call (447 instructions in the
  2644	interpreter form, against 136 for a frame op), so its share of runtime is much
  2645	larger than its share of call sites. The frame and measurement ops that do fold
  2646	well are individually cheap. That is the regression in one table: the ops worth
  2647	specializing are the ones that cost little, and the op that dominates the time
  2648	is the one specialization cannot touch. §11.1 shows what happened when it was
  2649	specialized anyway.
  2650	
  2651	---
  2652	
  2653	### 7.10 S8 — Pauli-mask index folding (a bounded gain, for a different reason)
  2654	
  2655	**The op** (`v2_ops_body.inc:406-413`):
  2656	
  2657	```c
  2658	static inline __attribute__((always_inline)) void v2_op_apply_pauli(V2State* st, const CV2Mask* pauli_masks, u32 cp, u32 cond_slot) {
  2659	    u32 t = v2_tid();
  2660	    if (IS_OWNER && mget(st->meas, cond_slot) != 0) {
  2661	        const CV2Mask* m = &pauli_masks[cp];
  2662	        for (u32 w = 0; w < CLIFFT_V2_PAULI_WORDS; ++w) { st->px[w] ^= m->x[w]; st->pz[w] ^= m->z[w]; }
  2663	    }
  2664	    v2_barrier();
  2665	}
  2666	```
  2667	
  2668	The case comment states the boundary precisely:
  2669	
  2670	> the mask **INDEX** and the measurement slot fold, the mask **CONTENTS** do not
  2671	> (they live in a device buffer).
  2672	
  2673	**Result:** 185→137 instructions (1.35×), VALU 71→63 (1.13×), VGPR 22→21, SGPR
  2674	44→42, `s_load` 17→11, **branches 3→3**.
  2675	
  2676	This is a different shape of bounded gain from S6. In S6 the *arithmetic* was
  2677	irreducible; here the **memory traffic** is. `pauli_masks[cp]` with a constant
  2678	`cp` becomes a fixed displacement — visible directly in the load offsets, which
  2679	go from a *computed* index in the interpreter form
  2680	
  2681	```
  2682	:32  s_load_dwordx2 s[4:5], s[34:35], s7 offset:0x50    ; index in a REGISTER
  2683	```
  2684	
  2685	to three literal displacements in the specialized form, one per call site:
  2686	
  2687	```
  2688	:19  s_load_dwordx16 s[0:15],  s[36:37], 0x0
  2689	:74  s_load_dwordx16 s[0:15],  s[36:37], 0x58   ; pauli_masks[1]
  2690	:118 s_load_dwordx16 s[0:15],  s[36:37], 0xb0   ; pauli_masks[2]
  2691	```
  2692	
  2693	`0x58` is 88 bytes, which is `sizeof(CV2Mask)` for
  2694	`CLIFFT_V2_PAULI_WORDS = 5` — two arrays of five `uint64_t` plus a sign byte,
  2695	padded to 8. The stride between the three constants is exactly one mask. That is
  2696	the 17→11 `s_load` drop. But the five words still have to be *loaded* and XORed
  2697	into the frame, and the number of loads is set by the mask width, not by the
  2698	operand.
  2699	
  2700	**The branch count does not move — but two of the three branches change kind,
  2701	and that is the more interesting fact.** The interpreter form has one
  2702	`s_cbranch_scc1` and two `s_cbranch_vccnz`; the specialized form has three
  2703	`s_cbranch_scc1`. A `vccnz` branch tests the *vector* condition code — the
  2704	lane-mask result of a `v_cmp` — where `scc1` tests the scalar condition code.
  2705	Specialization did not delete the branch, but it moved the *predicate* that
  2706	drives it off the vector pipe, exactly as §7.3 described for the arithmetic.
  2707	What it could not do is remove the branch entirely, because the condition
  2708	`mget(st->meas, cond_slot) != 0` tests a **runtime measurement outcome**. It is
  2709	the same wall as S6's `in_state`: data that only exists once the shot is running.
  2710	Constant `cond_slot` fixes *which bit* is tested; nothing fixes its value.
  2711	
  2712	Together S6, S7, and S8 delimit the technique. Specialization folds:
  2713	
  2714	- **operand-derived address and mask arithmetic** (S1, S5) — large win;
  2715	- **control flow selected by constant flags and bounds** (S2, S3, S6's guard) —
  2716	  large win, and the branch removal matters more on a GPU than the count suggests;
  2717	- **loop trip counts derived from a statically-tracked rank** (S3, S4, S5) — the
  2718	  structural win, and the one V1 tried to get by unrolling instead.
  2719	
  2720	It does **not** fold:
  2721	
  2722	- floating-point arithmetic on amplitudes (S4, S6);
  2723	- anything indexed by the live Pauli frame or a measurement outcome (S6, S8);
  2724	- anything whose trip count depends on the PRNG (S7).
  2725	
  2726	That taxonomy predicts the per-circuit results in §14 well enough that it is
  2727	worth stating as the chapter's conclusion: **a circuit's speedup under V2 is a
  2728	function of how much of its instruction mix falls in the first list.**
  2729	
  2730	---
  2731	
  2732	## 8. Progressive lowering: what the compiler does with it
  2733	
  2734	Chapter 7 measured what specialization does to a *single* op in isolation. This
  2735	chapter follows a whole circuit down both pipelines, stage by stage, and shows
  2736	the actual IR at each boundary.
  2737	
  2738	A note on framing, because the project's own documentation is misleading here
  2739	and the ground rule of this report is to trust data over text. **V2 does not
  2740	emit MLIR.** The directory is called `src/clifft/gpu/mlir/v2/` for historical
  2741	reasons, but `v2_specializer.cc:127` emits a C translation unit whose first line
  2742	is `#include "clifft/gpu/mlir/v2/v2_ops.h"`, and the path to `.hsaco` is
  2743	C → clang → llvm-link → opt → llc → ld.lld. The name is the only MLIR left in
  2744	V2. §5 established the corresponding fact about V1: its MLIR was 100 % `llvm`
  2745	dialect, it authored **zero** custom passes, and one of the three stock passes
  2746	it ran is a complete no-op.
  2747	
  2748	So this chapter cannot show "the diff each MLIR pass we introduced produces,"
  2749	because no such pass exists. What it can show — and does — is the real
  2750	progressive lowering of both pipelines, rendered as diffs, which turns out to be
  2751	a more useful comparison anyway: it shows *where each pipeline's representation
  2752	actually collapses*, and V1's and V2's collapse in completely different places.
  2753	
  2754	### 8.1 The corpus
  2755	
  2756	Thirteen stage pairs are rendered, each as a hunk-scoped `.diff` (greppable, for
  2757	this report) and a colorized side-by-side `.html` (for the deck). All under
  2758	`V2_performance/lowering/diffs/`:
  2759	
  2760	| Pipeline | Circuit | Chain |
  2761	|---|---|---|
  2762	| `v1` | `frame_h`, `circuit_d3` | `emitted.mlir` → `opt.mlir` → `translate.ll` → `optO2.ll` → `isa.s` |
  2763	| `v2` | `circuit_d3` | `emitted.c` → `clangO0.ll` → `clangO2.ll` → `isa.s` |
  2764	| `v1pass` | `frame_h` | `canonicalize` → `cse` → `convert-func-to-llvm` |
  2765	
  2766	The renderer (`lowering/render_diffs.py`) documents its own methodology, and the
  2767	reasoning is worth reproducing because it governs how these diffs should be
  2768	read:
  2769	
  2770	> Why not just `diff -u`: the stage files are up to 20k lines and the
  2771	> interesting change is almost never at the top. `--window` extracts the N
  2772	> largest contiguous change hunks so the report can quote a hunk that actually
  2773	> shows the transform, rather than the first one alphabetically.
  2774	>
  2775	> Stage files are **NOT line-aligned** across a lowering boundary (MLIR SSA
  2776	> names get renumbered by every pass), so a raw line diff between `.mlir` and
  2777	> `.ll` is noise. That is intentional: the diff is evidence of *how much* the
  2778	> representation changed, and the hunk excerpts show *what kind* of change.
  2779	
  2780	Diff sizes, which are themselves a signal:
  2781	
  2782	```
  2783	   16  v1pass.frame_h.1_cse__2_convert-func-to-llvm.diff     <- the no-op
  2784	  189  v1.circuit_d3.4_optO2__5_isa.diff
  2785	  189  v1.frame_h.4_optO2__5_isa.diff
  2786	  189  v2.circuit_d3.3_clangO2__4_isa.diff
  2787	  248  v1.circuit_d3.2_opt__3_translate.diff
  2788	  248  v1.frame_h.2_opt__3_translate.diff
  2789	  255  v1.circuit_d3.1_emitted__2_opt.diff
  2790	  255  v1.frame_h.1_emitted__2_opt.diff
  2791	  328  v2.circuit_d3.1_emitted__2_clangO0.diff
  2792	  404  v1.frame_h.3_translate__4_optO2.diff
  2793	  456  v2.circuit_d3.2_clangO0__3_clangO2.diff
  2794	  488  v1.circuit_d3.3_translate__4_optO2.diff
  2795	  576  v1pass.frame_h.0_canonicalize__1_cse.diff
  2796	```
  2797	
  2798	### 8.2 Line counts through both pipelines
  2799	
  2800	The most compact way to see the difference is the size of the representation at
  2801	each stage, for the same circuit (`circuit_d3`, 344 instructions, register tier):
  2802	
  2803	| Stage | V1 | | Stage | V2 |
  2804	|---|---|---|---|---|
  2805	| `1_emitted.mlir` | **23,002** | | `1_emitted.c` | **383** |
  2806	| `2_opt.mlir` | 14,257 | | `2_clangO0.ll` | 19,551 |
  2807	| `3_translate.ll` | 14,875 | | `3_clangO2.ll` | 8,132 |
  2808	| `4_optO2.ll` | 13,747 | | | |
  2809	| `5_isa.s` | 17,802 | | `4_isa.s` | 10,398 |
  2810	
  2811	And the small circuit, `frame_h` (4 instructions), on V1 only — V2 does not have
  2812	a rendered chain for it, but its emitted C is 43 lines:
  2813	
  2814	| Stage | V1 `frame_h` |
  2815	|---|---|
  2816	| `1_emitted.mlir` | 947 |
  2817	| `2_opt.mlir` | 606 |
  2818	| `3_translate.ll` | 562 |
  2819	| `4_optO2.ll` | 275 |
  2820	| `5_isa.s` | 524 |
  2821	
  2822	Two shapes, and they are opposites:
  2823	
  2824	- **V1 starts huge and shrinks.** 23,002 lines of `llvm`-dialect MLIR for 344
  2825	  instructions. Every optimization stage is *recovering* from the emitter.
  2826	- **V2 starts tiny and expands, then collapses.** 383 lines of C → 19,551 lines
  2827	  of unoptimized IR (because `-O0` gives every local variable an `alloca` and
  2828	  every access an `addrspacecast`) → 8,132 lines at `-O2`. The expansion is a
  2829	  *compiler artifact of `-O0`*, not something the emitter wrote down, and `-O2`
  2830	  removes it entirely.
  2831	
  2832	The endpoint is what matters: **17,802 vs 10,398 ISA lines for the same
  2833	circuit.** V2 produces a 1.7× smaller kernel from a 60× smaller source.
  2834	
  2835	<figure>
  2836	<img src="diagrams/lowering-pipelines.svg" alt="V1 and V2 lowering pipelines with per-stage sizes" width="100%">
  2837	<figcaption><b>Figure 8.1</b> — The two pipelines with per-stage line counts for
  2838	<code>circuit_d3</code>. V1's representation is largest where a human wrote it;
  2839	V2's is largest where the compiler expanded it at <code>-O0</code> and smallest
  2840	again after <code>-O2</code>.</figcaption>
  2841	</figure>
  2842	
  2843	---
  2844	
  2845	### 8.3 V1's MLIR stage: what `canonicalize` and `cse` actually did
  2846	
  2847	`mlir_codegen.cc:65-67` runs exactly three passes. Snapshots were taken with
  2848	`mlir-opt --mlir-print-ir-tree-dir` so each pass's output is a separate file
  2849	(`lowering/v1_passes/frame_h/`):
  2850	
  2851	```
  2852	0_canonicalize.mlir   736 lines
  2853	1_cse.mlir            608 lines
  2854	2_convert-func-to-llvm.mlir  608 lines
  2855	```
  2856	
  2857	(from an emitted 947.)
  2858	
  2859	**`canonicalize` folded duplicate constants.** The op census for `frame_h`
  2860	before and after the MLIR stage:
  2861	
  2862	| Op | emitted | after opt |
  2863	|---|---|---|
  2864	| `llvm.mlir.constant` | 240 | **59** |
  2865	| `llvm.and` | 54 | 9 |
  2866	| `llvm.shl` | 73 | 34 |
  2867	| `llvm.xor` | 68 | 29 |
  2868	| `llvm.ptr` † | 192 | 160 |
  2869	
  2870	† `llvm.ptr` is a *type*, not an op, and occurs several times on some lines
  2871	(each `getelementptr` mentions it twice). Its row counts occurrences (192 → 160)
  2872	where the op rows count one per line; by line it is 136 → 118. The other four
  2873	rows are unambiguous — one op per line.
  2874	
  2875	The emitter materialized the same constant over and over — 240 constant ops for
  2876	a four-instruction circuit — and canonicalization deduplicated them down to 59.
  2877	This is not a sophisticated transform; it is cleanup after a naive emitter.
  2878	
  2879	**`cse` deleted redundant loads and recomputation.** The
  2880	`0_canonicalize → 1_cse` diff is the largest in the corpus (576 lines), and the
  2881	hunk it selects is a PRNG update inside the shot loop that had been emitted
  2882	twice:
  2883	
  2884	```diff
  2885	     %138 = llvm.load %45 : !llvm.ptr -> i64
  2886	-    %139 = llvm.getelementptr inbounds %45[1] : (!llvm.ptr) -> !llvm.ptr, i64
  2887	-    %140 = llvm.load %139 : !llvm.ptr -> i64
  2888	-    %141 = llvm.getelementptr inbounds %45[2] : (!llvm.ptr) -> !llvm.ptr, i64
  2889	-    %142 = llvm.load %141 : !llvm.ptr -> i64
  2890	-    %145 = llvm.add %138, %144 : i64
  2891	-    %146 = llvm.shl %145, %16 : i64
  2892	-    %147 = llvm.lshr %145, %15 : i64
  2893	-    %148 = llvm.or %146, %147 : i64        ; xoshiro256++ rotate
  2894	-    %151 = llvm.xor %142, %138 : i64
  2895	-    %152 = llvm.xor %144, %140 : i64
  2896	-    ...
  2897	-    %165 = llvm.lshr %33, %35 : i64        ; frame word index, computed
  2898	-    %166 = llvm.getelementptr inbounds %49[%165] : ...
  2899	-    %175 = llvm.lshr %33, %35 : i64        ; ...and computed AGAIN
  2900	-    %176 = llvm.getelementptr inbounds %51[%175] : ...
  2901	```
  2902	
  2903	Note the last four lines: `llvm.lshr %33, %35` appears twice with identical
  2904	operands, once for `px` and once for `pz`. The emitter had no notion of a
  2905	common subexpression, so it wrote out the word-index computation separately for
  2906	each frame array. CSE removes it. This is exactly the class of redundancy that
  2907	**does not exist in V2's C**, because in V2 that computation is written once in
  2908	`fget` and inlined by clang after constant-folding the index away entirely
  2909	(§7.3).
  2910	
  2911	**`convert-func-to-llvm` did nothing at all.** The full rendered diff is 16
  2912	lines, and the only change is the pass-name banner:
  2913	
  2914	```diff
  2915	-// -----// IR Dump After CSE (cse) //----- //
  2916	+// -----// IR Dump After ConvertFuncToLLVMPass (convert-func-to-llvm) //----- //
  2917	 module attributes {llvm.target_triple = "amdgcn-amd-amdhsa"} {
  2918	   llvm.func @llvm.amdgcn.workitem.id.x() -> i32
  2919	   llvm.func @llvm.amdgcn.workgroup.id.x() -> i32
  2920	   llvm.func @llvm.amdgcn.s.barrier()
  2921	   llvm.func @llvm.amdgcn.ds.bpermute(i32, i32) -> i32
  2922	   llvm.func @clifft_log(%arg0: f64) -> f64 {
  2923	```
  2924	
  2925	608 lines in, 608 lines out, byte-identical below the banner. There is no `func`
  2926	dialect to convert *because the emitter never produced any* — everything was
  2927	already `llvm.func`. This is the clearest single piece of evidence that V1 was
  2928	not using MLIR as a multi-level IR: the pass whose entire job is to lower a
  2929	higher abstraction level found nothing to lower.
  2930	
  2931	> **What this means for the "MLIR passes" material.** The report's goal asked
  2932	> for progressive-lowering diffs of the passes introduced. The honest answer is
  2933	> that the value MLIR added to V1 was *two stock cleanup passes recovering from
  2934	> the emitter's own verbosity*. That is a real thing to show, and it is shown
  2935	> above — but it is not a compiler-engineering contribution, and V2 got the same
  2936	> cleanup for free by writing C and letting clang's front end never emit the
  2937	> redundancy in the first place.
  2938	
  2939	---
  2940	
  2941	### 8.4 V1: `translate` → `opt -O2`, where the real work happened
  2942	
  2943	MLIR's contribution ended at 608 lines. The transform that actually mattered
  2944	happened in LLVM. `frame_h.3_translate.ll` (562) → `frame_h.4_optO2.ll` (275)
  2945	is a 2× collapse, and the hunk shows what:
  2946	
  2947	```diff
  2948	-define amdgpu_kernel void @compiled_mlir_kernel(i64 inreg %0, ..., i32 inreg %9) {
  2949	-  %11 = alloca i32, align 4, addrspace(5)
  2950	-  %12 = addrspacecast ptr addrspace(5) %11 to ptr
  2951	-  %13 = alloca i64, i32 4, align 8, addrspace(5)
  2952	-  %14 = addrspacecast ptr addrspace(5) %13 to ptr
  2953	-  %15 = alloca i64, align 8, addrspace(5)
  2954	-  ...
  2955	-  %27 = alloca i8, i32 4096, align 1, addrspace(5)     ; <- 4 KB of scratch
  2956	-  %28 = addrspacecast ptr addrspace(5) %27 to ptr
  2957	-  %31 = call i32 @llvm.amdgcn.workitem.id.x()
  2958	+; Function Attrs: mustprogress nofree norecurse nounwind willreturn
  2959	+define amdgpu_kernel void @compiled_mlir_kernel(..., ptr noalias captures(none) %3,
  2960	+       ptr noalias readnone captures(none) %4, ...) local_unnamed_addr #4 {
  2961	+  %.global = addrspacecast ptr %3 to ptr addrspace(1)
  2962	+  %11 = tail call i32 @llvm.amdgcn.workitem.id.x()
  2963	```
  2964	
  2965	Ten `alloca`/`addrspacecast` pairs — the emitter's model of local state as stack
  2966	slots — promoted to SSA registers by SROA/mem2reg, and the kernel gains
  2967	`nofree norecurse nounwind willreturn` plus `noalias readnone captures(none)`
  2968	on the pointer arguments. **LLVM did this, not MLIR.** The MLIR stage had the
  2969	same `alloca`s in front of it for two passes and left every one of them
  2970	standing.
  2971	
  2972	For the larger circuit the collapse is far weaker: `circuit_d3` goes 14,875 →
  2973	13,747, only 7.6 %. That is the size-adaptive detuning from §5.3 starting to
  2974	bite — at 20 MB of IR, LLVM's own pass pipeline gets throttled
  2975	(`mlir_kernel_cache.cc:104-141`), and the very optimizations that rescued
  2976	`frame_h` stop being affordable.
  2977	
  2978	The final stage, `optO2 → isa`, actually *grows* the file: 275 → 524 lines for
  2979	`frame_h`, 13,747 → 17,802 for `circuit_d3`. That is normal — one IR instruction
  2980	becomes several machine instructions plus directives — but for `circuit_d3` it
  2981	means V1 shipped 17,802 lines of ISA for a 344-instruction circuit.
  2982	
  2983	---
  2984	
  2985	### 8.5 V2: `emitted.c` → `clangO0` → `clangO2`
  2986	
  2987	V2's chain has one interesting boundary and it is a very different one.
  2988	
  2989	**`emitted.c` → `clangO0.ll`: 383 → 19,551 lines.** A 51× expansion, and the
  2990	diff shows exactly why:
  2991	
  2992	```diff
  2993	-  %pauli_masks.addr.ascast = addrspacecast ptr addrspace(5) %pauli_masks.addr to ptr
  2994	-  %readout_noise.addr.ascast = addrspacecast ptr addrspace(5) %readout_noise.addr to ptr
  2995	-  %shot_id.ascast = addrspacecast ptr addrspace(5) %shot_id to ptr
  2996	-  store ptr %instrs, ptr %instrs.addr.ascast, align 8
