  3047	Read those last two rows carefully, because they are the whole point. `alloca`
  3048	goes from 2,271 to **3** — essentially all shot-local state now lives in
  3049	registers. The three survivors are worth naming, because they are exactly the
  3050	state that *cannot* be promoted:
  3051	
  3052	```llvm
  3053	; the only allocas left in clifft_v2_spec after -O2
  3054	%st   = alloca %struct.V2State,          align 8, addrspace(5)
  3055	%vloc = alloca [16 x %struct.CV2Complex], align 8, addrspace(5)
  3056	%sloc = alloca [8  x %struct.CV2Complex], align 8, addrspace(5)
  3057	```
  3058	
  3059	All three are in `clifft_v2_spec` itself, all in `addrspace(5)` (private/scratch),
  3060	and all three are *aggregates whose address escapes* — `%st` is passed by pointer
  3061	to every `v2_op_*` that SROA could not fully inline away (the `noinline` noise
  3062	ops), and `%vloc`/`%sloc` are the fixed-size complex staging arrays. SROA
  3063	promotes scalars and small aggregates with non-escaping addresses; these are
  3064	neither. This is the residue that shows up in the `private=336` segment size in
  3065	the dispatch log, and it is the mechanism behind the scratch traffic discussed in
  3066	§10. **The interesting claim is not that `alloca` reached zero — it did not —
  3067	but that what remains is a fixed, per-kernel constant rather than something that
  3068	scales with circuit length.** 2,271 grew with the instruction count; 3 does not.
  3069	
