  3114	
  3115	### 8.6 The f64 gulf, and why it is not a precision difference
  3116	
  3117	The final ISA census turns up the single largest disparity anywhere in the
  3118	corpus, and it is the one most likely to be misread. For `circuit_d3`:
  3119	
  3120	| | V1 | V2 |
  3121	|---|---|---|
  3122	| `v_*_f64` | **4,347** | **187** |
  3123	| `v_*_f32` | 1,586 | 858 |
  3124	| `v_pk_*_f32` (packed) | **0** | **847** |
  3125	| total instructions | 16,675 | 9,421 |
  3126	
  3127	V1 issues **23× more f64 instructions**. The obvious reading — V1 did its
  3128	amplitude arithmetic in double and V2 dropped to single, trading accuracy for
  3129	speed — is wrong, and it matters that it is wrong, because §12 rests on both
  3130	backends being byte-exact against the same interpreter. They are. Both carry
  3131	amplitudes as `f32` pairs, and both widen to `f64` in exactly the same two
  3132	places, deliberately:
  3133	
  3134	```cpp
  3135	// V2 — v2_ops.h:206
  3136	// f64 scalar multiply then narrow — byte-exact with SVM cscale. Do NOT relax.
  3137	static inline CV2Complex cscale(CV2Complex a, double s) {
  3138	    CV2Complex r; r.re = (float)((double)a.re * s); r.im = (float)((double)a.im * s); return r;
  3139	}
  3140	static inline double cnorm(CV2Complex v) {
  3141	    double re = (double)v.re, im = (double)v.im; return re * re + im * im;
  3142	}
  3143	```
  3144	
  3145	```cpp
  3146	// V1 — mlir_emit.cc:826, inside emit_cnorm
  3147	// Match gold cnorm (hip_sampler.hip): extend each f32 component to f64,
  3148	// then square and sum in f64. Squaring in f32 first (then extending) loses
  3149	// precision and flips borderline stabilizer measurement branches on
  3150	// RNG-path-dependent shots.
  3151	```
  3152	
  3153	Same rule, same reason, two backends. So where do 4,347 f64 instructions come
  3154	from?
  3155	
  3156	#### The experiment
  3157	
  3158	`V2_performance/lowering/f64_attribution.sh` compiles V1's own stage-3 IR twice
  3159	through V1's own pipeline (`opt -O2 | llc -O2`), changing exactly one thing:
  3160	
  3161	- **A** — as-is. Asserted to reproduce `v1/circuit_d3.5_isa.s` byte-for-byte.
  3162	- **B** — `clifft_log` and `clifft_draw_next_noise` marked `noinline`. Same
  3163	  amplitude arithmetic, same PRNG, but the transcendental stays a call.
  3164	
  3165	```
  3166	variant, isa_lines, isa_total_instrs, v_f64, v_f32, v_pk_f32, scratch_ops, scratch_bytes, log_expansions
  3167	A,           17802,            16675,  4347,  1586,        0,          56,           156,             54
  3168	B,           12038,            11148,  1380,  1586,        0,         477,           224,              1
  3169	```
  3170	
  3171	<figure>
  3172	<img src="diagrams/f64-attribution.svg" alt="Where V1's 4,347 f64 instructions come from" width="100%">
  3173	<figcaption><b>Figure 8.2</b> — V1's f64 instruction volume for <code>circuit_d3</code>,
  3174	decomposed by the A/B experiment. 68 % is 54 inlined copies of a hand-written
  3175	log polynomial; the 1,380 that remain are the same PRNG, <code>cnorm</code> and
  3176	<code>cscale</code> sites V2 has. V2 keeps <code>log()</code> as a call to
  3177	<code>__ocml_log_f64</code> because it links ROCm device bitcode; V1 had no such
  3178	link and had to write the polynomial in MLIR.</figcaption>
  3179	</figure>
  3180	
  3181	`v_*_f32` is **identical** across A and B — 1,586 either way. The amplitude
  3182	arithmetic never moved. What moved is 2,967 f64 instructions, **68 % of V1's
  3183	entire f64 volume**, and 5,527 total instructions, all of it the log
  3184	polynomial.
  3185	
  3186	The count is exact, not estimated. `0x3FD5555555555555` is a coefficient unique
  3187	to that polynomial, so it counts expansions directly:
  3188	
  3189	```
  3190	per-coefficient occurrence count in the V1 kernel body (circuit_d3.4_optO2.ll):
  3191	  0x3FE62E42FEFA39EF  x54     <- ln 2
  3192	  0x3FA1A7B9611A7B96  x54
  3193	  0x3FD5555555555555  x54     <- the marker the script counts
  3194	```
  3195	
  3196	The count is uniform across every coefficient, which is what makes it a count
  3197	of *whole expansions* rather than of incidental constants: **54 copies**, one
  3198	per `clifft_log` inlined into the kernel. The emitter wrote **48** `clifft_draw_next_noise` call sites (each of which
  3199	contains a `clifft_log` call) plus one direct `clifft_log` site, against a
  3200	single shared `@clifft_log` definition; LLVM inlined all of them and cloned five
  3201	more while unrolling the surrounding loops. At 37 f64 ops per copy that
  3202	is 1,998 IR-level ops — 63.6 % of the kernel's 3,141 — before instruction
  3203	selection turns each `fdiv double` into the `v_rcp_f64` / `v_div_scale_f64` /
  3204	`v_div_fmas_f64` / `v_div_fixup_f64` sequence that pushes the ISA-level share to
  3205	68 %.
  3206	
  3207	#### What is actually left
  3208	
  3209	Strip the expansion and classify the residue by dataflow component:
  3210	
  3211	| what it is | ops | share |
  3212	|---|---|---|
  3213	| PRNG: `u64 → f64 × 2⁻⁵³` | 465 | 45.5 % |
  3214	| `cnorm` — f64 \|a\|² accumulation | 390 | 38.1 % |
  3215	| `cscale` — f32 → f64 × 1/√2 → f32 | 168 | 16.4 % |
  3216	| | **1,023** | |
  3217	
  3218	Three sites — and **the same three sites are all V2 has**, 101 f64 ops across
  3219	its whole module:
  3220	
  3221	| function | f64 ops | `u64→f64 × 2⁻⁵³` | `fpext`/`fptrunc` | what it is |
  3222	|---|---|---|---|---|
  3223	| `clifft_v2_spec` | 56 | 18 | 0 / 0 | 18 PRNG draws, nothing else |
  3224	| `v2_op_noise` | 10 | 2 | 0 / 0 | PRNG |
  3225	| `v2_op_noise_block` | 10 | 2 | 0 / 0 | PRNG |
  3226	| `v2_op_swap_meas_interfere` | 22 | 1 | 4 / 2 | cnorm + cscale |
  3227	| `v2_op_readout_noise` | 3 | 1 | 0 / 0 | PRNG |
  3228	
  3229	The specialized body itself touches f64 **only** to turn random bits into a
  3230	uniform — every `fpext`/`fptrunc` pair in the module is inside one `noinline`
  3231	measurement helper. Identical semantics to V1, three orders of magnitude apart
  3232	in count, because V2's loops stayed runtime loops and V1's were unrolled.
  3233	
  3234	#### Why V1 had a hand-written log at all
  3235	
  3236	V1's compile pipeline is `opt` → `llc` → `ld.lld`
  3237	(`mlir_kernel_cache.cc:124-150`). There is no `llvm-link` step and no ROCm
  3238	device bitcode, so `log()` simply does not exist as a symbol. The emitter had
  3239	to write the polynomial itself, in MLIR, by hand — `emit_log_body`,
  3240	`mlir_emit.cc:1355`. Its author saw the size problem coming and hoisted it:
  3241	
  3242	```cpp
  3243	// Emit the standalone @clifft_log(f64)->f64 function definition ONCE per
  3244	// module. Inlining the ~64-line log body at every noise draw produced
  3245	// hundreds of thousands of IR lines for large noisy circuits (e.g.
  3246	// surface_d9_t5: 1444 OP_NOISE × ~317 lines). Hoisting to a called function
  3247	// collapses that to one definition + short call sites.
  3248	void emit_log_function_def(std::ostringstream& out) {          // :1422
  3249	```
  3250	
  3251	The hoist worked at *emission* — the emitted MLIR has one `@clifft_log`
  3252	definition and 48 short call sites, and that is what kept `surface_d9_t5`
  3253	emittable at all. Then `opt -O2` inlined all 54 copies back. The emitter
  3254	controlled its own output and had no way to control what the optimizer did
  3255	next.
  3256	
  3257	V2 has the symbol, because it links the device libraries:
  3258	
  3259	```cpp
  3260	// v2_ops.h:262
  3261	extern double __ocml_log_f64(double);
  3262	static inline double ocml_log_f64(double x) { return __ocml_log_f64(x); }
  3263	```
  3264	
  3265	```cpp
  3266	// v2_compile_cache.cc:161 — step 2 of 5
  3267	run(llvmlink + " -o " + linked + " " + bc + " " +
  3268	    ctl + "/ocml.bc " + ctl + "/ockl.bc " + ...);
  3269	```
  3270	
  3271	In V2's final ISA the call survives as a relocation, three times:
  3272	
  3273	```asm
  3274		s_getpc_b64 s[0:1]
  3275		s_add_u32   s0, s0, __ocml_log_f64@rel32@lo+4
  3276		s_addc_u32  s1, s1, __ocml_log_f64@rel32@hi+12
  3277		s_swappc_b64 s[30:31], s[0:1]
  3278	```
  3279	
  3280	Three call sites, one shared body, against V1's 54 expansions.
  3281	
  3282	#### The one real arithmetic difference
  3283	
  3284	Underneath all of that there *is* a genuine codegen difference, and the f64
  3285	noise was hiding it. `v_pk_*_f32` is **0** in V1 — in both variant A and
  3286	variant B, so the log expansion was never what blocked it — and **847** in V2.
  3287	Counting packed instructions as two lanes, V2 covers 1,705 f32 lanes in 858
  3288	instructions where V1 needs 1,586 instructions for 1,586 lanes. That is the
  3289	`shufflevector` → `v_pk_add_f32` chain of §8.5, and it is available to V2 for
  3290	the reason §8.5 gives: the amplitudes are in registers, not in a stack array.
  3291	
  3292	**The honest summary: V1's f64 volume was a transcendental-inlining artifact,
  3293	not a numerical choice. Both backends compute the same quantities at the same
  3294	widths. The real instruction-level win is packing, and it is 2× on the f32
  3295	path — not 23× on the f64 one.**
  3296	
