  3757	now pass the gate and run specialized.
  3758	
  3759	(The step must be measured from the `specializer-final` column, not from the
  3760	italic `1.427`/`1.402`. Those intermediate cells are the gate-polluted ones:
  3761	they are *already partly specialized*, being the sum of an interpreter dispatch
  3762	and two specialized dispatches, so they sit between the two real values and
  3763	understate the step. The VALU counter makes the three states plain —
  3764	4.71e+09 interpreter, 3.49e+09 polluted mixture, 1.15e+09 specialized.)
  3765	
  3766	**But the stated explanation was wrong, and the correction matters.** The commit
  3767	attributed the divergence to `-O2` reassociating FP across inlined call
  3768	boundaries. Four commits later (`150d09f`) that theory was refuted:
  3769	
  3770	> That theory was refuted: the build is `-O2 -ffp-contract=off` with no
  3771	> fast-math, under which **inlining cannot legally change an FP result**, and
  3772	> `V2_GATE_SELFTEST` caught the kernel **disagreeing with itself across two runs
  3773	> of the same binary.**
  3774	
  3775	A deterministic kernel that disagrees with itself is not a rounding problem. It
  3776	is a race. §11.2 tells that story. `V2_NOISE_ATTR` was retained — but for code
  3777	size, not correctness — and `V2_SPEC_NOISE_INLINE=1` exists precisely so the
  3778	hypothesis can be A/B tested rather than assumed.
  3779	
  3780	This is the single best example in the project of the report's ground rule
  3781	paying off. The fence *worked*; the *reason given for why it worked* was false;
  3782	and believing the false reason would have left the actual race in the code.
  3783	
  3784	---
  3785	
  3786	### 9.6 One more correctness fix that looked like a performance result (`842d646`)
  3787	
  3788	Worth including because it is a trap this kind of work sets constantly. V2
  3789	counted the **raw** observable parity; every reference implementation — GPU-SVM
  3790	in `hip_sampler.hip` and the CPU sampler in `svm.cc` — XORs it against the
  3791	noiseless reference syndrome first. On any circuit whose reference observable is
  3792	1, V2 reported the exact **complement** of SVM's count: `surface_d11_t19` gave
  3793	674 where SVM gave 1326 of 2000 passed shots.
  3794	
  3795	The fix threads the reference through as a packed `u32 expected_obs_mask`. The
  3796	implementation detail is the interesting part:
  3797	
  3798	> placed in the implicit pad slot after `num_noise_sites` so the kernarg struct
  3799	> **SIZE is unchanged** (a 120-vs-116 mismatch silently breaks every dispatch).
  3800	
  3801	The layout confirms it — compiling `device_abi.h` and printing the offsets:
  3802	
  3803	```
  3804	sizeof(CV2KernArgs)        = 152
  3805	offsetof num_noise_sites   = 112
  3806	offsetof expected_obs_mask = 116
  3807	```
  3808	
  3809	The new field sits at 116, in the four bytes of padding that the `u32` at 112
  3810	already forced the compiler to reserve ahead of the next 8-byte-aligned member.
  3811	**A whole new argument was added for free.** (The commit's "120-vs-116" refers
  3812	to that boundary, not to the total, which is 152 both before and after.)
  3813	
  3814	With HSA dispatch there is no runtime checking the kernarg layout for you (§13):
  3815	the AQL packet carries a pointer and the kernel casts it. A size or offset
  3816	mismatch is not an error, it is silently reading the wrong bytes. That is why
  3817	`device_abi_checks.cc` exists — 55 `static_assert`s pinning every offset and
  3818	size in the device header to the host structs in `gpu_types.h`, plus the opcode
  3819	numbering:
  3820	
  3821	```c
  3822	static_assert(static_cast<int>(clifft::Opcode::OP_FRAME_CNOT) == 0, "opcode FRAME_CNOT");
  3823	static_assert(static_cast<int>(clifft::Opcode::OP_EXPAND)     == 19, "opcode EXPAND");
  3824	static_assert(sizeof(CV2Instr) == sizeof(GpuInstr), "CV2Instr size");
  3825	static_assert(offsetof(CV2Instr, opcode) == offsetof(GpuInstr, opcode), "instr.opcode");
  3826	```
  3827	
  3828	Its header comment records that this is not hypothetical: *"this caught an
  3829	off-by-one: EXPAND/MEAS were numbered -1."* A build error instead of a silent
  3830	GPU miscompute is the whole return on the file.
  3831	
  3832	---
  3833	
  3834	### 9.7 Where the wins came from, summarized
  3835	
  3836	| Change | Mechanism | Who it helped | Magnitude |
  3837	|---|---|---|---|
  3838	| P0 shot-packed register tier | topology: 1 shot/thread instead of 256 threads/shot | rank ≤ 4 | **28.3× → 1.2×** |
  3839	| P1a/P1b LDS reclamation | 25,088 → 13,312 B/workgroup | all coop | ~10 %, mechanism unconfirmed on gfx950 (§9.3) |
  3840	| Specializer, register | constant-fold operands, delete dispatch | rank ≤ 4 | 3.3× over interpreter |
  3841	| Specializer, coop | same, plus rank-folded loop bounds | rank 5–10 | **3.5–3.7×** over interpreter |
  3842	| Noise fence + gate | made noise circuits *eligible* to specialize | noise-heavy coop | 1.79 → 0.50 |
  3843	| Specializer, global | fold `scatter_bits_2`, no LUT needed | rank 11–19 | **3.3–3.9×** over interpreter |
  3844	| Rank cap 19 → 26 | size HBM from circuit rank, not the cap | rank 20–26 | new capability (§10) |
  3845	
  3846	Two rows changed under audit and are worth naming: the coop specializer is
  3847	**3.5–3.7×**, not the 5.5–7.6× its commit message claims (§9.4), and the P1
  3848	occupancy mechanism does not hold on this chip (§9.3). Both corrections came
  3849	from the archived runs rather than from the commit log.
  3850	
  3851	The specialization rows now agree with each other — **~3.3–3.9× across all
  3852	three tiers** — which is a stronger claim than the scattered original figures,
  3853	because it says the lever is the same lever everywhere: resolve the opcode at
  3854	compile time.
  3855	
  3856	The pattern across the whole table: **every large win came from removing
  3857	something the runtime was doing, not from making the arithmetic faster.** The
  3858	arithmetic — the complex multiplies in the butterfly — is essentially unchanged
  3859	from SVM. What changed is that V2 stopped fetching instructions, stopped
  3860	switching on opcodes, stopped recomputing indices, and stopped reloading the
  3861	rank.
  3862	
  3863	---
  3864	
  3865	## 10. Extending past rank 19
  3866	
  3867	The global tier's cap was `kGlobalMaxPeakRank = 19` for the entire life of the
  3868	GPU backend. Raising it to 26 required exactly one substantive change, and
  3869	finding a benchmark that could exercise it required a separate investigation
  3870	that produced a more interesting result than the change itself.
  3871	
  3872	### 10.1 Why the cap could not simply be raised
  3873	
  3874	The blocker was an addressing convention, not a memory limit. Every global-tier
  3875	kernel strided its per-workgroup HBM slice by **`kGlobalMaxAmplitudes`** — the
  3876	*cap* — rather than by the circuit's actual amplitude count:
  3877	
  3878	```c
  3879	// before: every slot is 2^kGlobalMaxPeakRank amplitudes wide, regardless of circuit
  3880	CV2Complex* v = global_v + (u64)slot * kGlobalMaxAmplitudes;
  3881	```
  3882	
  3883	At rank 19 that reserves a 4 MB amplitude slice per resident workgroup, which is
  3884	fine. At rank 26 it reserves **512 MB per workgroup** — for a rank-3 circuit as
  3885	much as for a rank-26 one. With a 2,048-workgroup pool that is a terabyte of HBM
  3886	to run `frame_h`. (Both figures are the `global_v` slice alone, which is what
  3887	the stride above governs; §10.2's quoted code comment counts the half-size
  3888	scratch too, so the same two points read 6 MB and 768 MB there.) The cap was therefore self-limiting: raising the constant made *every
  3889	circuit* unaffordable, not just the large ones.
  3890	
  3891	### 10.2 The fix (`b266f80`)
  3892	
  3893	Derive every per-slot stride from `flat.peak_rank`. The commit had to change it
  3894	in **four** places that must agree with each other:
  3895	
  3896	| File | What it addresses |
  3897	|---|---|
  3898	| `hip_sampler.hip` | `sample_kernel_global_coop` device addressing **and** the matching host `v_count`/`scratch_count` allocation |
  3899	| `emit_global_kernel.cc` | the Hybrid compiled-codegen kernel |
  3900	| `mlir_emit.cc` | the MLIR V1 global kernel |
  3901	| `v2_kernel.cc` | already rank-derived on the device side; only the resident-pool sizing needed the same treatment |
  3902	
  3903	The `emit_global_kernel.cc` change fixed a **latent out-of-bounds bug** that had
  3904	nothing to do with the cap:
  3905	
  3906	> `scratch_v` used the FULL `v` stride while the host allocated scratch at HALF
  3907	> the amplitude count.
  3908	
  3909	Scratch is only ever `2^(k-1)` amplitudes — it holds the fold half — so the host
  3910	allocated half. The device strided as if it were full. Any global-tier circuit
  3911	using more than one workgroup was addressing past its scratch slice. It had
  3912	never been caught because the overrun landed in the *next* workgroup's scratch,
  3913	which is written before it is read on the next op.
  3914	
  3915	The second half of the fix is a **HBM budget** replacing the fixed pool
  3916	(`v2_kernel.cc:432-446`):
  3917	
  3918	```cpp
  3919	// Resident pool sized to a fixed HBM budget: each workgroup owns one
  3920	// amplitude slice (1<<peak_rank) + a half-size scratch, i.e. 12 bytes
  3921	// per amplitude. At rank 19 that is 6 MB/wg; at rank 26 it is 768 MB/wg.
  3922	// The 32 GB budget keeps the pool sane at both ends on a 288 GB MI355X.
  3923	const uint64_t amp = 1ull << flat.peak_rank;
  3924	const uint64_t bytes_per_wg = amp * sizeof(GpuComplex) + (amp / 2) * sizeof(GpuComplex);
  3925	const uint64_t budget = 32ull << 30;  // 32 GB
  3926	uint64_t wgs = budget / bytes_per_wg;
  3927	if (wgs < 1) wgs = 1;               // rank 26+: at least one resident wg
  3928	if (wgs > 2048) wgs = 2048;
  3929	global_grid_wgs = static_cast<uint32_t>(wgs);
  3930	// Prefer a multiple of the XCD count when the budget allows it, so the
  3931	// resident pool spreads evenly; never inflate past the budget.
  3932	if (global_grid_wgs > kNumXCDs) global_grid_wgs -= global_grid_wgs % kNumXCDs;
  3933	```
  3934	
  3935	So the pool now **shrinks as rank grows** instead of the allocation exploding.
  3936	At rank 19 the pool clamps at 2,048; at rank 26 it falls to a handful of
  3937	resident workgroups. The XCD-alignment step (`kNumXCDs = 8`) keeps the pool
  3938	spread evenly across the device's eight compute dies.
  3939	
  3940	One constraint worth recording: the cap is kept `≤ 30` so `1u << rank` stays
  3941	inside a `u32`.
  3942	
  3943	<figure>
  3944	<img src="diagrams/hbm-budget-pool.svg" alt="Resident pool size vs circuit rank under the 32 GB budget" width="100%">
  3945	<figcaption><b>Figure 10.1</b> — Resident workgroup pool under the 32 GB budget.
  3946	Below rank ~19 the pool is clamped at 2,048 workgroups; above it, the pool
  3947	halves with every rank while per-workgroup memory doubles. The product is
  3948	constant, which is the point.</figcaption>
  3949	</figure>
  3950	
  3951	### 10.3 The gate had the same bug
  3952	
  3953	The specializer's correctness gate dispatched a **fixed 64-workgroup pool and a
  3954	fixed 5,000 shots** regardless of rank (`6960527`). At rank ≤ 19 that is
  3955	harmless. At rank 20+ it means the gate validates on a pool tens of times
  3956	narrower than the real dispatch, while per-shot cost grows as `2^peak_rank`:
  3957	
  3958	> The gate for `qv20` had not finished after 10 minutes — longer than the
  3959	> benchmark it gates.
  3960	
  3961	Fixed by giving the gate the *same* budget formula (capped at 512 — it only
  3962	needs enough parallelism to finish promptly) and tapering the shot sample above
  3963	rank 19: 1,000 shots at rank 20–21, 250 at rank 22+. The justification is
  3964	statistical rather than budgetary — *"a divergence needs far fewer shots to
  3965	surface at high rank, since each shot touches millions of amplitudes"* — and
  3966	`V2_GATE_SHOTS` overrides it for paranoid validation. Verdicts were unchanged
  3967	everywhere they had already been computed.
  3968	
  3969	### 10.4 Finding a circuit that actually reaches rank 20
  3970	
  3971	This is the part worth reading. With the cap raised, nothing in the tree could
  3972	exercise it — and the reason is a compiler pass working correctly.
  3973	
  3974	**`StatevectorSqueezePass` defeats most "high rank" circuits.** It reorders
  3975	operations so that T-injection circuits with only local Clifford glue collapse
  3976	almost completely. From `4b9efc7`:
  3977	
  3978	> `rank19_q100_d10_wide` compiles to **peak_rank = 1**, and the `surface_d11`
  3979	> family tops out around 14.
  3980	
  3981	A fixture named for rank 19 that compiles to rank 1. This is not a bug — it is
  3982	the entire premise of the SVM, which is that near-Clifford circuits have a small
  3983	*active* rank regardless of how many qubits they name.
  3984	
  3985	**Quantum Volume circuits do not squeeze.** Each QV layer applies a random SU(4)
  3986	to a fresh random pairing of *all* qubits, so the whole register stays entangled
  3987	and `peak_rank == num_qubits` exactly. `generate_qv_circuits.py` emits them with
  3988	U3 angles in clifft's normalized convention. Verified with a host-side rank
  3989	probe running the identical compile path as `run_v2` (trace → HIR passes →
  3990	reference syndrome → lower → bytecode passes):
  3991	
  3992	```
  3993	qv20_L8 = 20    qv21_L8 = 21    qv22_L6 = 22    qv23_L5 = 23    qv24_L4 = 24
  3994	```
  3995	
  3996	Layer counts taper with width (`L8` → `L4`) to keep compile and run time
  3997	bounded.
  3998	
