  4151	
  4152	The full listing sharpens the boundary. **Rank 20–21 is the only region in the
  4153	tier where the allocator does not take the VGPR 128 / AGPR 64 cap** — three
  4154	kernels at 104–106 / 40–42, all with zero spill and the smallest scratch in the
  4155	tier (96–112 B). Every other kernel, at rank 11–14 and at rank 22–24 alike, is
  4156	at the cap. So the rank-22 break is not "the allocator hits the cap"; the
  4157	long rank-11–14 kernels are at the cap too and spill 4 SGPRs at most. It is that
  4158	rank 22 hits the cap **and** finds nothing left to overflow into.
  4159	
  4160	What actually changes at the boundary is narrower than expected. Diffing the
  4161	emitted C for rank 21 against rank 22 with all numeric literals normalized shows
  4162	**no structural difference** — same preprocessor preamble, same `spec_body`
  4163	shape, near-identical opcode mix (169/80/42 `array_u2`/`array_u4`/`array_rot` vs
  4164	142/66/44). The tier wrapper is rank-independent by construction
  4165	(`v2_specializer.cc:196-224`). The one thing that differs is a single baked
  4166	constant:
  4167	
  4168	```c
  4169	const u64 amp_capacity = 2097152ull;   // rank 21
  4170	const u64 amp_capacity = 4194304ull;   // rank 22
  4171	```
  4172	
  4173	Crossing 2²² also moves the allocator's chosen register split: the non-spilling
  4174	rank 20/21 kernels sit at VGPR 104–106 / AGPR 40–42, while every spilling kernel
  4175	jumps to the VGPR 128 / AGPR 64 cap. On CDNA the AGPR file is repurposed as
  4176	overflow storage when MFMA is unused (and `SQ_INSTS_MFMA = 0` throughout here),
  4177	so the pattern is consistent with the allocator exhausting AGPR overflow at the
  4178	cap and falling through to scratch. **That is an inference from resource
  4179	metadata, not a verified mechanism** — the emitted C is essentially unchanged, so
  4180	the cause lies inside LLVM's allocation for the wider constant, which has not
  4181	been isolated. Stated as an open question rather than an answer.
  4182	
  4183	What *is* solid: the correlation is exact. Every global kernel at rank ≤ 21
  4184	spills ≤ 4 SGPRs and wins 0.72–0.84; every one at rank ≥ 22 spills 594–762 SGPRs
  4185	to HBM-backed scratch — the same memory the kernel is already bandwidth-bound on
  4186	— and the win collapses to 0.98–0.99. So the honest conclusion holds:
  4187	**V2's specialization advantage decays above rank 21 and is essentially gone by
  4188	rank 23.** §16 lists it as the top open item; the first step is an LLVM
  4189	allocation study at the rank-21/22 boundary, not a code change.
  4190	
  4191	---
  4192	
  4193	## 11. Pitfalls
  4194	
  4195	Five things went wrong in V2 that are worth writing down. In four of them a
  4196	*plausible* explanation was adopted, documented, and built on — and in every one
  4197	of those four the plausible explanation was wrong. The pattern is uniform enough to state
  4198	up front:
  4199	
  4200	> Each bug was diagnosed by analogy ("this looks like an FP problem", "this looks
  4201	> like a fixture problem") and each was actually resolved by an experiment that
  4202	> asked the system a question it could only answer one way. The diagnosis-by-
  4203	> analogy was never merely incomplete; it pointed at the wrong subsystem.
  4204	
  4205	### 11.1 The noise regression, and the theory that was wrong
  4206	
  4207	**The symptom.** The `circuit_d5` / `cultivation_d5` family — rank 10, 1,720
  4208	instructions, the noisiest circuits in the corpus — produced measurement
  4209	outcomes from the specialized kernel that differed from the interpreter on
  4210	roughly one shot in five thousand, on some seeds.
  4211	
  4212	**The theory.** Straight-lining the bytecode duplicates each noise op's body at
  4213	every call site. Noise ops carry scalar FP: an `ocml_log_f64` hazard draw,
  4214	cumulative-probability sums, RNG. If `-O2` reassociated that FP differently
  4215	across copies, a probability could shift by ~1 ULP, `sample_branch` could take
  4216	the other side of a comparison, and — because a branch decides *whether a PRNG
  4217	draw is consumed* — the two streams would decorrelate permanently and never
  4218	resynchronize.
  4219	
  4220	This story is mechanically coherent, it names a real hazard, and it fits the
  4221	observation that only the noisiest circuit family was affected. It motivated
  4222	`V2_NOISE_ATTR` (`9d9cc68`): emit the noise ops `noinline` in the specialized
  4223	build so `-O2` cannot merge or reassociate across the copies.
  4224	
  4225	**Why it was wrong.** Two facts, either of which is sufficient:
  4226	
  4227	1. **The build cannot legally do what the theory requires.** The specializer
  4228	   compiles with `-O2 -ffp-contract=off` and no fast-math. Under those flags
  4229	   reassociation of floating-point is *not permitted*, so inlining cannot change
  4230	   an FP result. `v2_compile_cache.cc` even says so at the flag site:
  4231	
  4232	   ```
  4233	   // Flags are IDENTICAL to the build-time interpreter pipeline
  4234	   // (ClifftAmdgcn.cmake) so the shared v2_op_*() bodies compile to the same
  4235	   // code -> byte-exact. -ffp-contract=off with no fast-math is what makes that
  4236	   // hold regardless of inlining decisions: without reassociation or
  4237	   // contraction the optimizer cannot change an FP result. Do NOT add
  4238	   // vectorize/unroll/fast-math flags that would diverge from the interpreter.
  4239	   ```
  4240	
  4241	2. **The kernel disagreed with itself.** `V2_GATE_SELFTEST` (`ca82260`) runs a
  4242	   kernel against *itself*, twice, same seed, same shots, same binary. Rounding
  4243	   is deterministic for a fixed binary, so any disagreement is proof of
  4244	   nondeterminism. Result:
  4245	
  4246	   Per-seed, 5,000 shots each, `circuit_d5_p0.001` (job 50361). The counts are
  4247	   `obs0` ones from run A vs run B of the *same binary*:
  4248	
  4249	   | seed | interp vs interp | verdict | spec vs spec | verdict |
  4250	   |---|---|---|---|---|
  4251	   | 1 | 1620 / 1620 | deterministic | 1620 / **1619** | **NONDETERMINISTIC** |
  4252	   | 7 | 1724 / 1724 | deterministic | 1724 / **1723** | **NONDETERMINISTIC** |
  4253	   | 42 | 1704 / 1704 | deterministic | 1704 / 1704 | deterministic |
  4254	   | 99 | 1720 / 1720 | deterministic | 1721 / **1720** | **NONDETERMINISTIC** |
  4255	   | 123 | 1691 / 1691 | deterministic | 1690 / **1691** | **NONDETERMINISTIC** |
  4256	   | 2718 | 1681 / 1681 | deterministic | 1681 / 1681 | deterministic |
  4257	   | | **0 / 6 diverge** | | **4 / 6 diverge** | |
  4258	
  4259	   Note that `passed` was 5000/5000 in *every* cell — the shots all completed;
  4260	   only the observable flipped. The divergence is one sample in ~1,700, which is
  4261	   exactly the "one shot in five thousand" the symptom section describes, now
  4262	   attributed to the right cause. No rounding model explains a binary disagreeing
  4263	   with itself. This is a race.
  4264	
  4265	`V2_GATE_BISECT` then localized it. The instrumentation
  4266	(`v2_kernel.cc:226-247`) re-runs each shot as its own dispatch —
  4267	`run_one(..., i, i+1, ...)` — and in the coop tier one shot is exactly one
  4268	workgroup. Scanning all 5,000 shots of the worst seed emitted **not a single
  4269	`DIVERGES` line**: at one workgroup the two kernels agree on every shot. The bug
  4270	needed *concurrent* workgroups, which also rules out any single shot's
  4271	arithmetic — the same instruction stream, on the same inputs, is correct when
  4272	run alone.
  4273	
  4274	The A/B on the fix itself was the last nail: `V2_SPEC_NOISE_INLINE=1`, which
  4275	flips `V2_NOISE_ATTR` back to `always_inline`, made things **worse** — 5/6 seeds
  4276	diverging rather than 4/6, and with *larger* deltas (seed 99 went from off-by-1
  4277	to off-by-3, 1721 / 1718). That is consistent with perturbing a race's timing,
  4278	and inconsistent with the attribute being the load-bearing fix. It also shows
  4279	the trap in the original diagnosis: had the A/B not been run, the attribute
  4280	would have looked like a partial fix rather than no fix at all, because both
  4281	arms diverge on *some* seeds and the seed set is small.
  4282	
  4283	**What was kept and why.** `V2_NOISE_ATTR` stayed, but its justification was
  4284	rewritten in the source to say what is actually true:
  4285	
  4286	> The attribute is kept because it measurably helps register pressure and code
  4287	> size on large circuits, **NOT** because it is load-bearing for correctness.
  4288	
  4289	That is the honest outcome: a change introduced for a wrong reason that happens
  4290	to pay for itself for a different, measurable reason. The comment at
  4291	`v2_ops_body.inc:409-427` is labelled `HISTORICAL NOTE` and states the refutation
  4292	inline, so the next reader cannot re-derive the dead theory from the code.
  4293	
  4294	### 11.2 The real bug: a barrier that ordered execution but not memory
  4295	
  4296	`v2_barrier()` was a bare `__builtin_amdgcn_s_barrier()`.
  4297	
  4298	On AMDGCN that is an **execution** barrier only. LLVM declares the intrinsic
  4299	`IntrNoMem`, so the backend emits no `s_waitcnt lgkmcnt(0)` ahead of it and the
  4300	scheduler is free to sink or hoist LDS accesses across it. A wave can retire the
  4301	barrier with a `ds_write` still in flight while a peer wave reads the stale word.
  4302	
  4303	Measured on the pre-fix gfx950 ISA — barriers with a `ds_*` op in flight and no
  4304	intervening `s_waitcnt lgkmcnt(0)`. Every number below was re-derived for this
  4305	report by disassembling the archived binaries with `llvm-objdump` and by
  4306	rebuilding the interpreter twice from the same source with only the barrier
  4307	body swapped:
  4308	
  4309	| kernel | barriers | unfenced | how measured |
  4310	|---|---|---|---|
  4311	| interpreter (`clifft_v2_coop`) | 80 | **50 (62.5 %)** | A/B rebuild, `s_barrier` vs release/acquire, `-O2` asm |
  4312	| **specialized** (`coop_r10_n1720`) | 1,509 | **1,400 (92.8 %)** | `llvm-objdump -d` on the archived pre-fix `.hsaco` |
  4313	
  4314	**Both kernels were wrong.** The specializer has **~28× more** exposed barriers
  4315	because it straight-lines every op, which is precisely why only it failed the
  4316	correctness gate — the interpreter was equally incorrect in principle and simply
  4317	lost the race far less often.
  4318	
  4319	> **Correction.** Commit `150d09f`'s message, and the header comment in
  4320	> `V2_performance/scratch/d5_fence.sh`, both record the specialized figure as
  4321	> **1439 / 1509 (95.4 %)**. That number does not reproduce. Disassembling the
  4322	> archived pre-fix `coop_r10_n1720_977e1e830813621d.hsaco` gives **1,400** under
  4323	> every reasonable definition of "in flight" — scanning back 24, 32, 48, 64 or
  4324	> unbounded instructions to the previous barrier all converge on 1,400, and
  4325	> loosening `lgkmcnt(0)` to any `lgkmcnt` does not move it either. The only
  4326	> definitions that yield anything else are degenerate: counting barriers not
  4327	> *immediately* preceded by a wait gives 1,509, and a 3-instruction window gives
  4328	> 1,460. The interpreter figure is likewise 50 / 80, not 52 / 81 — the archived
  4329	> count was taken on a `.hsaco` linked against `ocml`/`ockl`, which contributes
  4330	> one extra barrier and one extra unfenced site from device-library code that is
  4331	> not V2's. **None of this changes the conclusion**; the corrected ratio (28×) is
  4332	> marginally *larger* than the one originally claimed.
  4333	
  4334	This also explains why the SVM and Hybrid backends never saw it: HIP's
  4335	`__syncthreads()` already expands to the fenced sequence. **V2 hand-rolled the
  4336	barrier to avoid HIP and lost the fence along with it** — a direct, and
  4337	expensive, cost of the no-HIP policy (§13).
  4338	
  4339	The fix (`150d09f`) wraps the barrier in a release/acquire pair:
  4340	
  4341	```c
  4342	// s_barrier alone is an EXECUTION barrier only: LLVM models the intrinsic as
  4343	// IntrNoMem, so it neither emits `s_waitcnt lgkmcnt(0)` nor stops the scheduler
  4344	// from sinking/hoisting LDS accesses across it. A wave can therefore retire the
  4345	// barrier with a ds_write still in flight while a peer wave reads the stale
  4346	// word. The release/acquire fence pair is what makes it a MEMORY barrier: the
  4347	// release forces the waitcnt before s_barrier, the acquire keeps later loads
  4348	// from being hoisted above it. HIP's __syncthreads() expands to exactly this,
  4349	// which is why the SVM backend never saw the race.
  4350	static inline void v2_barrier(void) {
  4351	    __builtin_amdgcn_fence(__ATOMIC_RELEASE, "workgroup");
  4352	    __builtin_amdgcn_s_barrier();
  4353	    __builtin_amdgcn_fence(__ATOMIC_ACQUIRE, "workgroup");
  4354	}
  4355	```
  4356	
  4357	`coop_reduce2`'s three raw barriers were converted too — each guards a
  4358	read-after-write on `lds_red0`/`lds_red1`, so a lost partial sum perturbs the
  4359	reduction total and flips `sample_branch`. **That is the causal path by which a
  4360	missing fence became a wrong measurement outcome**, and it is why the symptom
  4361	looked numerical: the corrupted value really was a probability.
  4362	
  4363	The cost was measured directly, by compiling `coop_interpreter.c` twice — once
  4364	with the bare `s_barrier`, once with the fence pair, same source, same flags
  4365	(`-O2 -ffp-contract=off -mcpu=gfx950`), disassembling both:
  4366	
  4367	| build | instructions | barriers | fenced | `ds_*` in flight, unfenced |
  4368	|---|---|---|---|---|
  4369	| pre-fix interpreter | 5,629 | 80 | 1 (1.2 %) | **50 (62.5 %)** |
  4370	| post-fix interpreter | 5,848 | 80 | 74 (92.5 %) | **0 (0.0 %)** |
  4371	| pre/post register tier | 4,457 | 0 | — | — |
  4372	
  4373	The A/B was re-run from scratch for this report — same source tree, the only
  4374	difference being `v2_barrier()`'s three-line body — and every cell above
  4375	reproduces exactly. The "fenced" column uses a 3-instruction lookback; at a
  4376	1-instruction lookback the post-fix figure is 73 rather than 74, which is the
  4377	one place the definition matters and it moves nothing. The register-tier row is
  4378	not an approximation: with `-DV2_REGISTER` the two `.s` files are **identical by
  4379	md5**, because `v2_barrier()` compiles to nothing there (`v2_ops.h:131`).
  4380	
  4381	Two things fall out. **The fix is complete**: the unfenced-with-`ds`-in-flight
  4382	count goes to exactly zero — the 6 barriers that remain unfenced have no LDS
  4383	write pending, so the wait is correctly elided rather than missing. And the
  4384	price is **+3.89 % static instructions** (5,629 → 5,848), matching the +3.88 %
  4385	recorded at the time. The register tier is unaffected in both directions: with
  4386	`-DV2_REGISTER` there are no barriers at all, so the two builds are
  4387	byte-identical at 4,457 instructions — **the fix costs the register tier
  4388	nothing**, which is why §9's register-tier numbers need no restatement.
  4389	
  4390	The same audit on the archived specialized binaries:
  4391	
  4392	| `.hsaco` | barriers | fenced (`lgkmcnt(0)` within 3) | `ds_*` in flight, unfenced |
  4393	|---|---|---|---|
  4394	| pre-fix `coop_r10_n1720` | 1,509 | **49 (3.2 %)** | **1,400 (92.8 %)** |
  4395	| post-fix `coop_r10_n1720` | 1,509 | **1,404 (93.0 %)** | **0 (0.0 %)** |
  4396	
  4397	Note the two columns measure different things and both are worth reporting: the
  4398	pre-fix kernel has 49 barriers that *happen* to sit behind a wait emitted for an
  4399	unrelated reason — accidentally correct, not correct by construction. Post-fix,
  4400	the in-flight column is zero, which is the property that actually matters.
  4401	
  4402	With the fence in place the gate **passes** on `coop_r10_n1720`, and the
  4403	specializer is selected for all six circuits. Kernel time, median of 5, 10,000
  4404	shots, both arms back-to-back on one node (job 50389, gfx950):
  4405	
  4406	| circuit | interpreter | specialized | speedup |
  4407	|---|---|---|---|
  4408	| circuit_d5_p0.0005 | 14.76 ms | 8.56 ms | 1.72× |
  4409	| circuit_d5_p0.001 | 14.89 ms | 8.56 ms | 1.74× |
  4410	| circuit_d5_p0.002 | 15.09 ms | 8.63 ms | 1.75× |
  4411	| circuit_d5_p0.003 | 15.30 ms | 8.68 ms | 1.76× |
  4412	| circuit_d5_p0.005 | 15.62 ms | 8.87 ms | 1.76× |
  4413	| circuit_d3_p0.001 | 0.624 ms | 0.379 ms | 1.65× |
  4414	
  4415	<figure>
  4416	<img src="diagrams/barrier-race.svg" alt="Execution-only barrier vs release/acquire fenced barrier" width="100%">
  4417	<figcaption><b>Figure 11.1</b> — Why an execution-only barrier loses a partial
  4418	sum. Wave 0 retires <code>s_barrier</code> with its <code>ds_write</code> to
  4419	<code>lds_red0</code> still in flight; wave 1 passes the same barrier and reads
  4420	the stale word. The reduction total is wrong, so <code>sample_branch</code>
  4421	compares against a corrupted probability and takes the other side — consuming a
  4422	different number of PRNG draws from that shot onward.</figcaption>
  4423	</figure>
  4424	
  4425	### 11.3 The threshold that was calibrated for the wrong precision
  4426	
  4427	Covered in full in §12, but it belongs in this list because it has the same
  4428	shape: `V2_DUST_EPS` was `1e-18`, a sensible clamp for f64 amplitudes, compared
  4429	against **f32** amplitudes. The clamp never fired on the GPU where it fired on
  4430	the CPU; `sample_branch` returns *without drawing a PRNG value* when a branch is
  4431	dust, so one side consumed a random number the other did not, and the streams
  4432	desynchronized permanently.
  4433	
  4434	The lesson is not "f32 is imprecise." It is that **a constant outlived the
  4435	representation it was chosen for**, and nothing in the type system noticed.
  4436	
  4437	### 11.4 The pitfall that nearly reached this report: a stale specialization cache
  4438	
  4439	This one was found while writing this chapter, and it is the most instructive of
  4440	the four because it corrupted *the measurements themselves* rather than the code.
  4441	
  4442	**The mechanism.** `compile_specialized()` computes a content hash to decide
  4443	whether a cached `.hsaco` can be reused:
  4444	
  4445	```cpp
  4446	// before
  4447	std::string ident = csrc + "|" + llvm_bin("clang") + "|" + arch() + "|" + bitcode_dir();
  4448	size_t h = std::hash<std::string>{}(ident);
  4449	```
  4450	
  4451	`csrc` is the generated C. But the generated C is only *half* the translation
  4452	unit — it is a list of `v2_op_*(...)` calls plus `#include "v2_ops.h"`. Every op
  4453	body, `v2_barrier()`, and every tunable constant lives in the headers, and **the
  4454	headers are not in the hash.** Change `v2_ops.h`, and the key does not move: the
  4455	day-old `.hsaco` is reused, *and so is its `<hsaco>.gate` verdict*, which is
  4456	keyed off the same path.
  4457	
  4458	**How far it got.** The benchmark run submitted specifically to guarantee that
  4459	"every performance claim in the upcoming report reflects shipped, correct code"
  4460	dispatched pre-fix kernels for every specialized circuit. Verified on the
  4461	binaries with `llvm-objdump`, on two independent markers:
  4462	
  4463	| marker | build cache (what the run used) | fresh cache |
  4464	|---|---|---|
  4465	| barriers with `ds_*` in flight and no `lgkmcnt(0)` (the `150d09f` fence) | **1400 / 1509 (92.8 %)** | **0 / 1509 (0.0 %)** |
  4466	| `s_barrier` preceded by `lgkmcnt(0)` within 3 instructions | **49 / 1509 (3.2 %)** | 1404 / 1509 (93.0 %) |
  4467	| `V2_DUST_EPS` constant baked into the binary | **`0x3c32725d…` = `1e-18`** (pre-`2a015fd`) | `0x3da5fd7f…` = `1e-11` (fixed) |
  4468	
  4469	The dust marker is the cleaner of the two because it is a bit pattern, not a
  4470	heuristic: the IEEE-754 double `1e-18` has high word `0x3c32725d` and `1e-11`
  4471	has `0x3da5fd7f`, and each appears exactly twice in its respective binary and
  4472	zero times in the other. There is no interpretation involved — the stale kernel
  4473	provably contains the pre-fix constant.
  4474	
  4475	Both markers agree with the timestamps: 32 of the 36 cached kernels are dated
  4476	2026-07-25 and the remaining 4 are from 07-26 00:00–01:48 — all of them before
  4477	the barrier fix at 07-26 06:33, and well before the dust fix at 07-26 13:31.
  4478	Every kernel the run dispatched predates both fixes.
  4479	
  4480	**The consequence for the in-tree conclusions.** The stale run recorded the
  4481	`coop_r10_n1720` gate as *failing*, which is a verdict on the **pre-fence**
  4482	binary — and the ledger built an entire section around "the specializer is still
  4483	incorrect on this shape," listing it as *"the single largest open correctness
  4484	item in V2."* It was already fixed. The claim survived only because the cache
  4485	kept handing back the binary from before the fix.
  4486	
  4487	**The near-miss that makes this worth reporting.** The hazard was *known*.
  4488	`d5_fence.sh` documents it exactly:
  4489	
  4490	> The `.gate` verdict is keyed on the generated-C hash, which does **NOT** change
  4491	> when `v2_ops.h` changes — a stale "0" would hide the fix. Each arm uses a fresh
  4492	> `V2_SPEC_CACHE_DIR` so every verdict below is recomputed from scratch.
  4493	
  4494	Every `d5_*.sh` diagnostic script therefore sets its own `V2_SPEC_CACHE_DIR` and
  4495	got correct results. **The workaround was applied per-script instead of fixing
  4496	the cache**, so the one path that did *not* set the variable — the build tree's
  4497	default cache, used by the benchmark sweep — silently kept serving stale
  4498	kernels. A known bug, correctly documented, routed around rather than fixed, in
  4499	the one place it mattered most.
  4500	

