  4518	### 11.5 The same bug, in the build system, three minutes later
  4519	
  4520	The specializer's runtime cache was not the only thing keyed on the wrong
  4521	inputs. `ClifftAmdgcn.cmake`'s `add_custom_command` for each `.hsaco` listed
  4522	`DEPENDS "${_src}"` — the `.c` file alone. CMake does no implicit header
  4523	scanning for custom commands, so editing `v2_ops.h` did not invalidate the
  4524	output and an incremental build kept linking the previously-compiled kernel.
  4525	
  4526	This matters because of what those `.c` files are. `coop_interpreter.c` is
  4527	~150 lines wrapping the entire op library; **the headers *are* the kernel**. The
  4528	commit that fixed it (`72aee12`, 07-26 06:36) says where it was caught:
  4529	
  4530	> This bit during the barrier-fence fix: the first verification job would have
  4531	> measured the pre-fix kernel and reported the fix as ineffective.
  4532	
  4533	Note the timestamps. The barrier fix landed at 06:33 and this landed at
  4534	**06:36** — the staleness was hit immediately, on the very first attempt to
  4535	measure the fix, and caught within three minutes. The specializer's cache
  4536	(§11.4) had the identical defect and was not fixed until **22:14 the same day**,
  4537	sixteen hours later, after it had already contaminated a full benchmark sweep.
  4538	
  4539	Two caches, one failure mode — *the identity of a compiled artifact omitted the
  4540	headers that define it*. The difference in how long each survived is entirely
  4541	explained by how loudly it failed. The build-system instance broke a
  4542	verification the author was actively watching; the runtime instance quietly
  4543	returned plausible numbers.
  4544	
  4545	The fix globs the device headers from the source's own directory and adds them
  4546	to `DEPENDS`:
  4547	
  4548	```cmake
  4549	get_filename_component(_src_dir "${_src}" DIRECTORY)
  4550	file(GLOB _dev_hdrs "${_src_dir}/*.h" "${_src_dir}/*.inc")
  4551	...
  4552	DEPENDS "${_src}" ${_dev_hdrs}
  4553	```
  4554	
  4555	A glob in `DEPENDS` is evaluated at configure time, so a *newly added* header
  4556	still needs a re-configure to be tracked — an acceptable residual, since the
  4557	three headers that matter already exist and the failure mode it removes was the
  4558	one actually observed. The commit also records the scope constraint explicitly:
  4559	`ClifftAmdgcn.cmake` is included only inside the `CLIFFT_ENABLE_MLIR_V2` block,
  4560	so **no SVM or hybrid build path is touched** (§5).
  4561	
  4562	### 11.6 The common thread
  4563	
  4564	| # | plausible story | what it actually was | what settled it |
  4565	|---|---|---|---|
  4566	| 11.1 | `-O2` reassociates FP across inlined noise ops | a data race between workgroups | kernel disagreed **with itself** (`V2_GATE_SELFTEST`) |
  4567	| 11.2 | (see above) | `s_barrier` orders execution, not memory | ISA audit: 92.8 % of barriers unfenced |
  4568	| 11.3 | f32 is less precise than f64 | a threshold constant calibrated for f64, compared against f32 | two-arm A/B on the constant |
  4569	| 11.4 | the benchmark measures current code | the cache served day-old binaries | `llvm-objdump` on the dispatched `.hsaco` |
  4570	| 11.5 | an incremental build rebuilds what changed | `DEPENDS` omitted the headers that *are* the kernel | the fence fix measured as ineffective |
  4571	
  4572	Three of the five were settled by an experiment whose outcome the wrong theory
  4573	*could not* produce — a self-comparison, a static audit, a controlled A/B. None
  4574	were settled by reasoning harder about the plausible story. The fourth was
  4575	caught only because this report's ground rule (*trust data, not text*) required
  4576	re-deriving an in-tree claim from the artifact instead of quoting it. The fifth
  4577	was caught for free, by being loud.
  4578	
  4579	**A sixth, smaller instance belongs here, because it happened while writing this
  4580	chapter.** The 95.4 % figure above was quoted from `150d09f`'s commit message
  4581	and from a comment in the script that produced it. Re-running the measurement on
  4582	the archived binary gave 92.8 %, under every definition tried. The original
  4583	number is not recoverable and the script no longer carries the exact counting
  4584	code that produced it — the audit trail ends at prose. This is precisely the
  4585	failure mode the ground rule exists for, and it is worth noting that it caught a
  4586	number in a *correct* section, written by someone who had the artifact in hand at
  4587	the time. The conclusion did not change; a number that had been repeated three
  4588	times did.
  4589	
  4590	---
  4591	
  4592	## 12. The gap: f32 vs f64, and how it was narrowed
  4593	
  4594	The GPU carries amplitudes in f32; the CPU reference carries them in f64. That
  4595	much is by design, and it is stated in the device ABI:
  4596	
  4597	```c
  4598	// ---- Complex amplitude (f32 storage; f64 used only in reductions) ----------
  4599	typedef struct __attribute__((aligned(8))) { float re; float im; } CV2Complex;
  4600	```
  4601	
  4602	against `std::complex<double>` throughout `src/clifft/svm/svm.h`. What the
  4603	in-tree documentation asserted — `docs/v2/pre_V2.md:66`, *"f32 vs f64
  4604	accumulation differs across backends"* — is prose. This section replaces it with
  4605	an experiment, and the result is more specific and more actionable than the
  4606	prose suggests.
  4607	
  4608	### 12.1 Why a precision difference becomes a *correctness* difference
  4609	
  4610	The mechanism is not that the answers are slightly different. It is that the two
  4611	backends can consume **different numbers of random numbers**.
  4612	
  4613	`sample_branch` is the hinge:
  4614	
  4615	```c
  4616	// The clamp decision must match the SVM's on every call: a branch clamped on
  4617	// one side and rolled on the other consumes a PRNG draw the other never did,
  4618	// and the two streams never resynchronize. See V2_DUST_EPS.
  4619	static inline u8 sample_branch(u64* rng, double p0, double p1, double total) {
  4620	    double eps = V2_DUST_EPS * total;
  4621	    if (p1 <= eps) return 0;          // <-- returns WITHOUT drawing
  4622	    if (p0 <= eps) return 1;          // <-- returns WITHOUT drawing
  4623	    return (rng_uniform(rng) * total < p0) ? 0u : 1u;
  4624	}
  4625	```
  4626	
  4627	Both early returns skip `rng_uniform`. So if one backend clamps and the other
  4628	does not, the clamping side is one draw behind forever. The PRNG is
  4629	xoshiro256++: a shifted stream is not *approximately* right, it is a different
  4630	sequence. Every subsequent measurement in that shot decorrelates.
  4631	
  4632	This yields two distinct failure modes, and separating them is the whole story
  4633	of this section:
  4634	
  4635	1. **Branch-probability rounding.** Probabilities are reduced in f64 but from
  4636	   f32 *inputs*, so `p0/total` is good to only ~1e-6 relative after summing
  4637	   `2^k` terms. When `rand*total` lands within that window of `p0`, the two
  4638	   sides take different branches. Prediction: divergence should scale with
  4639	   amplitudes summed — invisible at register rank, visible at coop rank 10.
  4640	2. **A mis-calibrated clamp threshold.** Independent of rounding, and — as it
  4641	   turned out — the dominant effect.
  4642	
  4643	<figure>
  4644	<img src="diagrams/prng-desync.svg" alt="How one skipped PRNG draw desynchronizes two backends permanently" width="100%">
  4645	<figcaption><b>Figure 12.1</b> — The desynchronization mechanism. A single
  4646	clamp taken on one side and not the other shifts the entire remaining draw
  4647	sequence for that shot. The outcome of the clamped branch is <em>identical</em>
  4648	on both sides; it is the bookkeeping that diverges.</figcaption>
  4649	</figure>
  4650	
  4651	### 12.2 The dominant cause: a constant that outlived its precision
  4652	
  4653	`V2_DUST_EPS` was copied verbatim from the SVM's `kDustEpsilon = 1e-18`
  4654	(`svm_internal.h:46`). That value is calibrated for `std::complex<double>`, whose
  4655	analytically-zero interference lands at 1e-30…1e-24. V2 stores f32, where the
  4656	same interference concentrates at `fp32_eps² = 1.4e-14` — **four decades above
  4657	the threshold**. On the GPU the dust branch therefore *never clamped*.
  4658	
  4659	The reference case is a four-line circuit (`H; T^4; H; M`, the one
  4660	`tests/test_svm.cc:1995` pins):
  4661	
  4662	| precision | p0 | vs `1e-18` | behaviour |
  4663	|---|---|---|---|
  4664	| fp64 | 2.465e-32 | ≤ eps | **clamp**, no draw |
  4665	| fp32 | 8.882e-16 | > eps | **no clamp**, draw consumed |
  4666	
  4667	Both sides still pick the same *outcome* — `p0/total` is `1 - 1e-15`, so any
  4668	uniform draw lands the same way — but the GPU has consumed a draw the CPU has
  4669	not. In a surface-code circuit nearly every stabilizer measurement is
  4670	deterministic, so **this fires constantly**.
  4671	
  4672	As the commit puts it: *"A threshold that never fires is a correctness bug, not
  4673	dead code."*
  4674	
  4675	**Sizing the replacement.** The new value is not a guess. A branch probability is
  4676	a sum of `half = 1 << (active_k - 1)` squared fp32 magnitudes, and the rounding
  4677	error is *relative to each amplitude* — so the dust floor does **not** grow with
  4678	term count; summing more terms averages the residuals rather than accumulating
  4679	them. Measured with `V2_performance/tools/dust_floor.py` (`p1/total`, 2,000
  4680	trials per rank):
  4681	
  4682	| rank | median | max |
  4683	|---|---|---|
  4684	| 1 | 9.7e-15 | **1.2e-13** |
  4685	| 4 | 1.3e-14 | 5.8e-14 |
  4686	| 12 | 1.42e-14 | 1.6e-14 |
  4687	| 26 | 1.42e-14 | 1.5e-14 |
  4688	
  4689	The distribution concentrates on `fp32_eps² = 1.42e-14` and **tightens** with
  4690	rank; the widest tail is at *low* rank (~1.2e-13). One constant therefore covers
  4691	ranks 1–26. `1e-11` sits ~2 decades above that tail and ~5 decades below the
  4692	smallest probability f32 can meaningfully carry, leaving margin on both sides.
  4693	Nothing real is clamped away: genuine small probabilities (the SVM comment cites
  4694	`R_ZZ` angles producing ~1e-16) are not representable in f32 storage in the
  4695	first place.
  4696	
  4697	> **Provenance.** These four rows were originally recorded only as prose, in the
  4698	> `V2_DUST_EPS` comment — the generating code was never committed, the same gap
  4699	> that produced the incorrect barrier figure in §11.2. Re-deriving it turned up
  4700	> a detail the prose elides. "Residual model" is literal: it is a *statistical*
  4701	> model, not a simulation of the kernel's arithmetic. It assumes each
  4702	> analytically-zero output carries a relative error at full `eps`
  4703	> (`resid_i = a_i · eps · z_i`, `z_i` unit-variance complex Gaussian), giving
  4704	> `p1/total = eps² · Σ|a_i|²|z_i|² / Σ|a_i|²` — `eps²` times a weighted mean of
  4705	> `Exp(1)` variables. Re-running that model reproduces the table to the digit
  4706	> (9.730e-15 / 1.222e-13, 1.315e-14 / 5.048e-14, 1.422e-14 / 1.586e-14,
  4707	> 1.422e-14 / 1.476e-14). Both the model and the four values are now committed
  4708	> as `dust_floor.py --model residual`, its default.
  4709	>
  4710	> Directly simulating the arithmetic instead — `fl(fl(u·a) + fl(v·b))` over
  4711	> fp32-stored amplitudes whose exact fp64 counterparts cancel identically, via
  4712	> `--model butterfly` — puts the real floor **~48× lower**: median 2.9e-16 at
  4713	> rank 26, 2.7e-16 at rank 12, and a worst tail of 2.7e-15 at rank 1 (2.9e-15
  4714	> at 20,000 trials — a max is an order statistic and grows with the trial
  4715	> count, so it is only comparable across rows at equal `--trials`). At rank 1
  4716	> the median is exactly **zero**: with a single term there is nothing to cancel
  4717	> against, so the fp32 product is either exact or it is not. The residual model
  4718	> is the conservative envelope, because it assumes every term rounds at full
  4719	> `eps` and that the errors never cancel against one another.
  4720	>
  4721	> This does not disturb the choice of `1e-11`, and it is worth being precise
  4722	> about why. Every structural fact the threshold rests on holds under *both*
  4723	> estimators: the floor is rank-independent, the spread tightens with rank so
  4724	> the widest tail is at low rank, and the floor is insensitive to circuit depth
  4725	> (`--depth 256` moves the medians by <5 %, because accumulated error is itself
  4726	> relative and divides out of the ratio). The two models disagree only on the
  4727	> absolute location, and `1e-11` clears the *conservative* one by two decades —
  4728	> so it clears the simulated one by nearly four. What the correction changes is
  4729	> the margin, which is larger than claimed, not the decision.
  4730	>
  4731	> Both tables above were regenerated for this report from the committed tool,
  4732	> whose RNG is seeded `1234 + rank` and so reproduces to the digit:
  4733	> `dust_floor.py --model residual` and `--model butterfly`, defaults otherwise.
  4734	
  4735	This is worth pausing on, because it inverts the intuition the "f32 vs f64"
  4736	framing invites. The dust floor is **rank-independent**, and the *low*-rank
  4737	circuits have the widest spread. A threshold picked for the largest circuit would
  4738	have been wrong for the smallest.
  4739	
  4740	### 12.3 The A/B that confirmed it
  4741	
  4742	Two arms, identical in every respect but the constant (job 50444, node
  4743	`smci350-rck-g03-f13-21`):
  4744	
  4745	| arm | `V2_DUST_EPS` | d5 (coop, rank 10) shot 0 | d3 (register, rank 4) shot 0 |
  4746	|---|---|---|---|
  4747	| A | `1e-18` (fp64-calibrated) | gpu=[0] cpu=[1] **MISMATCH** | gpu=[0] cpu=[0] ok |
  4748	| B | `1e-11` (fp32-calibrated) | gpu=[1] cpu=[1] **match** | gpu=[0] cpu=[0] ok |
  4749	
  4750	The rank-dependence prediction from §12.1 holds exactly: the register-tier
  4751	circuit agreed under *both* thresholds, and the coop-tier circuit disagreed only
  4752	under the fp64-calibrated one. Fixed in `2a015fd` — **a constant-only change**;
  4753	all four `sample_branch` call sites are untouched.
  4754	
  4755	### 12.4 Post-fix verification: separating logic from statistics
  4756	
  4757	The mistake available here is to check aggregate counts, see them differ by a
  4758	percent or two, and conclude the backend is still wrong. `d5_verify.sh`
  4759	(job 50453) therefore asks two different questions.
  4760	
  4761	**Q1 — same-stream, shot-for-shot. Must be EXACT.** Twelve seeds × three
  4762	circuits, comparing the GPU seed against the CPU seed that reproduces the
  4763	identical 256-bit xoshiro state:
  4764	
  4765	> **Q1: 36/36 exact.**
  4766	
  4767	Every shot matches. This is the *logical* claim, and it is binary: either the
  4768	two backends compute the same thing or they do not.
  4769	
  4770	**Q2 — statistical convergence.** Two *independent* streams sampling the same
  4771	distribution should converge as `1/√shots`:
  4772	
  4773	| circuit | shots | cpu | gpu | rel gap | z |
  4774	|---|---|---|---|---|---|
  4775	| circuit_d5_p0.001 | 2,000 | 709 | 661 | 6.77 % | −1.60 |
  4776	| | 10,000 | 3,395 | 3,364 | 0.913 % | −0.46 |
  4777	| | 50,000 | 17,005 | 16,862 | 0.841 % | −0.96 |
  4778	| | 200,000 | 67,376 | 66,968 | 0.606 % | −1.37 |
  4779	| circuit_d3_p0.001 | 2,000 | 52 | 63 | 21.15 % | 1.04 |
  4780	| | 10,000 | 250 | 270 | 8.00 % | 0.89 |
  4781	| | 50,000 | 1,225 | 1,224 | 0.082 % | −0.02 |
  4782	| | 200,000 | 4,811 | 4,922 | 2.31 % | 1.14 |
  4783	
  4784	**Every |z| < 1.7.** The residual gap is sampling noise between two independent
  4785	streams, not a numerical defect — and Q1 is what proves that: since
  4786	shot-for-shot agreement is exact, the aggregate difference *can only be* the
  4787	difference between two valid samples of the same distribution.
  4788	
  4789	Note the `circuit_d3` row at 200,000 shots: the relative gap goes *up* (0.082 %
  4790	→ 2.31 %) while `|z|` stays near 1. Reading the percentage alone would suggest a
  4791	regression at scale. The z-score says otherwise, and the z-score is the right
  4792	statistic — the raw counts are small (≈4,800 of 200,000), so the relative gap is
  4793	dominated by Poisson noise.
  4794	
  4795	<figure>
  4796	<img src="diagrams/q2-convergence.svg" alt="Statistical convergence of GPU vs CPU observable counts" width="100%">
  4797	<figcaption><b>Figure 12.2</b> — Q2 convergence. Relative gap shrinks roughly as
  4798	1/√shots while every z-score stays inside ±1.7. The two backends are sampling
  4799	the same distribution with different streams.</figcaption>
  4800	</figure>
  4801	
  4802	### 12.5 What "narrowing the gap" actually meant
  4803	
  4804	The framing this section inherited — f32 vs f64 — is not what was fixed, and
  4805	saying so plainly matters more than preserving the tidier story:
  4806	
  4807	- The gap was **not** about precision in the *arithmetic*. No accumulation order
  4808	  was changed, no reduction was promoted to f64, no amplitude was widened. §8.6
  4809	  makes the same point from the codegen side: V1's 23× larger f64 instruction
  4810	  count is 68 % inlined `log()` polynomial, and once that is factored out both
  4811	  backends widen to f64 in exactly the same three places, deliberately, to stay
  4812	  byte-exact with the interpreter.
  4813	- It was about a **threshold constant calibrated for one precision and left in
  4814	  place when the storage format changed** — whose effect was to make one side
  4815	  consume a random number the other side did not.
  4816	- The fix is one token. The diagnosis was the entire cost.
  4817	
  4818	And the residual, genuine f32-vs-f64 effect predicted in §12.1 — branch
  4819	probabilities good to only ~1e-6 relative — is real but is *not* what the d5
  4820	divergence was. It remains a bounded source of rare divergence at high rank, and
  4821	nothing in this corpus has yet isolated an instance of it. Stated as an open
  4822	item rather than a solved one (§16).
  4823	
  4824	---
  4825	
  4826	## 13. Removing HIP, introducing HSA
  4827	
  4828	V2's dispatch policy is stated as an absolute: **avoid HIP at all cost; HSA-based
  4829	dispatch and runtime management.** This section measures what that is worth,
  4830	corrects the in-tree justification for it by a factor of ~44, bounds the benefit
  4831	honestly against V2's actual workloads, and prices the bill the policy has
  4832	already come due for.
  4833	
  4834	The short version, stated up front so the numbers below are read correctly:
  4835	
  4836	- HSA's persistent path beats HIP's synchronized launch by **1.74×** (4,671 ns).
  4837	- The pre-existing naive HSA path was **31× worse than either** — the single
  4838	  largest dispatch finding here, and the one the in-tree comments got most wrong.
  4839	- But V2 issues **one dispatch per run**, so at the median circuit that 1.74×
  4840	  is worth **0.03 % of kernel time**. It is decisive only in the short tail.
  4841	- The policy's real cost was a correctness bug (§11.2), not a performance one.
  4842	
  4843	### 13.1 What HIP actually does per launch
  4844	
  4845	A HIP kernel launch is not a doorbell write. `hipLaunchKernelGGL` enters the
  4846	CLR runtime, which validates arguments, resolves the kernel from a module,
  4847	packs the kernarg segment into a runtime-managed pool, builds an AQL packet,
  4848	and enqueues it on a stream — and `hipStreamSynchronize` then re-enters the
  4849	runtime to wait. Underneath, all of it becomes the same AQL packet on the same
  4850	hardware queue that HSA exposes directly.
  4851	
  4852	The question is only what the abstraction costs. Every prior answer in this tree
  4853	predates the current hardware and none of them compares the two runtimes.
  4854	`hsa_persistent_dispatch.h` documents per-op costs (`alloc_kernarg ~800 ns`,
  4855	`allow_gpu_access ~1200 ns`, `signal_create ~600 ns`, "~3.3 µs saved of ~4.5 µs
  4856	total") under the label *"measured on MI300X"* — a different chip, a different
  4857	ROCm, and no reproducible artifact in the tree. There is no HIP-side number at
  4858	all. So the policy that shapes V2's entire host path rested on an unverified
  4859	comparison that was never actually run.
  4860	
  4861	### 13.2 The experiment
  4862	
  4863	An empty amdgcn kernel — the smallest thing that can be dispatched — built with
  4864	the **exact** flags the V2 specializer uses, so the code object is
  4865	representative:
  4866	
  4867	```c
  4868	// empty_kernel.c — the smallest possible amdgcn kernel.
  4869	//
  4870	// The point of an empty kernel is that its GPU execution time is a floor
  4871	// (a few hundred ns of wave launch + s_endpgm), so end-to-end launch-to-
  4872	// completion latency measured on the host is DOMINATED by the runtime's
  4873	// dispatch path. That is exactly the quantity we want to attribute to
  4874	// HIP vs raw HSA.
  4875	__attribute__((visibility("default")))
  4876	__attribute__((amdgpu_kernel))
  4877	void bench_empty(unsigned char* out, u64 a, u64 b, u64 c, u64 d) {
  4878	    u32 t = __builtin_amdgcn_workitem_id_x();
  4879	    if (t == 0u) out[0] = (unsigned char)(a ^ b ^ c ^ d);
  4880	}
  4881	```
  4882	
  4883	It takes five arguments and writes one byte so the compiler cannot delete the
  4884	body and the kernarg segment is non-trivially sized. The benchmark reads the
  4885	segment size back out of the loaded code object rather than assuming it, and
  4886	prints it in the run header — `kernarg_seg=40` — so the packet being timed is
  4887	demonstrably carrying real arguments.
  4888	
  4889	Compiled with the specializer's flags verbatim (`run_bench.sh:28-29`):
  4890	
  4891	```sh
  4892	clang --target=amdgcn-amd-amdhsa -mcpu=gfx950 -ffreestanding -nostdlib \
  4893	      -nogpulib -std=c23 -O2 -ffp-contract=off -c -emit-llvm -o bench_empty.bc
  4894	```
  4895	
  4896	Six modes, three per runtime, all measuring **launch-to-completion wall latency
  4897	per dispatch** — the same quantity on both sides, which is what makes them
  4898	comparable:
  4899	
  4900	| runtime | mode | what it does |
  4901	|---|---|---|
  4902	| HSA | `naive` | alloc kernarg + `agents_allow_access` + `signal_create` per dispatch, destroy + free after — what `hsa_dispatch_and_wait` did before `PersistentDispatcher` |
  4903	| HSA | `persistent` | kernarg + signal allocated once; packet written per dispatch; signal reset with `store_relaxed` — **V2's hot path** |
  4904	| HSA | `batched16` | 16 packets published back-to-back, doorbell rung once, wait once on the last |
  4905	| HIP | `sync` | `<<<>>>` + `hipDeviceSynchronize` |
  4906	| HIP | `stream_sync` | `<<<>>>` + `hipStreamSynchronize` |
  4907	| HIP | `launch_only` | `<<<>>>` only, no sync — HIP's *asynchronous enqueue floor* |
  4908	
  4909	Both runtimes in **one SLURM job on one node** (50507, `smci350-rck-g03-d13-21`,
  4910	gfx950, ROCm 7.2.3), 2,000 iterations × 5 reps. The partition is heterogeneous,
  4911	so measuring the two arms on different nodes would have been worthless.
  4912	
  4913	### 13.3 The result
  4914	
  4915	ns per dispatch, all five reps:
  4916	
  4917	| mode | rep 0 | rep 1 | rep 2 | rep 3 | rep 4 | steady state |
  4918	|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
  4919	| HSA `naive` | 198,920 | 201,622 | 203,587 | 193,931 | 191,615 | **197,935** |
  4920	| HSA `persistent` | 6,333 | 6,329 | 6,323 | 6,325 | 6,320 | **6,326** |
  4921	| HSA `batched16` | 2,326 | 2,318 | 2,318 | 2,318 | 2,318 | **2,320** |
  4922	| HIP `sync` | 13,668 | 10,739 | 10,731 | 10,745 | 10,734 | **10,737** |
  4923	| HIP `stream_sync` | 10,993 | 11,002 | 10,992 | 11,000 | 10,997 | **10,997** |
  4924	| HIP `launch_only` | 2,106 | 2,088 | 2,090 | 2,085 | 2,095 | **2,093** |
  4925	
  4926	Reproducibility is strong in every mode that matters. Across reps 1–4 the
  4927	peak-to-peak spread is **0.15 % for `persistent`, 0.02 % for `batched16`,
  4928	0.13 % for HIP `sync` and 0.09 % for `stream_sync`** — the two numbers the
  4929	headline rests on are stable to two parts in a thousand. HIP `sync` rep 0
  4930	carries a visible first-touch cost (13,668 → 10,739), which is why it is
  4931	excluded from its steady-state column.
  4932	
  4933	Two modes are noisier and should be read with that in mind: `launch_only`
  4934	spreads 0.44 % and `naive` spreads **6 %** (191.6–203.6 µs). The naive path's
  4935	variance is unsurprising — it makes two KFD ioctls and a page-table update per
  4936	dispatch, so it inherits kernel-side scheduling jitter. It doesn't matter for
  4937	any conclusion drawn here, because the effect being measured is 31×.
  4938	
  4939	**The headline comparison** — same kernel, same node, same completion semantics:
  4940	
  4941	| | ns/dispatch | vs HIP `stream_sync` |
  4942	|---|---|---|
  4943	| HIP `stream_sync` | 10,997 | 1.00× |
  4944	| **HSA `persistent`** (V2's hot path) | **6,326** | **1.74× faster** (−4,671 ns) |
  4945	| **HSA `batched16`** (not yet used) | **2,320** | **4.74× faster** (−8,677 ns) |
  4946	
  4947	<figure>
  4948	<img src="diagrams/dispatch-latency.svg" alt="Per-dispatch latency, HSA vs HIP, six modes" width="100%">
  4949	<figcaption><b>Figure 13.1</b> — Launch-to-completion latency per dispatch on
  4950	gfx950 (job 50507). Log scale: the naive HSA path is 31× worse than V2's
  4951	persistent path, and batched HSA <em>with</em> a completion wait lands within
  4952	11 % of what HIP charges for an <em>unsynchronized</em> enqueue.</figcaption>
  4953	</figure>
  4954	
  4955	### 13.4 Reading the result honestly
  4956	
  4957	Three observations, and the third is the one that matters most:
  4958	
  4959	**1. HSA is worth 1.74× on the synchronized path.** 6,326 vs 10,997 ns. That
  4960	4,671 ns per dispatch is pure runtime abstraction — identical kernel, identical
  4961	hardware queue, identical completion semantics, differing only in who builds the
  4962	AQL packet.
  4963	
  4964	**2. Batching is worth more than the runtime choice.** `batched16` at 2,320 ns
  4965	is 2.73× better than `persistent` — a larger factor than HSA-vs-HIP's 1.74×.
  4966	Amortizing the doorbell and the completion wait across 16 packets beats any
  4967	per-dispatch micro-optimization available on either runtime.
  4968	
  4969	The useful comparison is against **HIP's `launch_only` floor of 2,093 ns**, the
  4970	cost of enqueueing without ever waiting. Batched HSA reaches 2,320 ns *while
  4971	still paying for a completion wait* — 11 % above HIP's fire-and-forget cost, for
  4972	strictly stronger semantics. Put the other way: HIP charges nearly as much to
  4973	merely hand a packet to a stream as batched HSA charges to dispatch it and
  4974	observe it finish.
  4975	
  4976	**3. The in-tree numbers this replaces were wrong by a factor of ~44.**
  4977	`hsa_persistent_dispatch.h:36-42` carries a cost model — labelled *"measured on
  4978	MI300X"* — that itemizes exactly the operations the `naive` mode performs:
  4979	
  4980	```
  4981	/// Eliminated per-dispatch costs (measured on MI300X):
  4982	///   - alloc_kernarg:      ~800ns  (pool allocator + bookkeeping)
  4983	///   - allow_gpu_access:   ~1200ns (kernel call into KFD for page table update)
  4984	///   - signal_create:      ~600ns  (KFD ioctl for doorbell-backed signal)
  4985	///   - signal_destroy:     ~400ns  (KFD ioctl)
  4986	///   - free_kernarg:       ~300ns  (pool return + bookkeeping)
  4987	///   Total saved:          ~3.3us per dispatch (of ~4.5us total overhead)
  4988	```
  4989	
  4990	The `naive` mode is a faithful reimplementation of that op sequence, so the two
  4991	are directly comparable:
  4992	
  4993	| claim | asserted | measured | error |
  4994	|---|---|---|---|
  4995	| naive per-dispatch cost | ~4.5 µs total | **~198 µs** | 44× understated |
  4996	| saving from persistent resources | ~3.3 µs | **~192 µs** (198,000 − 6,326) | 58× understated |
  4997	
  4998	One precision note in the header's favour: its "~4.5 µs" is labelled *overhead*,
  4999	while 198 µs is total launch-to-completion latency. But the two are separated by
  5000	only the 6,326 ns `persistent` floor — subtract it and the eliminated cost is

