 succeeded in 153ms:
  5001	still ~192 µs against an asserted ~3.3 µs. The gap is not an accounting artifact.
  5002	
  5003	The *direction* was right and the *magnitude* was badly wrong. Per-dispatch
  5004	`hsa_amd_memory_pool_allocate` + `hsa_amd_agents_allow_access` +
  5005	`hsa_signal_create` costs ~198 µs, not ~4.5 µs — `agents_allow_access` in
  5006	particular is a KFD call that updates GPU page tables, and paying that per
  5007	launch dwarfs everything else in the system. This is the strongest single
  5008	argument for `PersistentDispatcher`, and nobody had actually measured it.
  5009	
  5010	It also reframes the priority order. Against a ~10 µs HIP dispatch, a
  5011	millisecond-scale kernel is dominated by compute. Against a ~198 µs naive
  5012	dispatch, dispatch is a first-order cost for anything short — which is exactly
  5013	the regime the v1-era `PERFORMANCE_OPTIONS.md` was written in, and why "host
  5014	dispatch overhead" was labelled the dominant bottleneck there.
  5015	
  5016	### 13.4a How much of this does V2 actually collect?
  5017	
  5018	Honesty requires answering the obvious follow-up: V2 does **one dispatch per
  5019	run**, not one per shot. The shot loop is inside the kernel (§4's R5 principle),
  5020	and `n_dispatches` is `1` for all 26 tier-5+ circuits in the corpus. So the
  5021	4,671 ns saving is paid once, and its weight is 4,671 ns divided by the whole
  5022	kernel time:
  5023	
  5024	Measured on the canonical run (job 50793), whose `n_dispatches` is **1 for all
  5025	26 circuits** — so this is exact, not an assumption:
  5026	
  5027	| circuit | tier | V2 kernel | 4,671 ns as % of it |
  5028	|---|---|---:|---:|
  5029	| `frame_h` | register | 12.1 µs | **38.7 %** |
  5030	| `four_t` | register | 13.1 µs | **35.7 %** |
  5031	| `circuit_d3_p0.001` | coop | 220.7 µs | 2.12 % |
  5032	| `qv10` | coop | 1.386 ms | 0.337 % |
  5033	| `circuit_d5_p0.001` | coop | 8.600 ms | 0.054 % |
  5034	| `cultivation_d5` | coop | 16.42 ms | 0.028 % |
  5035	| `qv24_L4_seed42` | global | 7.242 s | 0.00006 % |
  5036	
  5037	> **An earlier version of this table was stale in a way worth recording.** It
  5038	> quoted `circuit_d5_p0.001` at 14.76 ms and `cultivation_d5` at 30.15 ms from
  5039	> the two `f13-21` runs. Those are **interpreter** times: both circuits map to
  5040	> `coop_r10_n1720`, whose gate verdict was a stale pre-fence *failure* (§11.4),
  5041	> so V2 fell back to the interpreter for exactly that shape. The canonical run
  5042	> has the gate passing and the specializer selected, at 8.60 ms and 16.42 ms —
  5043	> 1.72× and 1.84× faster. The percentages barely moved (0.032 % → 0.054 %) so no
  5044	> conclusion changed, but the absolute times were measuring a different kernel
  5045	> than the one the surrounding text describes.
  5046	
  5047	The two register-tier rows carry the effect and deserve a stability note: at
  5048	12–13 µs a kernel is close enough to the measurement floor that run-to-run
  5049	variation is comparable to the effect. The two earlier `f13-21` runs put
  5050	`four_t` at 13.2 and 10.1 µs and `frame_h` at 13.6 and 10.0 µs, spanning
  5051	34–47 %. Read the short-tail figure as **~35–47 %**, not as a single number.
  5052	Those two circuits are also unaffected by the stale cache in the first place:
  5053	they are register tier (LDS = 0, VGPR = 32), and §11.2's A/B rebuild showed the
  5054	register-tier binary is **byte-identical by md5** before and after the barrier
  5055	fix — 4,457 instructions, zero barriers, nothing to fence. So all three runs
  5056	measured the same code for these rows.
  5057	
  5058	The conclusion is unambiguous and cuts against a naive reading of §13.3:
  5059	**for V2's production workloads, the HSA-vs-HIP dispatch difference is
  5060	negligible.** At the median circuit it is well under 0.1 % of kernel time. The 1.74× is a
  5061	real property of the dispatch path and it is not where V2's speedup comes from
  5062	— §14 attributes that to the kernel.
  5063	
  5064	Where it *does* matter is the short tail. `four_t` and `frame_h` run for 10–13 µs,
  5065	so a single HIP dispatch would add 35–47 % to their cost, and the ~198 µs naive
  5066	path would have cost **15–20× the kernel itself** (16.4× and 15.1× on the
  5067	canonical run's 12.1 and 13.1 µs). Those two circuits are also
  5068	exactly the ones a user iterates on interactively. And the correctness gate
  5069	(§9) dispatches per validation, as does any future per-batch structure.
  5070	
  5071	So the defensible framing of the no-HIP policy is not "it makes V2 fast." It is:
  5072	*dispatch overhead is a fixed floor that becomes the entire cost at small
  5073	problem sizes, and HSA puts that floor 1.74× lower — and 31× lower than the
  5074	naive path the code started from.*
  5075	
  5076	### 13.5 What the no-HIP policy has cost
  5077	
  5078	The policy is not free, and §11.2 is the invoice.
  5079	
  5080	`v2_barrier()` was hand-rolled to avoid HIP's `__syncthreads()`. HIP's version
  5081	expands to a release/acquire fence pair around `s_barrier`; V2's expanded to a
  5082	bare `s_barrier`, which on AMDGCN orders **execution but not memory**. The
  5083	result was a workgroup-level data race exposed at 92.8 % of the specialized
  5084	kernel's barriers, which corrupted reduction totals, which flipped measurement
  5085	outcomes.
  5086	
  5087	> V2 hand-rolled the barrier to avoid HIP and lost the fence with it.
  5088	
  5089	That is the honest shape of the trade. **HIP's abstraction costs 4,671 ns per
  5090	dispatch, and it also encodes correctness knowledge that is easy to lose when
  5091	you reimplement it.** Combine that with §13.4a — where the saving is worth
  5092	0.03 % of a median run — and the accounting is uncomfortable: on the workloads
  5093	V2 actually runs, the no-HIP policy bought a fraction of a percent and cost
  5094	weeks of debugging a memory-model detail that `__syncthreads()` had been
  5095	handling silently.
  5096	
  5097	That is not an argument for reverting. The policy remains right for three
  5098	reasons that are not about the 1.74×:
  5099	
  5100	1. **The short tail is real.** At 13 µs, `four_t` pays 35 % for a HIP dispatch.
  5101	2. **Self-containment.** V2's whole premise is emitting freestanding amdgcn and
  5102	   loading it directly; a `.hsaco` produced by `clang --target=amdgcn-amd-amdhsa`
  5103	   has no HIP module to be launched from. HSA is not an optimization here so
  5104	   much as the native interface for what V2 already builds.
  5105	3. **The naive-path finding.** 198 µs → 6.3 µs is a 31× win that exists only
  5106	   because the dispatch layer is owned rather than delegated.
  5107	
  5108	The cost is stated as plainly as the benefit: reimplementing a runtime means
  5109	reimplementing its invariants, and §11.2 is what that looks like when one is
  5110	missed.
  5111	
  5112	Also worth stating plainly: HIP has not been removed from the *repository*. The
  5113	SVM and Hybrid backends still use it (`hip_sampler.hip`), and the constraint on
  5114	this work was explicitly *"no CMake, ONLY on the MLIR part, no changes to SVM or
  5115	hybrid."* What V2 removed is HIP from **its own** dispatch and device-side
  5116	synchronization path. That removal is total and checkable:
  5117	
  5118	```
  5119	$ grep -rn "#include.*hip"            src/clifft/gpu/mlir/v2/   # (none)
  5120	$ grep -rnE "\bhip[A-Z][A-Za-z]*\s*\(" src/clifft/gpu/mlir/v2/  # (none)
  5121	$ grep -rn "__syncthreads"             src/clifft/gpu/mlir/v2/
  5122	v2_ops.h:130:  // ... HIP's __syncthreads() expands to exactly this,
  5123	```
  5124	
  5125	Zero HIP headers, zero HIP API calls. All 13 lines in the directory that match
  5126	"hip" case-insensitively are comments — and the sole `__syncthreads` reference
  5127	is the §11.2 post-mortem note explaining what V2's hand-rolled barrier had been
  5128	missing. Dispatch instead runs on 14 direct `hsa_*` call sites in
  5129	`v2_kernel.cc`.
  5130	
  5131	### 13.6 What is still unmeasured
  5132	
  5133	`batched16` is a benchmark mode, **not** something V2 does. V2's hot path is
  5134	`hsa_dispatch_and_wait` (the `persistent` shape, 6,326 ns), called exactly once
  5135	per run. A batched entry point exists — `hsa_dispatch_batch_and_wait`, which
  5136	reserves N packets plus a barrier in a single CAS — but grep finds **no caller**
  5137	outside the dispatch layer itself. It is capability, not usage.
  5138	
  5139	Given §13.4a, that is the right call for now: with one dispatch per run and
  5140	millisecond-to-second kernels, batching has nothing to amortize. It would only
  5141	pay if V2 moved to a multi-dispatch structure — chunked shot batches for
  5142	progress reporting or memory-bounded rank-26 runs. Recorded in §16 as
  5143	conditional, not as a missed win.
  5144	
  5145	The larger caveat on this whole section: an empty kernel isolates dispatch cost
  5146	by construction, which is what makes the comparison clean, and also what makes
  5147	it an upper bound on relevance. §13.4a is the correction, and it should be read
  5148	as part of the result rather than as a footnote to it.
  5149	
