  5253	
  5254	### 14.3 Where the time goes: the scalar pipe
  5255	
  5256	The strongest and most consistent signal in the corpus is **not** the vector
  5257	instruction count. It is the scalar one.
  5258	
  5259	| circuit | V2/SVM time | VALU ratio | **SALU ratio** | SALU reduction |
  5260	|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
  5261	| `four_t` | 0.587 | 0.432 | **0.110** | 9.1× |
  5262	| `surface_d9_t19` | 0.264 | 0.482 | 0.137 | 7.3× |
  5263	| `surface_d11_t15` | 0.258 | 0.482 | 0.138 | 7.3× |
  5264	| `surface_d11_t19` | 0.256 | 0.490 | 0.138 | 7.2× |
  5265	| `circuit_d3_p0.001` | 0.509 | 0.817 | 0.150 | 6.7× |
  5266	| `circuit_d5_p0.001` | 0.847 | 0.639 | 0.156 | 6.4× |
  5267	| `qv10` | 0.319 | 0.445 | 0.198 | 5.1× |
  5268	| `frame_h` | 0.742 | 0.429 | 0.218 | 4.6× |
  5269	| `qv20_seed42` | 0.850 | 0.821 | 0.345 | 2.9× |
  5270	| `qv24_L4_seed42` | 0.882 | 0.863 | **0.361** | 2.8× |
  5271	
  5272	**On 26 of 26 circuits the SALU ratio is below the VALU ratio** — the scalar
  5273	count falls faster than the vector count, without exception. The range is
  5274	0.110–0.361: V2 issues between 2.8× and 9.1× fewer scalar instructions than the
  5275	SVM interpreter.
  5276	
  5277	On the surface and `circuit_d5` families the reduction is larger in *absolute*
  5278	terms as well, by a factor of 3.8×–7.3×:
  5279	
  5280	| circuit | ΔSALU | ΔVALU | \|ΔSALU\|/\|ΔVALU\| |
  5281	|---|---:|---:|---:|
  5282	| `circuit_d3_p0.001` | −6 M | −0.8 M | **7.31** |
  5283	| `surface_d11_t10` | −17,152 M | −2,832 M | 6.06 |
  5284	| `surface_d7_t15` | −4,603 M | −761 M | 6.05 |
  5285	| `cultivation_d5` | −4,053 M | −693 M | 5.85 |
  5286	| `circuit_d5_p0.001` | −2,029 M | −352 M | 5.76 |
  5287	| `surface_d11_t19` | −8,691 M | −2,275 M | 3.82 |
  5288	
  5289	On the **QV family this inverts**: `qv20_seed42` removes 9,094 M scalar
  5290	instructions against 37,752 M vector ones, a ratio of 0.24. The scalar *ratio*
  5291	still improves more (0.345 vs 0.821), but the QV circuits are so vector-dominated
  5292	in absolute terms that the vector reduction is the larger number. Both statements
  5293	are true of the same data, and which one matters depends on which pipe is the
  5294	limiter — a distinction §14.5 develops.
  5295	
  5296	**What those scalar instructions were.** The interpreter's inner loop is
  5297	
  5298	```c
  5299	for (u32 pc = 0; pc < num_instrs; ++pc) {
  5300	    CV2Instr ins = instrs[pc];      // a load
  5301	    u32 k = st->active_k;           // another load
  5302	    switch (ins.opcode) { ... }     // a jump table
  5303	```
  5304	
  5305	Per bytecode instruction that is: an address computation (`pc * 40`, needing a
  5306	64-bit multiply — see §7.3), a `global_load`, a `v_readfirstlane` to move the
  5307	operand to the scalar unit, an `s_load` of `active_k`, a bounds compare, and a
  5308	jump through a switch table. None of it computes an amplitude. V2 emits the call
  5309	directly with its operands as literals, and all of it disappears — which is
  5310	exactly what the eight microbenchmarks of §7 predicted, case by case.
  5311	
  5312	> **A retraction, because an earlier draft of this report got the mechanism
  5313	> backwards.** §7 previously described this as work *moved* from the vector pipe
  5314	> to the scalar pipe — "the same computation, once per wavefront instead of once
  5315	> per lane." That is wrong. Counting scalar instructions in the same sixteen
  5316	> `.s` files that produced §7's VALU numbers shows SALU falling in all eight
  5317	> cases (1.26×–3.09×), and the corpus shows it falling *faster* than VALU on
  5318	> 26 of 26 circuits. Nothing migrates. Both pipes issue less, and the scalar
  5319	> pipe issues much less. The claim survived several drafts because it was
  5320	> plausible and because `stats.csv` had a `v_alu` column and no `s_alu` column —
  5321	> the falsifying number was one `grep` away and structurally invisible.
  5322	
  5323	### 14.4 Why the speedup varies: instruction mix
  5324	
  5325	§7 ended with a taxonomy — what specialization folds and what it cannot. The
  5326	corpus tests it.
  5327	
  5328	The **surface family** (0.256–0.534) is dominated by frame ops and dormant
  5329	measurements, precisely the S1 and S2 classes that fold hardest (5.50× and 4.53×
  5330	on VALU, 18 branches → 0). The **`circuit_d5` family** (0.786–0.856) is 19.1 %
  5331	noise ops by call count — the S7 class, which folds at 1.02× — and those ops are
  5332	individually the most expensive in the ISA (447 interpreter-form instructions
  5333	against 136 for a frame op), so their share of *time* far exceeds their share of
  5334	*call sites*. The prediction §7.9 made from a single microbenchmark, before any
  5335	of these circuits were measured, was that `circuit_d5` would be the corpus's
  5336	weakest coop-tier result. It is.
  5337	
  5338	The **QV family** (0.732–0.990) is weak for a different reason, developed next.
  5339	
  5340	### 14.5 Why the QV family resists: the resident pool shrinks
  5341	
  5342	The QV circuits have *better* VALU and SALU ratios than `circuit_d5` (0.82–0.86
  5343	and 0.34–0.36 against 0.64–0.66 and 0.16), yet worse time ratios. Instruction
  5344	count is not what limits them.
  5345	
  5346	**The resident pool shrinks with rank.** Global-tier kernels size a persistent
  5347	workgroup pool from an HBM budget: each workgroup owns an amplitude slice plus a
  5348	half-size scratch — 12 bytes per amplitude, since `GpuComplex` is
  5349	`{float re; float im;}` — against a 32 GB budget, capped at 2,048 and rounded
  5350	down to a multiple of `kNumXCDs = 8` (`v2_kernel.cc:436-445`):
  5351	
  5352	```cpp
  5353	const uint64_t amp = 1ull << flat.peak_rank;
  5354	const uint64_t bytes_per_wg = amp * sizeof(GpuComplex) + (amp / 2) * sizeof(GpuComplex);
  5355	const uint64_t budget = 32ull << 30;  // 32 GB
  5356	uint64_t wgs = budget / bytes_per_wg;
  5357	if (wgs < 1) wgs = 1;               // rank 26+: at least one resident wg
  5358	if (wgs > 2048) wgs = 2048;
  5359	global_grid_wgs = static_cast<uint32_t>(wgs);
  5360	if (global_grid_wgs > kNumXCDs) global_grid_wgs -= global_grid_wgs % kNumXCDs;
  5361	```
  5362	
  5363	Evaluating that arithmetic against the grids `rocprofv3` actually recorded:
  5364	
  5365	| rank | bytes/wg | predicted wgs | measured wgs |
  5366	|---|---:|---:|---:|
  5367	| 20 | 12 MB | 2,048 (capped) | **2,048** ✓ |
  5368	| 21 | 24 MB | 1,360 | **1,360** ✓ |
  5369	| 22 | 48 MB | 680 | **680** ✓ |
  5370	| 23 | 96 MB | 336 | **336** ✓ |
  5371	| 24 | 192 MB | 168 | **168** ✓ |
  5372	
  5373	Five predictions, five exact matches. Every added qubit halves the pool. By rank
  5374	24 there are 168 workgroups on a device with 256 CUs — **the machine cannot be
  5375	filled**, and no amount of instruction-level improvement changes that. This is
  5376	the clearest single explanation for the QV band, and it is structural rather
  5377	than incidental: it follows from the budget constant and the rank, both known
  5378	before the kernel launches.
  5379	
  5380	**The second mechanism is register spilling, and it is measured, not inferred.**
  5381	The three weakest circuits — `qv22_L6` (0.980), `qv23_L5` (0.990), `qv24_L4`
  5382	(0.882) — are exactly the three whose kernels spill. This does not require
  5383	interpreting a performance counter: the AMDHSA metadata note in each `.hsaco`
  5384	states it directly.
  5385	
  5386	| rank | circuit | ratio | `.vgpr_count` | `.agpr_count` | `.vgpr_spill_count` | `.sgpr_spill_count` | scratch |
  5387	|---:|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
  5388	| 20 | `qv20_seed42` | 0.850 | 104 | 40 | **0** | **0** | 96 |
  5389	| 20 | `qv20_L8` | 0.732 | 106 | 42 | **0** | **0** | 112 |
  5390	| 21 | `qv21_L8` | 0.736 | 104 | 40 | **0** | **0** | 112 |
  5391	| 22 | `qv22_L6` | 0.980 | 128 | 64 | **136** | **762** | 448 |
  5392	| 23 | `qv23_L5` | 0.990 | 128 | 64 | **199** | **662** | 576 |
  5393	| 24 | `qv24_L4` | 0.882 | 128 | 64 | **152** | **594** | 480 |
  5394	
  5395	Read out of the binaries with `llvm-readelf --notes`, e.g. for rank 22:
  5396	
  5397	```
  5398	.vgpr_count:       128       .agpr_count:       64
  5399	.vgpr_spill_count: 136       .sgpr_spill_count: 762
  5400	.private_segment_fixed_size: 448
  5401	```
  5402	
  5403	Ranks 20 and 21 sit *below* the VGPR 128 / AGPR 64 cap and spill nothing. Rank
  5404	22 and above take the cap and spill 594–762 scalars to scratch — which is
  5405	HBM-backed, and therefore the same memory these kernels are already
  5406	bandwidth-bound on. Spilling and the collapse in speedup begin at exactly the
  5407	same rank.
  5408	
  5409	> **A note on two register numbers that disagree.** `rocprofv3` reports 52, 56
  5410	> and 64 VGPRs for these kernels where the metadata says 104, 106 and 128. The
  5411	> scratch sizes match one-to-one across the two sources (96/112/112/448/576/480),
  5412	> so these are certainly the same kernels. The counts differ by roughly 2× and
  5413	> `rocprofv3` reports `Accum_VGPR_Count = 0` where the metadata reports 40–64
  5414	> AGPRs, so the profiler is evidently reporting a different quantity — an
  5415	> allocation granule or an architected-only count — rather than contradicting
  5416	> the binary. §15.5 tabulates the profiler's numbers because that is what that
  5417	> chapter's other columns come from; **the spill counts above are from the
  5418	> `.hsaco` and are the authoritative ones.** §10 analyses this boundary in
  5419	> depth from the same metadata.
  5420	
  5421	§10 establishes what this is *not*: it is not driven by circuit length. The
  5422	three spilling kernels are the three **shortest** in the tier (320–359
  5423	instructions), while a rank-14 kernel emitting 16,521 instructions — 46× more —
  5424	spills 4 SGPRs. What changes at the boundary is a single baked constant, the
  5425	`amp_capacity` crossing 2²². The mechanism inside LLVM's allocator has not been
  5426	isolated, and §16 keeps that as the top open item.
  5427	
  5428	> **What this section does not claim.** An earlier draft argued the QV band was
  5429	> explained by an occupancy inversion — "V2 runs a third as many waves, each 3×
  5430	> longer" — computed as `SQ_WAVE_CYCLES / SQ_WAVES`. That quotient is not
  5431	> admissible here. `SQ_WAVES` is collected in pass `pmcA` and `SQ_WAVE_CYCLES`
  5432	> in pass `pmcC`: **different executions of the kernel**. And `SQ_WAVES` does not
  5433	> reconcile with launch geometry on precisely these circuits — SVM launches an
  5434	> identical 512-workgroup, 256-thread grid on `qv20`, `qv21_L8` and `qv22_L6`
  5435	> and reports 8,832, 10,240 and 8,000 waves respectively, where the geometry
  5436	> implies 2,048 in all three. On the other 20 circuits `SQ_WAVES` equals
  5437	> workgroups × 4 exactly. Whatever the rank-≥20 counts measure, dividing a
  5438	> pass-C cycle count by a pass-A wave count that disagrees with the launch
  5439	> geometry does not measure per-wave cost. The claim was removed rather than
  5440	> repaired.
  5441	
  5442	### 14.6 What V2 does *not* spend
  5443	
  5444	Three counters are worth reporting for what they rule out.
  5445	
  5446	**MFMA = 0 on all 52 cells.** 26 circuits × 2 backends, none missing. Neither
  5447	backend issues a single matrix-core instruction. The workload is a butterfly
  5448	reduction over amplitude pairs — `cadd`/`csub`/`cmul` on 2-element complex values
  5449	with a strided access pattern — not a GEMM. Any proposal to bring the matrix
  5450	cores to bear on this workload is unsupported by this corpus, and the zero is
  5451	measured rather than assumed. (An earlier draft could only claim 51 of 52:
  5452	`qv24_L4_seed42`'s SVM counter block was missing from the truncated job 50785,
  5453	and was recorded as *unmeasured* rather than rounded down to zero.)
  5454	
  5455	**LDS is cheaper on V2 by construction, not by optimization.**
  5456	
  5457	| tier | V2 LDS | SVM LDS | ratio |
  5458	|---|---:|---:|---:|
  5459	| coop | 13,312 B | 23,040 B | 0.578 |
  5460	| global | 1,024 B | 8,704 B | **0.118** |
  5461	| register | 0 B | 0 B | — |
  5462	
  5463	The coop figure is the sum of the declared arrays — `lds_v[1024]` and
  5464	`lds_red_scratch[512]` at 8 B each is 12,288 B, plus `lds_red0`/`lds_red1`
  5465	(128 B) and `lds_state`, for 13,064 B in the ELF — and the global-tier kernel
  5466	declares neither amplitude array, because at rank > 10 the amplitudes live in
  5467	HBM. Its ELF figure is 784 B of `lds_state` and `lds_shot` alone
  5468	(`v2_specializer.cc:204-205`). The 8.5× gap is therefore a consequence of the
  5469	tier's design, not a tuning result.
  5470	
  5471	> The table quotes `rocprofv3`'s `LDS_Block_Size`, which is the ELF
  5472	> `.group_segment_fixed_size` **rounded up to a 256-byte granule**: 13,064 →
  5473	> 13,312 and 784 → 1,024. Both columns are rounded the same way, so the ratios
  5474	> are unaffected. See §9.3 for how this granule was mistaken for a code change.
  5475	
  5476	The dynamic counter agrees. `SQ_INSTS_LDS` on the coop tier runs 0.60–0.77× SVM;
  5477	on the register tier V2 executes **zero** LDS instructions against SVM's 25,280.
  5478	On the surface family's coop and global circuits, however, V2 issues *more*
  5479	(1.13–1.25×) — folded axes turn some LDS traffic into direct indexed access, and
  5480	the direction of that trade depends on the circuit.
  5481	
  5482	**Scratch, on the register tier, is 4.3× smaller**: 656–1,040 B against SVM's
  5483	uniform 4,480 B. The SVM kernel must provision for the worst opcode it might
  5484	interpret; V2 provisions for the opcodes this circuit actually contains.
  5485	
  5486	### 14.7 A sanity check: does the counter model explain the time?
  5487	
  5488	If the story above is right, a single derived quantity should predict the
  5489	measured ratio. `SQ_BUSY_CYCLES` — cycles during which the SQ has work — is the
  5490	natural candidate, and unlike §14.5's rejected quotient it is a ratio of the same
  5491	counter between two arms, not a division across passes.
  5492	
  5493	Across all 26 circuits, the busy-cycle ratio against the kernel-time ratio gives
  5494	**Pearson r = 0.942**, median relative error **5.5 %**, mean **9.0 %**.
  5495	
  5496	The residual is informative. The six largest errors are, in order,
  5497	`circuit_d5_p0.0005` (28.2 %), `circuit_d5_p0.001` (27.2 %),
  5498	`circuit_d5_p0.002` (26.4 %), `circuit_d5_p0.003` (25.0 %),
  5499	`circuit_d5_p0.005` (21.2 %) and `cultivation_d5` (17.9 %) — **the entire d5
  5500	family, and nothing else**. §14.8 is about the same six circuits, and the two

