  5503	### 14.8 One result that does not fit
  5504	
  5505	The `circuit_d5` family wins, but its **L2 hit rate is still depressed**: 91.1–92.1 %
  5506	for V2 against 97.6–98.4 % for SVM, and `cultivation_d5` shows 90.9 % against
  5507	98.9 %. This is a residue of the regression documented in §11.1 — the same
  5508	circuits, the same direction, at a fraction of the magnitude (pre-fence the gap
  5509	was 71.5 % against 98.0 %, `VERIFIED_FACTS.md:158`).
  5510	
  5511	They win anyway, on the strength of a 0.64 VALU ratio and a 0.16 SALU ratio. But
  5512	a 6-point L2 deficit on the one circuit family V2 finds hardest, showing up in
  5513	the same six circuits as §14.7's busy-cycle residual, is not explained by
  5514	anything in this chapter. §16 records it as open. The honest summary is that V2
  5515	wins these circuits *despite* a memory-system disadvantage it did not have to
  5516	have.
  5517	
  5518	A second unexplained result, in the opposite direction: on the two smallest
  5519	register-tier circuits V2's L2 hit rate is far *worse* than SVM's — `four_t`
  5520	52.3 % against 74.4 %, `frame_h` 52.2 % against 66.8 % — and V2 wins both anyway
  5521	(0.587, 0.742). At 13 µs and 12 µs these kernels are small enough that cache
  5522	behaviour is dominated by cold misses on first touch, and the hit *rate* is
  5523	computed over a small denominator. The result is recorded because it was
  5524	measured, not because it is understood.
