  5821	### 15.8 Reproduction
  5822	
  5823	```bash
  5824	sbatch V2_performance/tools/bench_all.sh report-final-allfixtures
  5825	```
  5826	
  5827	The label is positional; partition, `--gpus=1` and the 01:55:00 walltime are
  5828	`#SBATCH` directives in the script itself. `V2_SPECIALIZE=1` is set by the
  5829	script rather than left to the caller, because unset it measures the bytecode
  5830	interpreter and reports a fake 2–3× regression.
  5831	
  5832	The script is in-tree for a reason worth repeating. Its header records that it
  5833	"produced `20260726T182433Z_report-final-postdust` and every run before it, but
  5834	was never committed — it lived in `/tmp` and was lost." Every number in this
  5835	chapter is reproducible only because the harness that produced it is now
  5836	versioned alongside the results.
  5837	
  5838	Two further guarantees: the run aborts rather than continuing past a missing
  5839	fixture (§14.1), and `manifest.json` records `dirty` — **every figure above is
  5840	void if it reads `true`.** For job 50793 it reads `false`. The expected
  5841	denominator is 26 and `summary.md` states the actual one.
  5842	
  5843	## 16. Conclusions and open items
  5844	
  5845	### 16.1 What was built, and what it is worth
  5846	
  5847	Three rewrites of the `clifft` GPU backend, each answering the previous one's
  5848	failure:
  5849	
  5850	| | approach | outcome |
  5851	|---|---|---|
  5852	| **SVM** | one interpreter kernel, three tiers, bytecode in HBM | the baseline. Correct, general, and paying interpretation cost on every instruction of every shot. |
  5853	| **Hybrid** | interpreter + per-circuit HIP source compiled at runtime | worked, did not push execution further. Compilation cost and HIP's launch path ate the gain. |
  5854	| **V1** | per-circuit MLIR → single monolithic kernel, no interpreter, no runtime | **failed.** Unrolling the circuit into straight-line IR produced kernels too large to compile or schedule. |
  5855	| **V2** | per-circuit specialization *over* a runtime, direct C → amdgcn, HSA dispatch | **0.573 geometric mean against SVM, 26 of 26 circuits faster.** |
  5856	
  5857	The load-bearing lesson sits between V1 and V2, and it is not "MLIR was the
  5858	wrong tool." It is that **specialization and unrolling are separable, and V1
  5859	conflated them.** V1 assumed that to specialize a circuit you must emit it as
  5860	straight-line code. V2 specializes exactly as aggressively — the same constants
  5861	folded, the same branches removed — while keeping loops as loops and keeping a
  5862	runtime underneath. The result compiles in seconds instead of failing to
  5863	compile, and it is faster.
  5864	
  5865	### 16.2 Where the speedup comes from
  5866	
  5867	Three mechanisms, in descending order of how much of the corpus they explain.
  5868	
  5869	**1. Scalar instruction deletion (§14.3).** The dominant effect, and not the one
  5870	the project expected. On **26 of 26 circuits** the SALU ratio is below the VALU
  5871	ratio — V2 issues 2.8× to 9.1× fewer scalar instructions. What disappears is the
  5872	interpreter's per-instruction overhead: bytecode address arithmetic (`pc * 40`,
  5873	a 64-bit multiply), the load, the `v_readfirstlane` to move the operand to the
  5874	scalar unit, the `active_k` reload, the bounds check, the switch dispatch. None
  5875	of it computed an amplitude.
  5876	
  5877	This was documented for several drafts as work *moved* from the vector pipe to
  5878	the scalar pipe. That was wrong, and §14.3 retracts it: nothing migrates, both
  5879	pipes issue less, and the scalar pipe issues much less.
  5880	
  5881	**2. Resource footprint (§14.6, §15.5).** V2's global-tier kernels declare 1,024
  5882	bytes of LDS against SVM's 8,704 — 8.5× — because with the amplitudes in HBM
  5883	there is nothing to stage. On the register tier V2 allocates 656–1,040 bytes of
  5884	scratch against SVM's uniform 4,480, and executes **zero** LDS instructions
  5885	against SVM's 25,280. These are consequences of specialization knowing what the
  5886	circuit contains, not of tuning.
  5887	
  5888	**3. Nothing exotic.** `SQ_INSTS_MFMA` is **0 across all 52 cells** — 26
  5889	circuits × 2 backends. The workload is a butterfly reduction over pairs of
  5890	complex amplitudes, not a GEMM, and no part of this result comes from the matrix
  5891	cores. Any future proposal to use them is unsupported by this corpus.
  5892	
  5893	The proximate check on all of it: `SQ_BUSY_CYCLES` ratio predicts kernel-time
  5894	ratio at **Pearson r = 0.942**, and `GRBM_GUI_ACTIVE` — an independent activity
  5895	counter — agrees with `SQ_BUSY_CYCLES` within a few percent on 24 of 26.
  5896	
  5897	### 16.3 Where it does not come from
  5898	
  5899	**The rank ceiling is real and it is register spilling (§14.5, §10).** Above
  5900	rank 21 the win collapses to 0.88–0.99. The cause is visible in the AMDHSA
  5901	metadata rather than inferred from counters:
  5902	
  5903	| rank | spill (VGPR / SGPR) | ratio |
  5904	|---:|---|---:|
  5905	| 20–21 | 0 / 0 | 0.732–0.850 |
  5906	| 22 | 136 / 762 | 0.980 |
  5907	| 23 | 199 / 662 | 0.990 |
  5908	| 24 | 152 / 594 | 0.882 |
  5909	
  5910	Spilling begins at exactly the rank where the speedup dies, and it spills to
  5911	HBM-backed scratch — the memory these kernels are already bandwidth-bound on.
  5912	Compounding it, the resident workgroup pool halves with every added qubit: the
  5913	HBM budget formula yields 2048/1360/680/336/168 workgroups for ranks 20–24
  5914	(predicted from source constants, matching the measured grids **five for five**),
  5915	so by rank 24 there are 168 workgroups on a 256-CU device and the machine cannot
  5916	be filled.
  5917	
  5918	**Specialization does not help what it cannot fold.** §7's taxonomy predicted
  5919	this circuit-by-circuit before the corpus was measured: the surface family, all
  5920	frame ops and dormant measurements, folds 5.50× on VALU and runs at 0.256; the
  5921	d5 family is 19.1 % noise ops that fold 1.02×, and runs at 0.79–0.86. The
  5922	weakest coop-tier result was predicted from a single microbenchmark and was
  5923	correct.
  5924	
  5925	### 16.4 Open items
  5926	
  5927	Ordered by how much they would change the result.
  5928	
  5929	**1. The rank-21/22 allocation cliff.** *The top item.* The three spilling
  5930	kernels are the three **shortest** in the tier (320–359 instructions); a rank-14
  5931	kernel emitting 16,521 instructions spills 4 SGPRs. Circuit length is not the
  5932	mechanism. What changes at the boundary is one baked constant — `amp_capacity`
  5933	crossing 2²² — and the emitted C is otherwise structurally identical. The cause
  5934	is inside LLVM's allocator and has not been isolated. **First step is an LLVM
  5935	allocation study at the boundary, not a code change.**
  5936	
  5937	**2. The d5 anomaly: three counters, one population, no mechanism.** On all six
  5938	d5-family circuits, both activity counters say the GPU is ~0.63 as busy on V2
  5939	while kernel time says 0.79–0.86 — *less busy, and yet slower than that predicts*.
  5940	The same six circuits carry the entire busy-cycle residual (17.9–28.2 %, and
  5941	essentially zero elsewhere) and a residual L2 deficit (91 % against SVM's 98 %,
  5942	down from 71.5 % pre-fence but not gone). Three independent signals, one circuit
  5943	family, no explanation. This is the largest unexplained result in the report.
  5944	
  5945	**3. The specializer does not yet bake per-circuit LDS sizing.** It knows each
  5946	circuit's peak rank and could size the coop tier's LDS exactly; it currently
  5947	inherits a conservative static bound (§9.3). A known, bounded win nobody has
  5948	taken.
  5949	
  5950	**4. `batched16` is conditional, not a missed win.** HSA batched dispatch
  5951	measures 2,320 ns against the persistent path's 6,326 — 2.7× — but V2 issues
  5952	**one dispatch per run**, so there is nothing to amortize. It would pay only if
  5953	V2 moved to a multi-dispatch structure (chunked shot batches for progress
  5954	reporting, or memory-bounded rank-26 runs). Capability, not usage.
  5955	
  5956	**5. The residual f32-vs-f64 branch-probability effect.** §12 established that
  5957	the d5 divergence was *not* precision — it was a threshold constant calibrated
  5958	for f64 and left in place when storage moved to f32. The genuine precision
  5959	effect predicted in §12.1, branch probabilities good to ~1e-6 relative, remains
  5960	real and unobserved: nothing in this corpus has isolated an instance. Bounded,
  5961	rare, and still open.
  5962	
  5963	**6. The `rocprofv3` VGPR discrepancy.** The profiler reports 52–64 VGPRs and
  5964	`Accum_VGPR_Count = 0` where the `.hsaco` metadata reports 104–128 plus 40–64
  5965	AGPRs, for kernels whose scratch sizes match one-to-one. Not a threat to any
  5966	conclusion — the metadata is authoritative and the spill counts come from it —
  5967	but the report should not carry two numbers for one quantity indefinitely.
  5968	
  5969	**7. `SQ_WAVES` on persistent global-tier kernels.** The counter does not
  5970	reconcile with launch geometry above rank 19: three identical 512-workgroup SVM
  5971	grids report 8,832 / 10,240 / 8,000 waves where geometry implies 2,048, while on
  5972	the other 20 circuits it equals workgroups × 4 exactly. §14.5 retracted a claim
  5973	built on it. Worth understanding before any future analysis uses this counter.
  5974	
  5975	### 16.5 What this report is, and how to check it
  5976	
  5977	Every quantitative claim here is backed by an artifact in the tree: a run
  5978	directory under `V2_performance/runs/`, a `.s` file under `lowering/`, or a line
  5979	of committed source. `V2_performance/VERIFIED_FACTS.md` is the ledger — sixteen
  5980	audit passes, each recording what was checked, what was wrong, and a method note
  5981	about how the error survived.
  5982	
  5983	The instruction this report was written under was *trust data, not text*, and it
